('1ebe4c17-9005-495e-84b4-c19f7d97555f', '4942b243-3eda-4100-9200-942a29e98d11', 'flashcard', 'Distinguish between Municipal Bonds and Green Bonds (and green municipal bonds).', 'Priority: Tier 3 | PYQ Connection: Syllabus; the "do not confuse" requirement', 'Basis | Municipal Bonds | Green Bonds
Defined by | Who issues (an Urban Local Body) | How proceeds are used (green projects)
Issuer | Municipal corporations and municipalities only | Anyone: sovereign, PSU, corporate or municipality
Purpose | Urban infrastructure and services | Environment and climate projects
Regulator | SEBI (ILMDS Regulations 2015) | SEBI (green debt framework) and, for sovereign issues, the Government/RBI
Overlap | A green municipal bond is both: issued by a ULB for green projects (Lucknow 2021, Indore 2023) | ', NULL, NULL, NULL, 41, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '3e54a28d-992c-471c-bd9a-f9778f1e0aec', '52953b6e-aec4-4e2f-900e-bb2718655601', '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u3-c41-q-1", "text": "Distinguish between Municipal Bonds and Green Bonds (and green municipal bonds).", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u3-c41-a-1", "rows": [["Basis", "Municipal Bonds", "Green Bonds"], ["Defined by", "Who issues (an Urban Local Body)", "How proceeds are used (green projects)"], ["Issuer", "Municipal corporations and municipalities only", "Anyone: sovereign, PSU, corporate or municipality"], ["Purpose", "Urban infrastructure and services", "Environment and climate projects"], ["Regulator", "SEBI (ILMDS Regulations 2015)", "SEBI (green debt framework) and, for sovereign issues, the Government/RBI"], ["Overlap", "A green municipal bond is both: issued by a ULB for green projects (Lucknow 2021, Indore 2023)", ""]], "type": "table"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:07:23.563655+00', '2026-09-20 18:07:23.563655+00'),
('dfca2236-326d-48b7-aa43-7296c956596d', '4942b243-3eda-4100-9200-942a29e98d11', 'flashcard', 'What is Credit Rating? Explain its meaning, purpose and importance.', 'Priority: Tier 2 | PYQ Connection: Syllabus "Credit Rating of Bonds"; not asked in these two papers', 'Meaning: A credit rating is an independent opinion of a Credit Rating Agency (CRA) on the relative degree of credit risk of a borrower or a debt instrument, that is, its ability and willingness to pay interest and principal on time. It is expressed through letter symbols (AAA, AA, etc.).
Regulation: SEBI (Credit Rating Agencies) Regulations, 1999.
Purpose and importance:

Investor protection: helps investors judge credit risk without doing their own analysis.
Pricing: better ratings mean lower yield and lower borrowing cost for issuers, since the yield includes a credit spread.
Market access: rated bonds can be listed and marketed. Many institutions can invest only in investment-grade papers.
Reduces information asymmetry between issuer and investor.
Regulatory use: SEBI, RBI, IRDAI and PFRDA use ratings for investment norms and capital requirements.
Discipline for issuers: encourages better governance and disclosure.
Monitoring: ongoing surveillance flags deterioration early.

Note: A rating is an opinion, not a recommendation to buy, sell or hold.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 42, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '3e54a28d-992c-471c-bd9a-f9778f1e0aec', '1f0683af-9e25-413c-ac27-e26e9a7bacd3', '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u3-c42-q-1", "text": "What is Credit Rating? Explain its meaning, purpose and importance.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u3-c42-a-1", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Meaning: A credit rating is an independent opinion of a Credit Rating Agency (CRA) on the relative degree of credit risk of a borrower or a debt instrument, that is, its ability and willingness to pay interest and principal on time. It is expressed through letter symbols (AAA, AA, etc.).", "Regulation: SEBI (Credit Rating Agencies) Regulations, 1999.", "Purpose and importance:"]}, {"id": "edm-u3-c42-a-2", "type": "numberList", "items": ["Investor protection: helps investors judge credit risk without doing their own analysis.", "Pricing: better ratings mean lower yield and lower borrowing cost for issuers, since the yield includes a credit spread.", "Market access: rated bonds can be listed and marketed. Many institutions can invest only in investment-grade papers.", "Reduces information asymmetry between issuer and investor.", "Regulatory use: SEBI, RBI, IRDAI and PFRDA use ratings for investment norms and capital requirements.", "Discipline for issuers: encourages better governance and disclosure.", "Monitoring: ongoing surveillance flags deterioration early."]}, {"id": "edm-u3-c42-a-3", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Note: A rating is an opinion, not a recommendation to buy, sell or hold."]}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:07:23.563655+00', '2026-09-20 18:07:23.563655+00'),
('d6f72a59-0065-43c2-87df-140b389fbb32', '4942b243-3eda-4100-9200-942a29e98d11', 'flashcard', 'Explain the Credit Rating process.', 'Priority: Tier 2 | PYQ Connection: Syllabus (process, "where supported by the study material")', 'Request and mandate: The issuer applies and signs a rating agreement.
Assignment of an analytical team.
Information collection: financial statements, projections, business plans and legal documents.
Management meetings and site visits.
Analysis: business risk, financial risk, management quality, industry outlook and structure (security and covenants).
Rating committee decides the rating.
Communication to the issuer, who can accept it or appeal with additional information.
Publication / dissemination: The rating is publicly disclosed, with a rationale.
Surveillance: Ratings are reviewed at least annually. They may be upgraded, downgraded, or put on Rating Watch, and an Outlook (Positive / Stable / Negative) is given.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 43, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '3e54a28d-992c-471c-bd9a-f9778f1e0aec', '1f0683af-9e25-413c-ac27-e26e9a7bacd3', '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u3-c43-q-1", "text": "Explain the Credit Rating process.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u3-c43-a-1", "type": "numberList", "items": ["Request and mandate: The issuer applies and signs a rating agreement.", "Assignment of an analytical team.", "Information collection: financial statements, projections, business plans and legal documents.", "Management meetings and site visits.", "Analysis: business risk, financial risk, management quality, industry outlook and structure (security and covenants).", "Rating committee decides the rating.", "Communication to the issuer, who can accept it or appeal with additional information.", "Publication / dissemination: The rating is publicly disclosed, with a rationale.", "Surveillance: Ratings are reviewed at least annually. They may be upgraded, downgraded, or put on Rating Watch, and an Outlook (Positive / Stable / Negative) is given."]}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:07:23.563655+00', '2026-09-20 18:07:23.563655+00'),
('108bdbbb-0ad1-4b5a-b671-8ff5e722163a', '4942b243-3eda-4100-9200-942a29e98d11', 'flashcard', 'Explain the credit rating symbols (rating scale).', 'Priority: Tier 2 | PYQ Connection: Syllabus', 'Long-term instruments (bonds, NCDs)

Symbol | Meaning | Grade
AAA | Highest safety, lowest credit risk | Investment grade
AA | High safety, very low credit risk | Investment grade
A | Adequate safety, low credit risk | Investment grade
BBB | Moderate safety, moderate credit risk (lowest investment grade) | Investment grade
BB | Moderate risk | Speculative grade
B | High risk | Speculative grade
C | Very high risk | Speculative grade
D | Default | Default

"+" and "−" modifiers are used within AA to B.
Short-term instruments (CP, CDs): A1+ (highest), A1, A2, A3, A4 and D (default).
Investment grade = BBB− and above. Below it is speculative or "junk."
Notation differs by agency: CRISIL/CARE use "AAA", ICRA uses "[ICRA]AAA" and India Ratings uses "IND AAA."', NULL, NULL, NULL, 44, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '3e54a28d-992c-471c-bd9a-f9778f1e0aec', '1f0683af-9e25-413c-ac27-e26e9a7bacd3', '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u3-c44-q-1", "text": "Explain the credit rating symbols (rating scale).", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u3-c44-a-1", "bold": true, "text": "Long-term instruments (bonds, NCDs)", "type": "text", "style": "heading"}, {"id": "edm-u3-c44-a-2", "rows": [["Symbol", "Meaning", "Grade"], ["AAA", "Highest safety, lowest credit risk", "Investment grade"], ["AA", "High safety, very low credit risk", "Investment grade"], ["A", "Adequate safety, low credit risk", "Investment grade"], ["BBB", "Moderate safety, moderate credit risk (lowest investment grade)", "Investment grade"], ["BB", "Moderate risk", "Speculative grade"], ["B", "High risk", "Speculative grade"], ["C", "Very high risk", "Speculative grade"], ["D", "Default", "Default"]], "type": "table"}, {"id": "edm-u3-c44-a-3", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["\"+\" and \"−\" modifiers are used within AA to B.", "Short-term instruments (CP, CDs): A1+ (highest), A1, A2, A3, A4 and D (default).", "Investment grade = BBB− and above. Below it is speculative or \"junk.\"", "Notation differs by agency: CRISIL/CARE use \"AAA\", ICRA uses \"[ICRA]AAA\" and India Ratings uses \"IND AAA.\""]}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:07:23.563655+00', '2026-09-20 18:07:23.563655+00'),
('34a96f1e-6740-4ef0-9806-640cd9e6010e', '4942b243-3eda-4100-9200-942a29e98d11', 'flashcard', 'Name and explain the Credit Rating Agencies in India.', 'Priority: Tier 2 | PYQ Connection: Syllabus "Rating Agencies"', 'Regulated by SEBI under the CRA Regulations 1999. Agencies must be registered with SEBI.

Agency | Notes
CRISIL | India''s first rating agency (1987); subsidiary of S&P Global
ICRA | Set up in 1991; Moody''s is the largest indirect shareholder
CARE Ratings (CareEdge) | Established in 1993
India Ratings and Research (Ind-Ra) | Subsidiary of the Fitch Group
Acuité Ratings & Research | Formerly SMERA; focus on SMEs and mid-corporates
Brickwork Ratings | Promoted in 2007; Canara Bank is a strategic partner
Infomerics Valuation and Rating | Registered with SEBI; based in New Delhi

Newer entrants have been registered recently (verify the current list on SEBI''s website).
Services: Rating of bonds, NCDs, CPs, bank loans and structured finance, plus research.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 45, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '3e54a28d-992c-471c-bd9a-f9778f1e0aec', '1f0683af-9e25-413c-ac27-e26e9a7bacd3', '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u3-c45-q-1", "text": "Name and explain the Credit Rating Agencies in India.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u3-c45-a-1", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Regulated by SEBI under the CRA Regulations 1999. Agencies must be registered with SEBI."]}, {"id": "edm-u3-c45-a-2", "rows": [["Agency", "Notes"], ["CRISIL", "India''s first rating agency (1987); subsidiary of S&P Global"], ["ICRA", "Set up in 1991; Moody''s is the largest indirect shareholder"], ["CARE Ratings (CareEdge)", "Established in 1993"], ["India Ratings and Research (Ind-Ra)", "Subsidiary of the Fitch Group"], ["Acuité Ratings & Research", "Formerly SMERA; focus on SMEs and mid-corporates"], ["Brickwork Ratings", "Promoted in 2007; Canara Bank is a strategic partner"], ["Infomerics Valuation and Rating", "Registered with SEBI; based in New Delhi"]], "type": "table"}, {"id": "edm-u3-c45-a-3", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Newer entrants have been registered recently (verify the current list on SEBI''s website).", "Services: Rating of bonds, NCDs, CPs, bank loans and structured finance, plus research."]}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:07:23.563655+00', '2026-09-20 18:07:23.563655+00'),
('07c13ce9-0410-4c84-9fb0-2c06772f1bc2', '4942b243-3eda-4100-9200-942a29e98d11', 'flashcard', 'Factors considered in a credit rating, and its limitations.', 'Priority: Tier 3 | PYQ Connection: Syllabus', 'Factors considered:

Business risk: industry outlook, competitive position and operating efficiency.
Financial risk: leverage, interest and debt service coverage, cash flows and liquidity.
Management risk: track record and governance.
Project risk (for project-linked debt).
Structural features: security, covenants, escrow arrangements and external support.
Regulatory and macroeconomic environment.

Limitations:

Issuer-pays model may create a conflict of interest.
Timeliness: downgrades may lag events. The IL&FS episode in 2018 is often cited.
A rating is an opinion, not a guarantee.
Rating shopping and over-reliance by investors.
Ratings differ across agencies in quality and coverage.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 46, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '3e54a28d-992c-471c-bd9a-f9778f1e0aec', '1f0683af-9e25-413c-ac27-e26e9a7bacd3', '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u3-c46-q-1", "text": "Factors considered in a credit rating, and its limitations.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u3-c46-a-1", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Factors considered:"]}, {"id": "edm-u3-c46-a-2", "type": "numberList", "items": ["Business risk: industry outlook, competitive position and operating efficiency.", "Financial risk: leverage, interest and debt service coverage, cash flows and liquidity.", "Management risk: track record and governance.", "Project risk (for project-linked debt).", "Structural features: security, covenants, escrow arrangements and external support.", "Regulatory and macroeconomic environment."]}, {"id": "edm-u3-c46-a-3", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Limitations:"]}, {"id": "edm-u3-c46-a-4", "type": "numberList", "items": ["Issuer-pays model may create a conflict of interest.", "Timeliness: downgrades may lag events. The IL&FS episode in 2018 is often cited.", "A rating is an opinion, not a guarantee.", "Rating shopping and over-reliance by investors.", "Ratings differ across agencies in quality and coverage."]}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:07:23.563655+00', '2026-09-20 18:07:23.563655+00'),
('9e539c9e-83bc-4330-a20b-922be9e427f7', '4942b243-3eda-4100-9200-942a29e98d11', 'flashcard', 'Compare G-Secs, PSU Bonds, Corporate Bonds, Municipal Bonds and Green Bonds.', 'Priority: Tier 2 | PYQ Connection: The "do not confuse" instruction; a likely short-note/comparison question', 'Basis | Government Securities | PSU Bonds | Corporate Bonds | Municipal Bonds | Green Bonds
Issuer | Central / State Government | Government-owned companies (NHAI, REC, PFC) | Private/public companies, NBFCs, banks | Urban Local Bodies | Any issuer (sovereign, PSU, corporate, ULB)
Defined by | Issuer | Issuer | Issuer | Issuer | Use of proceeds
Purpose | Fiscal deficit, budgetary needs | Infrastructure, power, rail, housing | Expansion, capex, refinancing | Urban infrastructure | Environmental projects only
Credit risk | Virtually nil (sovereign) | Low (implicit government comfort) | Depends on rating | Moderate to high | Same as underlying issuer
Yield | Lowest (benchmark) | Slightly above G-secs | Highest among the group for the same tenor | Above G-secs / SDLs | Similar to issuer''s normal bonds
Liquidity | Highest | Moderate | Lower; mostly OTC | Very low | Limited
Regulator | RBI; G-Sec Act 2006 | SEBI, Companies Act | SEBI (NCS Regulations 2021), Companies Act | SEBI (ILMDS 2015) | SEBI green debt framework
Example | 7.18% GS 2037; T-bills | NHAI, REC, PFC, IRFC bonds | NCDs of companies and NBFCs | Pune Municipal Corp (2017) | Sovereign Green Bonds (2023)

Key sentence: "A green bond is a label based on use of proceeds. It can be a G-sec, PSU, corporate or municipal bond."', NULL, NULL, NULL, 47, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '3e54a28d-992c-471c-bd9a-f9778f1e0aec', '6250d677-63c3-401b-b672-02bbee157a8e', '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u3-c47-q-1", "text": "Compare G-Secs, PSU Bonds, Corporate Bonds, Municipal Bonds and Green Bonds.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u3-c47-a-1", "rows": [["Basis", "Government Securities", "PSU Bonds", "Corporate Bonds", "Municipal Bonds", "Green Bonds"], ["Issuer", "Central / State Government", "Government-owned companies (NHAI, REC, PFC)", "Private/public companies, NBFCs, banks", "Urban Local Bodies", "Any issuer (sovereign, PSU, corporate, ULB)"], ["Defined by", "Issuer", "Issuer", "Issuer", "Issuer", "Use of proceeds"], ["Purpose", "Fiscal deficit, budgetary needs", "Infrastructure, power, rail, housing", "Expansion, capex, refinancing", "Urban infrastructure", "Environmental projects only"], ["Credit risk", "Virtually nil (sovereign)", "Low (implicit government comfort)", "Depends on rating", "Moderate to high", "Same as underlying issuer"], ["Yield", "Lowest (benchmark)", "Slightly above G-secs", "Highest among the group for the same tenor", "Above G-secs / SDLs", "Similar to issuer''s normal bonds"], ["Liquidity", "Highest", "Moderate", "Lower; mostly OTC", "Very low", "Limited"], ["Regulator", "RBI; G-Sec Act 2006", "SEBI, Companies Act", "SEBI (NCS Regulations 2021), Companies Act", "SEBI (ILMDS 2015)", "SEBI green debt framework"], ["Example", "7.18% GS 2037; T-bills", "NHAI, REC, PFC, IRFC bonds", "NCDs of companies and NBFCs", "Pune Municipal Corp (2017)", "Sovereign Green Bonds (2023)"]], "type": "table"}, {"id": "edm-u3-c47-a-2", "text": "Key sentence: \"A green bond is a label based on use of proceeds. It can be a G-sec, PSU, corporate or municipal bond.\"", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph", "italic": true}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:07:23.563655+00', '2026-09-20 18:07:23.563655+00'),
('1bfcf5a8-05b0-4dd4-97bd-fabae9f76c3d', '4942b243-3eda-4100-9200-942a29e98d11', 'flashcard', 'Distinguish between Government Securities and Corporate Bonds.', 'Priority: Tier 2 | PYQ Connection: Combines two Tier 1 themes (2024 Q3Q, 2025 Q3.2)', 'Basis | Government Securities | Corporate Bonds
Issuer | Central / State Governments | Companies
Credit risk | Nil (sovereign) | Present, depends on rating
Return | Lower | Higher (credit spread)
Liquidity | Very high | Lower
Rating | Not required | Required
Security | Sovereign backing | Secured or unsecured
Regulator | RBI | SEBI
Market | Wholesale plus retail (Retail Direct) | Mostly private placement to institutions
SLR eligibility | Yes | No
Tax | Interest taxable | Interest taxable
Use | Benchmark for pricing | Priced as benchmark + spread', NULL, NULL, NULL, 48, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '3e54a28d-992c-471c-bd9a-f9778f1e0aec', '6250d677-63c3-401b-b672-02bbee157a8e', '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u3-c48-q-1", "text": "Distinguish between Government Securities and Corporate Bonds.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u3-c48-a-1", "rows": [["Basis", "Government Securities", "Corporate Bonds"], ["Issuer", "Central / State Governments", "Companies"], ["Credit risk", "Nil (sovereign)", "Present, depends on rating"], ["Return", "Lower", "Higher (credit spread)"], ["Liquidity", "Very high", "Lower"], ["Rating", "Not required", "Required"], ["Security", "Sovereign backing", "Secured or unsecured"], ["Regulator", "RBI", "SEBI"], ["Market", "Wholesale plus retail (Retail Direct)", "Mostly private placement to institutions"], ["SLR eligibility", "Yes", "No"], ["Tax", "Interest taxable", "Interest taxable"], ["Use", "Benchmark for pricing", "Priced as benchmark + spread"]], "type": "table"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:07:23.563655+00', '2026-09-20 18:07:23.563655+00'),
('3b7949a2-1039-4698-afb8-3fcf20532ea3', '4942b243-3eda-4100-9200-942a29e98d11', 'flashcard', 'Distinguish between PSU Bonds and Corporate Bonds.', 'Priority: Tier 3 | PYQ Connection: Syllabus "Public Sector Bonds & Corporate Bonds"', 'Basis | PSU Bonds | Corporate Bonds
Issuer | Government-owned enterprises | Private and public companies
Ownership comfort | Government ownership | None
Typical rating | Mostly AAA / AA+ | AAA to lower grades
Yield | Lower (perceived safety) | Higher for lower-rated issuers
Sector focus | Infrastructure, power, rail, housing | Wide range of industries
Special forms | Tax-free bonds, 54EC bonds | NCDs, convertibles, sustainability-linked
Liquidity | Better | Lower', NULL, NULL, NULL, 49, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '3e54a28d-992c-471c-bd9a-f9778f1e0aec', '6250d677-63c3-401b-b672-02bbee157a8e', '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u3-c49-q-1", "text": "Distinguish between PSU Bonds and Corporate Bonds.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u3-c49-a-1", "rows": [["Basis", "PSU Bonds", "Corporate Bonds"], ["Issuer", "Government-owned enterprises", "Private and public companies"], ["Ownership comfort", "Government ownership", "None"], ["Typical rating", "Mostly AAA / AA+", "AAA to lower grades"], ["Yield", "Lower (perceived safety)", "Higher for lower-rated issuers"], ["Sector focus", "Infrastructure, power, rail, housing", "Wide range of industries"], ["Special forms", "Tax-free bonds, 54EC bonds", "NCDs, convertibles, sustainability-linked"], ["Liquidity", "Better", "Lower"]], "type": "table"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:07:23.563655+00', '2026-09-20 18:07:23.563655+00'),
('23f526a4-502c-42ef-83d0-0ec66650da31', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'Explain the importance of advertising.', 'Priority: Tier 2', 'Advertising is important because it performs an information and persuasion function that benefits producers, consumers, and the economy at large.

Builds brand awareness — introduces new products and keeps existing brands top-of-mind.
Supports sales and revenue — stimulates demand and accelerates the buying decision.
Informs consumer choice — communicates features, price, and availability.
Creates brand differentiation — helps a brand stand apart in a competitive category.
Supports the media industry — a key revenue source for newspapers, television, and digital platforms.
Aids economic growth — supports employment across agencies, media, and production.

Example: Amazon''s festive-season campaigns simultaneously inform consumers of offers and drive a measurable spike in sales.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 3, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '2336ef22-ee28-4025-8ab7-16e92a0f915b', 'cf47b6a8-e024-4e1b-b03b-f15019d9618f', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u1-c3-q-1", "text": "Explain the importance of advertising.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u1-c3-a-1", "text": "Advertising is important because it performs an information and persuasion function that benefits producers, consumers, and the economy at large.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u1-c3-a-2", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Builds brand awareness — introduces new products and keeps existing brands top-of-mind.", "Supports sales and revenue — stimulates demand and accelerates the buying decision.", "Informs consumer choice — communicates features, price, and availability.", "Creates brand differentiation — helps a brand stand apart in a competitive category.", "Supports the media industry — a key revenue source for newspapers, television, and digital platforms.", "Aids economic growth — supports employment across agencies, media, and production."]}, {"id": "adv-u1-c3-a-3", "text": "Example: Amazon''s festive-season campaigns simultaneously inform consumers of offers and drive a measurable spike in sales.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:45.717564+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:45.717564+00'),
('8efa5d46-14a0-4246-bc82-470695dcbd3e', '4942b243-3eda-4100-9200-942a29e98d11', 'flashcard', 'Answer-writing templates for Unit 3 (by marks).', 'Priority: Tier 4', '7.5 marks (e.g. types of G-secs, STCI entities): 2-line definition + 6–8 points/categories with one line each + one table or diagram.
9–10 marks (e.g. G-secs with merits and demerits, OMO + PD): definition + features + classification table + merits/demerits table + factors or process + a one-line conclusion.
Classification questions ("types"): State the basis first (issuer, coupon, security…) and then list the types under each basis.
Paired questions ("X and Y"): Split the marks evenly, and add a link sentence between the two.
Numbers: Use only figures you are sure about, and prefer "roughly" and rounded figures for statistics.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 50, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '3e54a28d-992c-471c-bd9a-f9778f1e0aec', '309dfed1-1965-4866-987f-cb358801c4b9', '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u3-c50-q-1", "text": "Answer-writing templates for Unit 3 (by marks).", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u3-c50-a-1", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["7.5 marks (e.g. types of G-secs, STCI entities): 2-line definition + 6–8 points/categories with one line each + one table or diagram.", "9–10 marks (e.g. G-secs with merits and demerits, OMO + PD): definition + features + classification table + merits/demerits table + factors or process + a one-line conclusion.", "Classification questions (\"types\"): State the basis first (issuer, coupon, security…) and then list the types under each basis.", "Paired questions (\"X and Y\"): Split the marks evenly, and add a link sentence between the two.", "Numbers: Use only figures you are sure about, and prefer \"roughly\" and rounded figures for statistics."]}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:07:23.563655+00', '2026-09-20 18:07:23.563655+00'),
('313a434b-2d43-4177-a842-26ed764c6463', '4942b243-3eda-4100-9200-942a29e98d11', 'flashcard', 'One-liners and key facts.', 'Priority: Tier 4', 'G-secs: issued by Central and State Governments. RBI is the debt manager. G-Sec Act 2006. T-bills: 91/182/364 days, zero coupon. CMBs: less than 91 days (2010). Dated securities: 5–40 years, half-yearly coupon. States issue only SDLs.
OMO: RBI buys/sells G-secs to manage durable liquidity. Purchase = liquidity in, yields down. Sale = liquidity out, yields up.
Primary Dealers: system introduced in 1995; underwriting, market making and OMO conduit. Two types: standalone and bank PDs.
STCI: set up May 1994, promoted by the RBI. First PD 1996. PD business hived off June 2007. Renamed STCI Finance Ltd on 24 Oct 2011. Subsidiaries: STCI Primary Dealer and STCI Commodities.
Corporate bonds: benchmark yield + credit spread. SEBI NCS Regulations 2021. EBP for private placements above a threshold.
Green bonds: first in India by Yes Bank (2015). SEBI framework 2017. Sovereign Green Bonds 2023.
Municipal bonds: first by Bangalore (1997). SEBI ILMDS Regulations 2015.
Credit rating: SEBI CRA Regulations 1999. Investment grade = BBB− and above. CRISIL, ICRA, CARE, India Ratings, Acuité, Brickwork, Infomerics.
Institutional investors: banks (SLR), mutual funds (SEBI), insurers (IRDAI), pension funds (PFRDA/EPFO).', NULL, NULL, NULL, 51, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '3e54a28d-992c-471c-bd9a-f9778f1e0aec', '309dfed1-1965-4866-987f-cb358801c4b9', '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u3-c51-q-1", "text": "One-liners and key facts.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u3-c51-a-1", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["G-secs: issued by Central and State Governments. RBI is the debt manager. G-Sec Act 2006. T-bills: 91/182/364 days, zero coupon. CMBs: less than 91 days (2010). Dated securities: 5–40 years, half-yearly coupon. States issue only SDLs.", "OMO: RBI buys/sells G-secs to manage durable liquidity. Purchase = liquidity in, yields down. Sale = liquidity out, yields up.", "Primary Dealers: system introduced in 1995; underwriting, market making and OMO conduit. Two types: standalone and bank PDs.", "STCI: set up May 1994, promoted by the RBI. First PD 1996. PD business hived off June 2007. Renamed STCI Finance Ltd on 24 Oct 2011. Subsidiaries: STCI Primary Dealer and STCI Commodities.", "Corporate bonds: benchmark yield + credit spread. SEBI NCS Regulations 2021. EBP for private placements above a threshold.", "Green bonds: first in India by Yes Bank (2015). SEBI framework 2017. Sovereign Green Bonds 2023.", "Municipal bonds: first by Bangalore (1997). SEBI ILMDS Regulations 2015.", "Credit rating: SEBI CRA Regulations 1999. Investment grade = BBB− and above. CRISIL, ICRA, CARE, India Ratings, Acuité, Brickwork, Infomerics.", "Institutional investors: banks (SLR), mutual funds (SEBI), insurers (IRDAI), pension funds (PFRDA/EPFO)."]}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:07:23.563655+00', '2026-09-20 18:07:23.563655+00'),
('ba107348-71a0-4d9c-aa3e-dec077ddcce1', '4942b243-3eda-4100-9200-942a29e98d11', 'flashcard', 'Full forms.', 'Priority: Tier 4', 'G-Sec (Government Security), T-bill (Treasury Bill), CMB (Cash Management Bill), SDL (State Development Loan), FRB (Floating Rate Bond), STRIPS (Separate Trading of Registered Interest and Principal of Securities), SGL (Subsidiary General Ledger), NDS-OM (Negotiated Dealing System – Order Matching), OMO (Open Market Operations), LAF (Liquidity Adjustment Facility), SDF (Standing Deposit Facility), SLR (Statutory Liquidity Ratio), CRR (Cash Reserve Ratio), PD (Primary Dealer), SPD (Standalone Primary Dealer), STCI (Securities Trading Corporation of India), NBFC-ND-SI (Non-Banking Financial Company – Non-Deposit taking – Systemically Important), NCD (Non-Convertible Debenture), PSU (Public Sector Undertaking), ULB (Urban Local Body), ILMDS (Issue and Listing of Municipal Debt Securities), NCS (Non-Convertible Securities), EBP (Electronic Book Provider), RFQ (Request for Quote), CRA (Credit Rating Agency), ESG (Environmental, Social and Governance), SLB (Sustainability-Linked Bond), AT1 (Additional Tier 1), CCIL (Clearing Corporation of India Ltd), IRDAI (Insurance Regulatory and Development Authority of India), PFRDA (Pension Fund Regulatory and Development Authority), EPFO (Employees'' Provident Fund Organisation), NPS (National Pension System).', NULL, NULL, NULL, 52, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '3e54a28d-992c-471c-bd9a-f9778f1e0aec', '309dfed1-1965-4866-987f-cb358801c4b9', '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u3-c52-q-1", "text": "Full forms.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u3-c52-a-1", "text": "G-Sec (Government Security), T-bill (Treasury Bill), CMB (Cash Management Bill), SDL (State Development Loan), FRB (Floating Rate Bond), STRIPS (Separate Trading of Registered Interest and Principal of Securities), SGL (Subsidiary General Ledger), NDS-OM (Negotiated Dealing System – Order Matching), OMO (Open Market Operations), LAF (Liquidity Adjustment Facility), SDF (Standing Deposit Facility), SLR (Statutory Liquidity Ratio), CRR (Cash Reserve Ratio), PD (Primary Dealer), SPD (Standalone Primary Dealer), STCI (Securities Trading Corporation of India), NBFC-ND-SI (Non-Banking Financial Company – Non-Deposit taking – Systemically Important), NCD (Non-Convertible Debenture), PSU (Public Sector Undertaking), ULB (Urban Local Body), ILMDS (Issue and Listing of Municipal Debt Securities), NCS (Non-Convertible Securities), EBP (Electronic Book Provider), RFQ (Request for Quote), CRA (Credit Rating Agency), ESG (Environmental, Social and Governance), SLB (Sustainability-Linked Bond), AT1 (Additional Tier 1), CCIL (Clearing Corporation of India Ltd), IRDAI (Insurance Regulatory and Development Authority of India), PFRDA (Pension Fund Regulatory and Development Authority), EPFO (Employees'' Provident Fund Organisation), NPS (National Pension System).", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:07:23.563655+00', '2026-09-20 18:07:23.563655+00'),
('a13444c6-7de7-4718-9cde-2bdabab3622a', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'What is advertising? Define it.', 'Priority: Tier 2', 'Definition: Advertising is a paid, non-personal form of communication through mass media, undertaken by an identified sponsor, to inform, persuade, or remind a target audience about a product, service, brand, or idea, with the ultimate objective of influencing attitude or behaviour. Key elements:

Paid form — space/time is purchased, distinguishing it from publicity.
Non-personal — reaches a mass audience through media, not face-to-face.
Identified sponsor — the source is disclosed, unlike propaganda.
Persuasive intent — aims to inform, persuade, or remind.

Example: A television commercial for Amul butter that is paid for, broadcast to a mass audience, and clearly credits Amul as the sponsor.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '2336ef22-ee28-4025-8ab7-16e92a0f915b', 'cf47b6a8-e024-4e1b-b03b-f15019d9618f', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u1-c1-q-1", "text": "What is advertising? Define it.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u1-c1-a-1", "text": "Definition: Advertising is a paid, non-personal form of communication through mass media, undertaken by an identified sponsor, to inform, persuade, or remind a target audience about a product, service, brand, or idea, with the ultimate objective of influencing attitude or behaviour. Key elements:", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph", "italic": true}, {"id": "adv-u1-c1-a-2", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Paid form — space/time is purchased, distinguishing it from publicity.", "Non-personal — reaches a mass audience through media, not face-to-face.", "Identified sponsor — the source is disclosed, unlike propaganda.", "Persuasive intent — aims to inform, persuade, or remind."]}, {"id": "adv-u1-c1-a-3", "text": "Example: A television commercial for Amul butter that is paid for, broadcast to a mass audience, and clearly credits Amul as the sponsor.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:45.717564+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:45.717564+00'),
('2f79fe64-584c-4ccf-9831-add90e464a5c', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'Trace the evolution of advertising.', 'Priority: Tier 3', 'Pre-industrial era: town criers, signboards, and word-of-mouth in local markets.
Print era: newspaper and magazine advertisements following the spread of the printing press and mass literacy.
Industrial revolution: mass production created a need to reach wider markets, giving rise to organised advertising agencies.
Broadcast era: radio and later television enabled audio-visual, emotionally engaging advertising at national scale.
Digital era: internet, search, social media, and mobile advertising enabled targeting and two-way interaction.

Example: The shift of a brand like Fevicol from print/hoarding-led campaigns to television, and now to social-media-first content.

Exam Tip: Frame this as a short timeline; examiners reward a clear "then → now" progression more than exhaustive dates.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 2, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '2336ef22-ee28-4025-8ab7-16e92a0f915b', 'cf47b6a8-e024-4e1b-b03b-f15019d9618f', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u1-c2-q-1", "text": "Trace the evolution of advertising.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u1-c2-a-1", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Pre-industrial era: town criers, signboards, and word-of-mouth in local markets.", "Print era: newspaper and magazine advertisements following the spread of the printing press and mass literacy.", "Industrial revolution: mass production created a need to reach wider markets, giving rise to organised advertising agencies.", "Broadcast era: radio and later television enabled audio-visual, emotionally engaging advertising at national scale.", "Digital era: internet, search, social media, and mobile advertising enabled targeting and two-way interaction."]}, {"id": "adv-u1-c2-a-2", "text": "Example: The shift of a brand like Fevicol from print/hoarding-led campaigns to television, and now to social-media-first content.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u1-c2-a-3", "text": "Exam Tip: Frame this as a short timeline; examiners reward a clear \"then → now\" progression more than exhaustive dates.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:45.717564+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:45.717564+00'),
('beae2509-af31-4062-bd5c-fc854b70c7ec', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'Discuss the scope of advertising.', 'Priority: Tier 3', 'Product/service promotion — across consumer and industrial categories.
Brand building — long-term equity, not just short-term sales.
Institutional and corporate image building.
Social and public-interest communication — public service messages, awareness campaigns.
Political and ideological communication — advocacy and issue-based advertising.
Global reach — through digital media, advertising scope now extends across geographic borders.

Because it touches nearly every sector of the economy — from FMCG to government campaigns — the scope of advertising is considered very wide and continuously expanding with new media.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 4, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '2336ef22-ee28-4025-8ab7-16e92a0f915b', 'cf47b6a8-e024-4e1b-b03b-f15019d9618f', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u1-c4-q-1", "text": "Discuss the scope of advertising.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u1-c4-a-1", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Product/service promotion — across consumer and industrial categories.", "Brand building — long-term equity, not just short-term sales.", "Institutional and corporate image building.", "Social and public-interest communication — public service messages, awareness campaigns.", "Political and ideological communication — advocacy and issue-based advertising.", "Global reach — through digital media, advertising scope now extends across geographic borders."]}, {"id": "adv-u1-c4-a-2", "text": "Because it touches nearly every sector of the economy — from FMCG to government campaigns — the scope of advertising is considered very wide and continuously expanding with new media.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:45.717564+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:45.717564+00'),
('e3135a81-edcc-43d9-8d70-6f14cce5b670', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'Explain the main features/characteristics of advertising.', 'Priority: Tier 2', 'Paid communication — the advertiser pays for the space or time used.
Non-personal — directed at a mass, impersonal audience rather than one individual.
Identified sponsor — source of the message is disclosed.
Persuasive — designed to influence attitude, preference, or behaviour.
Mass-media dependent — uses print, broadcast, outdoor, or digital channels.
Repetitive — messages are typically repeated to build recall.
Creative and structured — combines art (creativity) with a defined communication objective.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 5, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '2336ef22-ee28-4025-8ab7-16e92a0f915b', 'cf47b6a8-e024-4e1b-b03b-f15019d9618f', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u1-c5-q-1", "text": "Explain the main features/characteristics of advertising.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u1-c5-a-1", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Paid communication — the advertiser pays for the space or time used.", "Non-personal — directed at a mass, impersonal audience rather than one individual.", "Identified sponsor — source of the message is disclosed.", "Persuasive — designed to influence attitude, preference, or behaviour.", "Mass-media dependent — uses print, broadcast, outdoor, or digital channels.", "Repetitive — messages are typically repeated to build recall.", "Creative and structured — combines art (creativity) with a defined communication objective."]}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:45.717564+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:45.717564+00'),
('bf584071-2318-4aba-8ce7-ca385f511b5a', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'Explain the benefits of advertising to different stakeholders.', 'Priority: Tier 3', 'To the manufacturer/marketer: Builds brand recall and loyalty; supports faster new-product launches; creates competitive differentiation. To the consumer: Provides product information for informed choice; widens awareness of alternatives; can reduce prices through economies of scale from higher demand.

To society/economy: Funds media houses and journalism; creates employment across the advertising value chain; can drive awareness on social causes (e.g. public service advertising).', NULL, NULL, NULL, 6, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '2336ef22-ee28-4025-8ab7-16e92a0f915b', 'cf47b6a8-e024-4e1b-b03b-f15019d9618f', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u1-c6-q-1", "text": "Explain the benefits of advertising to different stakeholders.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u1-c6-a-1", "text": "To the manufacturer/marketer: Builds brand recall and loyalty; supports faster new-product launches; creates competitive differentiation. To the consumer: Provides product information for informed choice; widens awareness of alternatives; can reduce prices through economies of scale from higher demand.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u1-c6-a-2", "text": "To society/economy: Funds media houses and journalism; creates employment across the advertising value chain; can drive awareness on social causes (e.g. public service advertising).", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:45.717564+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:45.717564+00'),
('2d19795d-be31-4dea-b2ca-25793104f2dc', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'What is Advertising Mix? Explain the meaning of the Five M''s.', 'Priority: Tier 1', 'The Advertising Mix refers to the set of core decision areas an advertiser must plan and combine to run an effective advertising campaign. These decision areas are popularly summarised as the "Five M''s of Advertising" — a framework used to structure the advertising planning process.

Diagram (practise drawing):

ADVERTISING MIX ┌──────────────┐ │   MISSION    │  → objectives ├──────────────┤ │    MONEY     │  → budget ├──────────────┤ │   MESSAGE    │  → content/creative ├──────────────┤ │    MEDIA     │  → channel ├──────────────┤ │  MEASUREMENT │  → evaluation └──────────────┘  Draw this as a simple five-box vertical or circular chart with an arrow flow — examiners reward a "neat labelled diagram" explicitly (Oct 2024, instruction 3).

Exam Tip: This is the single highest-yield concept in Unit 1: it appeared in both supplied papers. Learn Flashcards 7–9 together as one answer unit.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 7, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '2336ef22-ee28-4025-8ab7-16e92a0f915b', 'cf47b6a8-e024-4e1b-b03b-f15019d9618f', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u1-c7-q-1", "text": "What is Advertising Mix? Explain the meaning of the Five M''s.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u1-c7-a-1", "text": "The Advertising Mix refers to the set of core decision areas an advertiser must plan and combine to run an effective advertising campaign. These decision areas are popularly summarised as the \"Five M''s of Advertising\" — a framework used to structure the advertising planning process.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u1-c7-a-2", "bold": true, "text": "Diagram (practise drawing):", "type": "text", "style": "heading"}, {"id": "adv-u1-c7-a-3", "text": "ADVERTISING MIX ┌──────────────┐ │   MISSION    │  → objectives ├──────────────┤ │    MONEY     │  → budget ├──────────────┤ │   MESSAGE    │  → content/creative ├──────────────┤ │    MEDIA     │  → channel ├──────────────┤ │  MEASUREMENT │  → evaluation └──────────────┘  Draw this as a simple five-box vertical or circular chart with an arrow flow — examiners reward a \"neat labelled diagram\" explicitly (Oct 2024, instruction 3).", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u1-c7-a-4", "text": "Exam Tip: This is the single highest-yield concept in Unit 1: it appeared in both supplied papers. Learn Flashcards 7–9 together as one answer unit.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:45.717564+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:45.717564+00'),
('fa3ee665-49af-4542-a5da-92cf400c799c', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'Explain the Five M''s of Advertising in detail, with examples for each.', 'Priority: Tier 1', '1. Mission (objectives): Defines what the advertising is meant to achieve — e.g. build awareness, inform, persuade, or remind. Example: A new EV brand''s mission may be purely to build category awareness. 2. Money (budget): Decides how much to spend, based on factors like sales stage, market share, competition, and frequency needed. Example: A market leader like HUL may spend heavily to defend share; a challenger brand may spend selectively.

3. Message (content/creative): What is communicated and how — the core idea, appeal, and creative execution of the advertisement. Example: Cadbury''s messaging around "sharing joy" rather than just describing the chocolate.

4. Media (channel): The choice of vehicle to deliver the message — television, print, digital, outdoor, radio — based on reach, cost, and target audience habits. Example: A youth-focused sneaker brand favouring Instagram and YouTube over print.

5. Measurement (evaluation): Assessing whether the campaign achieved its objectives, using pre-testing and post-testing methods such as recall tests, sales tracking, or engagement metrics. Example: Tracking brand-recall scores after a festive campaign.

Conclusion: The Five M''s are interdependent: the Mission shapes the Money allocated, which in turn constrains Message and Media choices, while Measurement closes the loop by evaluating whether the Mission was met.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 8, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '2336ef22-ee28-4025-8ab7-16e92a0f915b', 'cf47b6a8-e024-4e1b-b03b-f15019d9618f', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u1-c8-q-1", "text": "Explain the Five M''s of Advertising in detail, with examples for each.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u1-c8-a-1", "text": "1. Mission (objectives): Defines what the advertising is meant to achieve — e.g. build awareness, inform, persuade, or remind. Example: A new EV brand''s mission may be purely to build category awareness. 2. Money (budget): Decides how much to spend, based on factors like sales stage, market share, competition, and frequency needed. Example: A market leader like HUL may spend heavily to defend share; a challenger brand may spend selectively.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u1-c8-a-2", "text": "3. Message (content/creative): What is communicated and how — the core idea, appeal, and creative execution of the advertisement. Example: Cadbury''s messaging around \"sharing joy\" rather than just describing the chocolate.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u1-c8-a-3", "text": "4. Media (channel): The choice of vehicle to deliver the message — television, print, digital, outdoor, radio — based on reach, cost, and target audience habits. Example: A youth-focused sneaker brand favouring Instagram and YouTube over print.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u1-c8-a-4", "text": "5. Measurement (evaluation): Assessing whether the campaign achieved its objectives, using pre-testing and post-testing methods such as recall tests, sales tracking, or engagement metrics. Example: Tracking brand-recall scores after a festive campaign.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u1-c8-a-5", "text": "Conclusion: The Five M''s are interdependent: the Mission shapes the Money allocated, which in turn constrains Message and Media choices, while Measurement closes the loop by evaluating whether the Mission was met.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:45.717564+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:45.717564+00'),
('4fb67c5b-450c-4f3b-bd0d-aa477ba6c8d8', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'How do the Five M''s help in advertising decision-making and campaign planning?', 'Priority: Tier 1', 'Provides a structured checklist so no critical decision area is missed while planning a campaign.
Sequences decisions logically — objectives (Mission) are set before budget (Money), which is set before creative (Message) and channel (Media) choices.
Enables accountability — Measurement ties spending and creative choices back to whether objectives were met.
Helps align stakeholders — marketing teams, agencies, and management use the same framework to evaluate a campaign proposal.

This is essentially the same content as Flashcards 7–8 restated as a "role/importance" question — a common Mumbai University technique of rephrasing the same core theme.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 9, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '2336ef22-ee28-4025-8ab7-16e92a0f915b', 'cf47b6a8-e024-4e1b-b03b-f15019d9618f', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u1-c9-q-1", "text": "How do the Five M''s help in advertising decision-making and campaign planning?", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u1-c9-a-1", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Provides a structured checklist so no critical decision area is missed while planning a campaign.", "Sequences decisions logically — objectives (Mission) are set before budget (Money), which is set before creative (Message) and channel (Media) choices.", "Enables accountability — Measurement ties spending and creative choices back to whether objectives were met.", "Helps align stakeholders — marketing teams, agencies, and management use the same framework to evaluate a campaign proposal."]}, {"id": "adv-u1-c9-a-2", "text": "This is essentially the same content as Flashcards 7–8 restated as a \"role/importance\" question — a common Mumbai University technique of rephrasing the same core theme.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:45.717564+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:45.717564+00'),
('7f936454-66fa-488d-bba3-19f11def2114', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'Explain the various types of advertising.', 'Priority: Tier 2', 'Type | Meaning | Example
Consumer | Aimed at end-users/households buying for personal consumption | Colgate toothpaste TV ad
Industrial | Aimed at businesses buying raw material, machinery, or components | An ad for industrial-grade steel in a trade publication
Institutional | Promotes the image/reputation of an organisation rather than a specific product | A bank''s "trusted for 50 years" brand campaign
Classified | Short, text-based listings grouped by category | "Jobs" or "For Sale" columns in a newspaper
National | Run across an entire country to build a mass brand | A pan-India festive campaign by a telecom brand
Generic | Promotes an entire product category rather than one brand | "Drink more milk"-style category campaigns', NULL, NULL, NULL, 10, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '2336ef22-ee28-4025-8ab7-16e92a0f915b', '58aed356-76fe-4be7-80b1-80b10dc6663b', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u1-c10-q-1", "text": "Explain the various types of advertising.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u1-c10-a-1", "rows": [["Type", "Meaning", "Example"], ["Consumer", "Aimed at end-users/households buying for personal consumption", "Colgate toothpaste TV ad"], ["Industrial", "Aimed at businesses buying raw material, machinery, or components", "An ad for industrial-grade steel in a trade publication"], ["Institutional", "Promotes the image/reputation of an organisation rather than a specific product", "A bank''s \"trusted for 50 years\" brand campaign"], ["Classified", "Short, text-based listings grouped by category", "\"Jobs\" or \"For Sale\" columns in a newspaper"], ["National", "Run across an entire country to build a mass brand", "A pan-India festive campaign by a telecom brand"], ["Generic", "Promotes an entire product category rather than one brand", "\"Drink more milk\"-style category campaigns"]], "type": "table"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:45.717564+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:45.717564+00'),
('a7b5d58b-44c0-4d06-9381-a645dbc1a756', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'Distinguish between Consumer, Industrial, and Institutional advertising.', 'Priority: Tier 2', 'Basis | Consumer | Industrial | Institutional
Target | Individual/household buyers | Businesses, manufacturers | General public/stakeholders
Focus | A specific product/brand | Raw materials, machinery, B2B goods | Organisation''s reputation/image
Purpose | Drive personal purchase | Drive business procurement decisions | Build goodwill and trust
Example | Maggi noodles ad | Ad for industrial cement mixers | Tata Group''s corporate campaigns', NULL, NULL, NULL, 11, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '2336ef22-ee28-4025-8ab7-16e92a0f915b', '58aed356-76fe-4be7-80b1-80b10dc6663b', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u1-c11-q-1", "text": "Distinguish between Consumer, Industrial, and Institutional advertising.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u1-c11-a-1", "rows": [["Basis", "Consumer", "Industrial", "Institutional"], ["Target", "Individual/household buyers", "Businesses, manufacturers", "General public/stakeholders"], ["Focus", "A specific product/brand", "Raw materials, machinery, B2B goods", "Organisation''s reputation/image"], ["Purpose", "Drive personal purchase", "Drive business procurement decisions", "Build goodwill and trust"], ["Example", "Maggi noodles ad", "Ad for industrial cement mixers", "Tata Group''s corporate campaigns"]], "type": "table"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:45.717564+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:45.717564+00'),
('75bd5dcd-7e85-4226-8ab7-80a89956a797', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'What is Classified advertising? What is National advertising?', 'Priority: Tier 3', 'Classified advertising: Short, low-cost, text-based advertisements grouped by category (jobs, property, matrimonial, for-sale) typically found in newspaper classified sections or listing websites. Focused on function over creativity. National advertising: Advertising undertaken by a brand or company across the entire country, aimed at building nationwide brand awareness and consistent brand identity, usually through mass media with wide reach.

Example: A nationwide launch campaign for a new smartphone model versus a local dealer''s classified listing for a used car.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 12, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '2336ef22-ee28-4025-8ab7-16e92a0f915b', '58aed356-76fe-4be7-80b1-80b10dc6663b', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u1-c12-q-1", "text": "What is Classified advertising? What is National advertising?", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u1-c12-a-1", "text": "Classified advertising: Short, low-cost, text-based advertisements grouped by category (jobs, property, matrimonial, for-sale) typically found in newspaper classified sections or listing websites. Focused on function over creativity. National advertising: Advertising undertaken by a brand or company across the entire country, aimed at building nationwide brand awareness and consistent brand identity, usually through mass media with wide reach.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u1-c12-a-2", "text": "Example: A nationwide launch campaign for a new smartphone model versus a local dealer''s classified listing for a used car.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:45.717564+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:45.717564+00'),
('47bb5c8b-e4fa-4f25-a157-e61be2c4d959', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'What is Puffery? Explain with an example.', 'Priority: Tier 1', 'Meaning: Puffery refers to exaggerated, subjective claims of superiority about a product that are understood by a reasonable consumer as opinion or hyperbole rather than a factual, verifiable claim. How it operates: It uses broad, unmeasurable superlatives ("the best," "the world''s greatest") that cannot realistically be proven false, which is why it is generally treated as legally permissible even though ethically debated.

Why it is debated: Critics argue it can still create unrealistic consumer expectations even without being a technically false statement.

Example: A tagline claiming a beverage gives you "the best taste in the world" — a subjective claim no consumer would take as a literal, testable fact.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 23, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '2336ef22-ee28-4025-8ab7-16e92a0f915b', 'ae5f8348-55c7-48c7-9538-9e738b187589', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u1-c23-q-1", "text": "What is Puffery? Explain with an example.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u1-c23-a-1", "text": "Meaning: Puffery refers to exaggerated, subjective claims of superiority about a product that are understood by a reasonable consumer as opinion or hyperbole rather than a factual, verifiable claim. How it operates: It uses broad, unmeasurable superlatives (\"the best,\" \"the world''s greatest\") that cannot realistically be proven false, which is why it is generally treated as legally permissible even though ethically debated.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u1-c23-a-2", "text": "Why it is debated: Critics argue it can still create unrealistic consumer expectations even without being a technically false statement.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u1-c23-a-3", "text": "Example: A tagline claiming a beverage gives you \"the best taste in the world\" — a subjective claim no consumer would take as a literal, testable fact.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:45.717564+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:45.717564+00'),
('f83ad5fd-7c7d-4c1d-ab7b-c5ac5a55e334', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'What is Generic advertising? How is it different from brand advertising?', 'Priority: Tier 3', 'Meaning: Generic advertising promotes an entire product category or commodity rather than a specific brand within it, usually undertaken by an industry association or government body. Difference from brand advertising:

Generic ads grow the total market ("eat more eggs"); brand ads compete for share within an existing market ("buy this brand of eggs").
Generic advertising is often funded collectively by an industry body rather than one company.

Example: A "Jago Grahak Jago" style consumer-awareness campaign versus a single brand''s product commercial.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 13, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '2336ef22-ee28-4025-8ab7-16e92a0f915b', '58aed356-76fe-4be7-80b1-80b10dc6663b', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u1-c13-q-1", "text": "What is Generic advertising? How is it different from brand advertising?", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u1-c13-a-1", "text": "Meaning: Generic advertising promotes an entire product category or commodity rather than a specific brand within it, usually undertaken by an industry association or government body. Difference from brand advertising:", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph", "italic": true}, {"id": "adv-u1-c13-a-2", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Generic ads grow the total market (\"eat more eggs\"); brand ads compete for share within an existing market (\"buy this brand of eggs\").", "Generic advertising is often funded collectively by an industry body rather than one company."]}, {"id": "adv-u1-c13-a-3", "text": "Example: A \"Jago Grahak Jago\" style consumer-awareness campaign versus a single brand''s product commercial.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:45.717564+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:45.717564+00'),
('4d13ed30-10ca-471f-8a65-e9e6f0f8782e', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'Discuss the various theories of advertising in brief.', 'Priority: Tier 1', 'These theories explain how an advertisement is believed to influence a consumer''s mind and behaviour. The six theories on this syllabus are:

Stimulus Theory — an ad is a stimulus that produces a predictable response.
AIDA — Attention, Interest, Desire, Action.
Hierarchy of Effects Model — a staged progression from awareness to purchase.
Means–End Theory — links product attributes to personal values.
Visual Verbal Imaging — combines pictures and words to build a mental image/association.
Cognitive Dissonance Theory — explains post-purchase doubt and advertising''s role in reassurance.

Exam Tip: Both papers allow "any two" or "any one" theory — you do not need to master all six equally; know AIDA and Hierarchy of Effects in full depth, and the rest at meaning + example level.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 14, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '2336ef22-ee28-4025-8ab7-16e92a0f915b', '34c7830f-7ba4-4449-9e4d-c814d7cbe582', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u1-c14-q-1", "text": "Discuss the various theories of advertising in brief.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u1-c14-a-1", "text": "These theories explain how an advertisement is believed to influence a consumer''s mind and behaviour. The six theories on this syllabus are:", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u1-c14-a-2", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Stimulus Theory — an ad is a stimulus that produces a predictable response.", "AIDA — Attention, Interest, Desire, Action.", "Hierarchy of Effects Model — a staged progression from awareness to purchase.", "Means–End Theory — links product attributes to personal values.", "Visual Verbal Imaging — combines pictures and words to build a mental image/association.", "Cognitive Dissonance Theory — explains post-purchase doubt and advertising''s role in reassurance."]}, {"id": "adv-u1-c14-a-3", "text": "Exam Tip: Both papers allow \"any two\" or \"any one\" theory — you do not need to master all six equally; know AIDA and Hierarchy of Effects in full depth, and the rest at meaning + example level.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:45.717564+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:45.717564+00'),
('cfd00669-7c82-4179-8c3b-ac4c0a07ba25', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'Explain the Stimulus (Stimulus–Response) Theory of advertising.', 'Priority: Tier 2', 'Meaning: Based on behavioural psychology, this theory treats an advertisement as a "stimulus" that, when repeated, triggers a predictable consumer "response" — such as recall, preference, or purchase. Main elements: Stimulus (the ad/message) → Organism (the consumer''s mental processing) → Response (the resulting action or attitude).

Advertising application: Explains why repetitive jingles, taglines, and mascots are used — repeated stimulus strengthens the association until the response becomes near-automatic.

Example: Repeated exposure to a jingle like Amul''s builds an almost reflexive positive association with the brand.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 15, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '2336ef22-ee28-4025-8ab7-16e92a0f915b', '34c7830f-7ba4-4449-9e4d-c814d7cbe582', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u1-c15-q-1", "text": "Explain the Stimulus (Stimulus–Response) Theory of advertising.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u1-c15-a-1", "text": "Meaning: Based on behavioural psychology, this theory treats an advertisement as a \"stimulus\" that, when repeated, triggers a predictable consumer \"response\" — such as recall, preference, or purchase. Main elements: Stimulus (the ad/message) → Organism (the consumer''s mental processing) → Response (the resulting action or attitude).", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u1-c15-a-2", "text": "Advertising application: Explains why repetitive jingles, taglines, and mascots are used — repeated stimulus strengthens the association until the response becomes near-automatic.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u1-c15-a-3", "text": "Example: Repeated exposure to a jingle like Amul''s builds an almost reflexive positive association with the brand.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:45.717564+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:45.717564+00'),
('5c2acaab-f204-4fe7-83b4-b0c49345f66d', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'Explain the AIDA model of advertising with an example.', 'Priority: Tier 1', 'Meaning: AIDA describes the sequential mental states a consumer passes through before making a purchase decision in response to advertising.

Diagram:

A — Attention   (grab notice) I — Interest    (build curiosity) D — Desire      (create want/need) A — Action      (drive purchase)

Attention: A bold visual, headline, or sound cuts through clutter.
Interest: Product features/benefits are presented to sustain engagement.
Desire: Emotional or rational appeal makes the consumer want the product.
Action: A clear call-to-action prompts purchase or enquiry.

Advertising application: Used heavily in print and digital ad copywriting to structure the flow of a single advertisement from headline to call-to-action.

Example: A print ad for a fitness app — bold headline (Attention), body copy on features (Interest), before/after testimonial (Desire), "Download now" button (Action).', NULL, NULL, NULL, 16, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '2336ef22-ee28-4025-8ab7-16e92a0f915b', '34c7830f-7ba4-4449-9e4d-c814d7cbe582', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u1-c16-q-1", "text": "Explain the AIDA model of advertising with an example.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u1-c16-a-1", "text": "Meaning: AIDA describes the sequential mental states a consumer passes through before making a purchase decision in response to advertising.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u1-c16-a-2", "bold": true, "text": "Diagram:", "type": "text", "style": "heading"}, {"id": "adv-u1-c16-a-3", "text": "A — Attention   (grab notice) I — Interest    (build curiosity) D — Desire      (create want/need) A — Action      (drive purchase)", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u1-c16-a-4", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Attention: A bold visual, headline, or sound cuts through clutter.", "Interest: Product features/benefits are presented to sustain engagement.", "Desire: Emotional or rational appeal makes the consumer want the product.", "Action: A clear call-to-action prompts purchase or enquiry."]}, {"id": "adv-u1-c16-a-5", "text": "Advertising application: Used heavily in print and digital ad copywriting to structure the flow of a single advertisement from headline to call-to-action.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u1-c16-a-6", "text": "Example: A print ad for a fitness app — bold headline (Attention), body copy on features (Interest), before/after testimonial (Desire), \"Download now\" button (Action).", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:45.717564+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:45.717564+00'),
('4eeeeb66-68da-4061-af5b-fcbf7573a0b0', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'Explain the Hierarchy of Effects Model with an example.', 'Priority: Tier 1', 'Meaning: Proposes that consumers pass through a series of cognitive, affective, and behavioural stages before purchase — a more detailed staged progression than AIDA.

Diagram:

Awareness → Knowledge → Liking → Preference → Conviction → Purchase

Main elements:

Cognitive stage: Awareness, Knowledge
Affective stage: Liking, Preference
Behavioural stage: Conviction, Purchase

Advertising application: Guides campaign sequencing — early-stage campaigns focus on awareness/knowledge (teaser ads), later-stage campaigns push conviction/purchase (offer-led ads).

Example: A new smartphone brand''s teaser campaign builds Awareness, spec-comparison content builds Knowledge and Preference, and a launch-day discount drives Purchase.

Exam Tip: Examiners often ask to compare AIDA and Hierarchy of Effects — both describe a "funnel," but Hierarchy of Effects has more, finer-grained stages.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 17, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '2336ef22-ee28-4025-8ab7-16e92a0f915b', '34c7830f-7ba4-4449-9e4d-c814d7cbe582', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u1-c17-q-1", "text": "Explain the Hierarchy of Effects Model with an example.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u1-c17-a-1", "text": "Meaning: Proposes that consumers pass through a series of cognitive, affective, and behavioural stages before purchase — a more detailed staged progression than AIDA.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u1-c17-a-2", "bold": true, "text": "Diagram:", "type": "text", "style": "heading"}, {"id": "adv-u1-c17-a-3", "text": "Awareness → Knowledge → Liking → Preference → Conviction → Purchase", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u1-c17-a-4", "text": "Main elements:", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph", "italic": true}, {"id": "adv-u1-c17-a-5", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Cognitive stage: Awareness, Knowledge", "Affective stage: Liking, Preference", "Behavioural stage: Conviction, Purchase"]}, {"id": "adv-u1-c17-a-6", "text": "Advertising application: Guides campaign sequencing — early-stage campaigns focus on awareness/knowledge (teaser ads), later-stage campaigns push conviction/purchase (offer-led ads).", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u1-c17-a-7", "text": "Example: A new smartphone brand''s teaser campaign builds Awareness, spec-comparison content builds Knowledge and Preference, and a launch-day discount drives Purchase.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u1-c17-a-8", "text": "Exam Tip: Examiners often ask to compare AIDA and Hierarchy of Effects — both describe a \"funnel,\" but Hierarchy of Effects has more, finer-grained stages.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:45.717564+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:45.717564+00'),
('9689a750-5d15-4be6-848c-579579c6de45', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'Explain the Means–End Theory of advertising.', 'Priority: Tier 2', 'Meaning: Argues that consumers buy a product (the "means") not for its attributes alone, but because those attributes lead to desired consequences, which connect to the consumer''s deeper personal values (the "end"). Main elements (chain): Product attribute → functional/psychological consequence → personal value.

Advertising application: Advertising built on this theory links a product feature all the way up to an aspirational value, rather than stopping at the feature itself.

Example: A skincare ad links "natural ingredients" (attribute) → "healthier skin" (consequence) → "confidence and self-respect" (value), rather than just listing ingredients.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 18, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '2336ef22-ee28-4025-8ab7-16e92a0f915b', '34c7830f-7ba4-4449-9e4d-c814d7cbe582', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u1-c18-q-1", "text": "Explain the Means–End Theory of advertising.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u1-c18-a-1", "text": "Meaning: Argues that consumers buy a product (the \"means\") not for its attributes alone, but because those attributes lead to desired consequences, which connect to the consumer''s deeper personal values (the \"end\"). Main elements (chain): Product attribute → functional/psychological consequence → personal value.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u1-c18-a-2", "text": "Advertising application: Advertising built on this theory links a product feature all the way up to an aspirational value, rather than stopping at the feature itself.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u1-c18-a-3", "text": "Example: A skincare ad links \"natural ingredients\" (attribute) → \"healthier skin\" (consequence) → \"confidence and self-respect\" (value), rather than just listing ingredients.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:45.717564+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:45.717564+00'),
('feaff68c-fc6d-4501-be5c-ae0566cb5b86', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'What is Visual Verbal Imaging in advertising?', 'Priority: Tier 2', 'Meaning: Holds that advertising effectiveness comes from the combined, reinforcing use of visual elements (images, colour, imagery) and verbal elements (words, tagline, tone) to create a single, memorable mental impression of the brand. Main elements: Visual component (imagery, layout, colour); Verbal component (headline, tagline, copy); their interaction — each reinforces the meaning of the other.

Advertising application: Explains why a strong tagline paired with a consistent visual identity (colour, mascot, typography) builds faster and stronger brand recall than either element alone.

Example: The pairing of a distinct visual (a red bindi/dot logo) with a consistent tagline builds a single recognisable brand image over time.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 19, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '2336ef22-ee28-4025-8ab7-16e92a0f915b', '34c7830f-7ba4-4449-9e4d-c814d7cbe582', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u1-c19-q-1", "text": "What is Visual Verbal Imaging in advertising?", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u1-c19-a-1", "text": "Meaning: Holds that advertising effectiveness comes from the combined, reinforcing use of visual elements (images, colour, imagery) and verbal elements (words, tagline, tone) to create a single, memorable mental impression of the brand. Main elements: Visual component (imagery, layout, colour); Verbal component (headline, tagline, copy); their interaction — each reinforces the meaning of the other.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u1-c19-a-2", "text": "Advertising application: Explains why a strong tagline paired with a consistent visual identity (colour, mascot, typography) builds faster and stronger brand recall than either element alone.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u1-c19-a-3", "text": "Example: The pairing of a distinct visual (a red bindi/dot logo) with a consistent tagline builds a single recognisable brand image over time.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:45.717564+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:45.717564+00'),
('dca181c7-8d16-4365-83e1-4a72d0e3a6cf', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'Explain Cognitive Dissonance Theory and its relevance to advertising.', 'Priority: Tier 1', 'Meaning: Describes the psychological discomfort ("dissonance") a consumer feels after making a purchase decision, especially when other attractive alternatives existed — commonly called buyer''s remorse. Main elements: Conflicting beliefs/choices after a decision → discomfort that motivates the consumer to seek reassurance → reduction of dissonance through post-purchase information.

Advertising application: Explains why brands run post-purchase reassurance communication — testimonials, warranty messaging, "you made the right choice" content — to reduce dissonance and encourage repeat purchase/positive word-of-mouth.

Example: A car brand sending a "welcome" email highlighting award-winning safety ratings right after purchase to reassure the buyer.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 20, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '2336ef22-ee28-4025-8ab7-16e92a0f915b', '34c7830f-7ba4-4449-9e4d-c814d7cbe582', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u1-c20-q-1", "text": "Explain Cognitive Dissonance Theory and its relevance to advertising.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u1-c20-a-1", "text": "Meaning: Describes the psychological discomfort (\"dissonance\") a consumer feels after making a purchase decision, especially when other attractive alternatives existed — commonly called buyer''s remorse. Main elements: Conflicting beliefs/choices after a decision → discomfort that motivates the consumer to seek reassurance → reduction of dissonance through post-purchase information.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u1-c20-a-2", "text": "Advertising application: Explains why brands run post-purchase reassurance communication — testimonials, warranty messaging, \"you made the right choice\" content — to reduce dissonance and encourage repeat purchase/positive word-of-mouth.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u1-c20-a-3", "text": "Example: A car brand sending a \"welcome\" email highlighting award-winning safety ratings right after purchase to reassure the buyer.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:45.717564+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:45.717564+00'),
('26e70dc8-b9fc-4ce1-877d-9c0b7d6289f5', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'How do theories of advertising influence the creation of ads? Explain with reference to any two theories.', 'Priority: Tier 1', 'This is a synthesis question — pick any two theories you know in most depth (AIDA and Hierarchy of Effects are the strongest choices) and explicitly connect each stage of the theory to a real creative or media decision. Suggested structure:

State that theories give copywriters and media planners a structured basis for creative and scheduling decisions rather than relying on guesswork.
Explain Theory 1 (e.g. AIDA) and show how each stage maps to a specific part of an actual advertisement (headline → Attention, etc.).
Explain Theory 2 (e.g. Hierarchy of Effects) and show how it guides campaign sequencing across a product''s launch timeline.
Conclude that theories collectively ensure advertising is psychologically grounded rather than purely creative guesswork.

Exam Tip: This exact framing appeared in the 2025 paper (Q1b) — answer using the full theory explanations from Flashcards 16 and 17.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 21, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '2336ef22-ee28-4025-8ab7-16e92a0f915b', '34c7830f-7ba4-4449-9e4d-c814d7cbe582', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u1-c21-q-1", "text": "How do theories of advertising influence the creation of ads? Explain with reference to any two theories.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u1-c21-a-1", "text": "This is a synthesis question — pick any two theories you know in most depth (AIDA and Hierarchy of Effects are the strongest choices) and explicitly connect each stage of the theory to a real creative or media decision. Suggested structure:", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u1-c21-a-2", "type": "numberList", "items": ["State that theories give copywriters and media planners a structured basis for creative and scheduling decisions rather than relying on guesswork.", "Explain Theory 1 (e.g. AIDA) and show how each stage maps to a specific part of an actual advertisement (headline → Attention, etc.).", "Explain Theory 2 (e.g. Hierarchy of Effects) and show how it guides campaign sequencing across a product''s launch timeline.", "Conclude that theories collectively ensure advertising is psychologically grounded rather than purely creative guesswork."]}, {"id": "adv-u1-c21-a-3", "text": "Exam Tip: This exact framing appeared in the 2025 paper (Q1b) — answer using the full theory explanations from Flashcards 16 and 17.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:45.717564+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:45.717564+00'),
('1c8c9c5a-bbc1-43f7-956b-de832d5ced41', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'Are ethics important in advertising? Discuss.', 'Priority: Tier 1', 'Yes — ethics are important in advertising because advertising shapes consumer perception and purchase decisions at scale, so misleading or manipulative practices can cause real financial and psychological harm. Why ethics matter:

Protects consumer trust — unethical ads erode long-term brand credibility once discovered.
Ensures fair competition — prevents brands from gaining an unfair advantage through deception.
Protects vulnerable audiences — especially children and impressionable consumers.
Maintains industry credibility — repeated unethical practices invite stricter regulation for the whole industry.
Legal compliance — avoids penalties under consumer protection and advertising codes.

This question is best answered together with Flashcards 23–28 (the specific unethical practices) as supporting evidence.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 22, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '2336ef22-ee28-4025-8ab7-16e92a0f915b', 'ae5f8348-55c7-48c7-9538-9e738b187589', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u1-c22-q-1", "text": "Are ethics important in advertising? Discuss.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u1-c22-a-1", "text": "Yes — ethics are important in advertising because advertising shapes consumer perception and purchase decisions at scale, so misleading or manipulative practices can cause real financial and psychological harm. Why ethics matter:", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u1-c22-a-2", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Protects consumer trust — unethical ads erode long-term brand credibility once discovered.", "Ensures fair competition — prevents brands from gaining an unfair advantage through deception.", "Protects vulnerable audiences — especially children and impressionable consumers.", "Maintains industry credibility — repeated unethical practices invite stricter regulation for the whole industry.", "Legal compliance — avoids penalties under consumer protection and advertising codes."]}, {"id": "adv-u1-c22-a-3", "text": "This question is best answered together with Flashcards 23–28 (the specific unethical practices) as supporting evidence.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:45.717564+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:45.717564+00'),
('b8f0668b-2d62-400e-9d79-c3f4d6f70199', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'What is Shock Advertising? Why is it used, and why is it controversial?', 'Priority: Tier 2', 'Meaning: Shock advertising deliberately uses provocative, taboo, or disturbing imagery/messaging to grab attention and generate strong emotional reaction and discussion. Why brands use it: Cuts through media clutter; generates free publicity/word-of-mouth; creates strong emotional recall.

Why it is controversial: Can cause genuine offence or distress to audiences; risk of brand backlash if perceived as exploitative; may normalise disturbing imagery for the sake of attention.

Example: Road-safety awareness campaigns that use graphic accident imagery to provoke a strong emotional response.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 24, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '2336ef22-ee28-4025-8ab7-16e92a0f915b', 'ae5f8348-55c7-48c7-9538-9e738b187589', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u1-c24-q-1", "text": "What is Shock Advertising? Why is it used, and why is it controversial?", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u1-c24-a-1", "text": "Meaning: Shock advertising deliberately uses provocative, taboo, or disturbing imagery/messaging to grab attention and generate strong emotional reaction and discussion. Why brands use it: Cuts through media clutter; generates free publicity/word-of-mouth; creates strong emotional recall.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u1-c24-a-2", "text": "Why it is controversial: Can cause genuine offence or distress to audiences; risk of brand backlash if perceived as exploitative; may normalise disturbing imagery for the sake of attention.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u1-c24-a-3", "text": "Example: Road-safety awareness campaigns that use graphic accident imagery to provoke a strong emotional response.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:45.717564+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:45.717564+00'),
('601ce832-3c68-4bd5-ad4f-9f3ebe82879a', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'What is Subliminal Advertising?', 'Priority: Tier 2', 'Meaning: Subliminal advertising refers to messages embedded below the threshold of conscious perception — intended, in theory, to influence the audience without their conscious awareness. Why it is considered unethical: It removes the consumer''s ability to consciously evaluate and consent to being persuaded, which conflicts with the principle of informed, transparent communication expected of ethical advertising.

Note: Its actual real-world effectiveness is widely debated academically; its ethical concern rests primarily on the intent to bypass conscious awareness, not on proven persuasive power.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 25, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '2336ef22-ee28-4025-8ab7-16e92a0f915b', 'ae5f8348-55c7-48c7-9538-9e738b187589', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u1-c25-q-1", "text": "What is Subliminal Advertising?", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u1-c25-a-1", "text": "Meaning: Subliminal advertising refers to messages embedded below the threshold of conscious perception — intended, in theory, to influence the audience without their conscious awareness. Why it is considered unethical: It removes the consumer''s ability to consciously evaluate and consent to being persuaded, which conflicts with the principle of informed, transparent communication expected of ethical advertising.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u1-c25-a-2", "text": "Note: Its actual real-world effectiveness is widely debated academically; its ethical concern rests primarily on the intent to bypass conscious awareness, not on proven persuasive power.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:45.717564+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:45.717564+00'),
('9389409a-7de2-4150-b6c9-ff92a5997dda', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'What is a Weasel Claim? Explain with an example.', 'Priority: Tier 1', 'Meaning: A weasel claim uses ambiguous or qualifying words that create a false impression of a strong claim while technically avoiding a false or provable statement — the qualifier "weasels" the advertiser out of a concrete commitment. How it operates: Words like "helps," "may," "up to," or "virtually" soften an absolute claim into a vague, unverifiable one, while the overall impression created is much stronger than the literal wording supports.

Example: "Helps reduce wrinkles in just 7 days" — the word "helps" avoids a guarantee, but the ad''s overall impression suggests a reliable, fast result.

Exam Tip: Distinguish clearly from Puffery: Puffery is an unverifiable opinion ("the best"); a Weasel Claim looks like a factual claim but is deliberately hedged ("helps reduce").', NULL, NULL, NULL, 26, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '2336ef22-ee28-4025-8ab7-16e92a0f915b', 'ae5f8348-55c7-48c7-9538-9e738b187589', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u1-c26-q-1", "text": "What is a Weasel Claim? Explain with an example.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u1-c26-a-1", "text": "Meaning: A weasel claim uses ambiguous or qualifying words that create a false impression of a strong claim while technically avoiding a false or provable statement — the qualifier \"weasels\" the advertiser out of a concrete commitment. How it operates: Words like \"helps,\" \"may,\" \"up to,\" or \"virtually\" soften an absolute claim into a vague, unverifiable one, while the overall impression created is much stronger than the literal wording supports.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u1-c26-a-2", "text": "Example: \"Helps reduce wrinkles in just 7 days\" — the word \"helps\" avoids a guarantee, but the ad''s overall impression suggests a reliable, fast result.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u1-c26-a-3", "text": "Exam Tip: Distinguish clearly from Puffery: Puffery is an unverifiable opinion (\"the best\"); a Weasel Claim looks like a factual claim but is deliberately hedged (\"helps reduce\").", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:45.717564+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:45.717564+00'),
('91510299-6368-47fc-8b63-a2c80f66a280', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'What is Surrogate Advertising? Explain with an example.', 'Priority: Tier 1', 'Meaning: Surrogate advertising promotes a restricted or banned product (such as liquor or tobacco) indirectly, by advertising a different, permissible product under the same brand name — keeping the original brand visible without directly advertising the restricted item. How it operates: A liquor or tobacco brand launches a soda, music CD, or playing-cards line under an identical brand name/logo, and advertises that instead, relying on consumers to associate it back with the original restricted product.

Why it is considered unethical: It circumvents legal restrictions on advertising certain products, indirectly promoting them to audiences (including minors) who would not otherwise be legally targeted.

Example: A liquor brand advertising a branded soda water or music CD line using the identical brand name and imagery.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 27, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '2336ef22-ee28-4025-8ab7-16e92a0f915b', 'ae5f8348-55c7-48c7-9538-9e738b187589', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u1-c27-q-1", "text": "What is Surrogate Advertising? Explain with an example.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u1-c27-a-1", "text": "Meaning: Surrogate advertising promotes a restricted or banned product (such as liquor or tobacco) indirectly, by advertising a different, permissible product under the same brand name — keeping the original brand visible without directly advertising the restricted item. How it operates: A liquor or tobacco brand launches a soda, music CD, or playing-cards line under an identical brand name/logo, and advertises that instead, relying on consumers to associate it back with the original restricted product.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u1-c27-a-2", "text": "Why it is considered unethical: It circumvents legal restrictions on advertising certain products, indirectly promoting them to audiences (including minors) who would not otherwise be legally targeted.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u1-c27-a-3", "text": "Example: A liquor brand advertising a branded soda water or music CD line using the identical brand name and imagery.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:45.717564+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:45.717564+00'),
('11659cda-be69-4bfc-bcf9-8ea1ae7c54f1', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'Discuss the role of social media in advertising.', 'Priority: Tier 2', 'Role of social media:

Direct audience access — brands can reach and engage consumers without relying solely on traditional media.
Influencer-led advertising — leverages creators'' credibility and niche audiences for persuasive reach.
Two-way communication — enables comments, feedback, and real-time customer service, unlike one-way traditional advertising.
Virality/organic reach — shareable content can extend reach beyond the paid audience.
Precise targeting and retargeting — using platform data to reach specific, relevant audiences.

Limitations/challenges: Rapidly changing algorithms affect organic reach unpredictably; risk of negative feedback or backlash spreading quickly; ad fatigue from oversaturated feeds.

Example: A skincare D2C brand relying primarily on Instagram and YouTube influencer partnerships instead of television.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 43, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '2336ef22-ee28-4025-8ab7-16e92a0f915b', '78634207-f3b5-4edc-899b-09356452118c', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u1-c43-q-1", "text": "Discuss the role of social media in advertising.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u1-c43-a-1", "text": "Role of social media:", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph", "italic": true}, {"id": "adv-u1-c43-a-2", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Direct audience access — brands can reach and engage consumers without relying solely on traditional media.", "Influencer-led advertising — leverages creators'' credibility and niche audiences for persuasive reach.", "Two-way communication — enables comments, feedback, and real-time customer service, unlike one-way traditional advertising.", "Virality/organic reach — shareable content can extend reach beyond the paid audience.", "Precise targeting and retargeting — using platform data to reach specific, relevant audiences."]}, {"id": "adv-u1-c43-a-3", "text": "Limitations/challenges: Rapidly changing algorithms affect organic reach unpredictably; risk of negative feedback or backlash spreading quickly; ad fatigue from oversaturated feeds.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u1-c43-a-4", "text": "Example: A skincare D2C brand relying primarily on Instagram and YouTube influencer partnerships instead of television.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:45.717564+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:45.717564+00'),
('46dd07f9-1278-462b-9a23-0fa987e97944', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'What is Comparative Advertising? Is it ethical?', 'Priority: Tier 1', 'Meaning: Comparative advertising directly or indirectly compares one''s own product to a competitor''s, usually to highlight a superior attribute such as price, performance, or quality. Ethical dimension:

Can be ethical when comparisons are factual, verifiable, and fair.
Becomes unethical when it disparages a competitor unfairly, uses misleading data, or distorts the comparison to create a false impression.

Example: A detergent brand''s ad showing a side-by-side stain-removal comparison against an unnamed "ordinary" competitor.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 28, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '2336ef22-ee28-4025-8ab7-16e92a0f915b', 'ae5f8348-55c7-48c7-9538-9e738b187589', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u1-c28-q-1", "text": "What is Comparative Advertising? Is it ethical?", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u1-c28-a-1", "text": "Meaning: Comparative advertising directly or indirectly compares one''s own product to a competitor''s, usually to highlight a superior attribute such as price, performance, or quality. Ethical dimension:", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph", "italic": true}, {"id": "adv-u1-c28-a-2", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Can be ethical when comparisons are factual, verifiable, and fair.", "Becomes unethical when it disparages a competitor unfairly, uses misleading data, or distorts the comparison to create a false impression."]}, {"id": "adv-u1-c28-a-3", "text": "Example: A detergent brand''s ad showing a side-by-side stain-removal comparison against an unnamed \"ordinary\" competitor.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:45.717564+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:45.717564+00'),
('edd1e8eb-4880-46be-bc65-fe35b801c18f', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'Distinguish between Puffery, Weasel Claims, and Surrogate Advertising.', 'Priority: Tier 2', 'Basis | Puffery | Weasel Claim | Surrogate Advertising
Nature | Exaggerated opinion | Hedged/qualified factual-sounding claim | Indirect promotion of a restricted product
Legality | Generally legal | Legally grey, ethically criticised | Often specifically restricted by law
Consumer risk | Unrealistic expectations | False sense of a guarantee | Indirect exposure to banned-product branding
Example | "World''s best taste" | "Helps reduce wrinkles" | Liquor brand''s soda-water ad

Exam Tip: A "discuss any four unethical practices" question (as in Oct 2024) is best answered by picking four of Puffery, Shock Ads, Subliminal, Weasel Claim, Surrogate, and Comparative — each written as a mini-flashcard answer.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 29, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '2336ef22-ee28-4025-8ab7-16e92a0f915b', 'ae5f8348-55c7-48c7-9538-9e738b187589', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u1-c29-q-1", "text": "Distinguish between Puffery, Weasel Claims, and Surrogate Advertising.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u1-c29-a-1", "rows": [["Basis", "Puffery", "Weasel Claim", "Surrogate Advertising"], ["Nature", "Exaggerated opinion", "Hedged/qualified factual-sounding claim", "Indirect promotion of a restricted product"], ["Legality", "Generally legal", "Legally grey, ethically criticised", "Often specifically restricted by law"], ["Consumer risk", "Unrealistic expectations", "False sense of a guarantee", "Indirect exposure to banned-product branding"], ["Example", "\"World''s best taste\"", "\"Helps reduce wrinkles\"", "Liquor brand''s soda-water ad"]], "type": "table"}, {"id": "adv-u1-c29-a-2", "text": "Exam Tip: A \"discuss any four unethical practices\" question (as in Oct 2024) is best answered by picking four of Puffery, Shock Ads, Subliminal, Weasel Claim, Surrogate, and Comparative — each written as a mini-flashcard answer.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:45.717564+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:45.717564+00'),
('6b534be8-75e4-4462-96b9-15be5bb2720e', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'Explain the Code of Ethics that governs advertising.', 'Priority: Tier 2', 'Meaning: A code of ethics in advertising is a set of self-regulatory principles that advertisers, agencies, and media are expected to follow to ensure truthful, fair, and responsible communication. Broad principles typically covered:

Advertisements should be truthful and not misleading.
Claims should be capable of substantiation.
Advertising should not offend generally accepted standards of decency.
Advertising directed at children should be handled with special care.
Advertising should not denigrate a competitor unfairly.
Advertisements should not encourage unsafe practices.

Note: Exact clause-level rules of any specific code are outside the scope of general Unit 1 preparation unless your professor has specified a particular code in class — treat these as broad, widely recognised principles rather than a specific statute.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 30, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '2336ef22-ee28-4025-8ab7-16e92a0f915b', 'ae5f8348-55c7-48c7-9538-9e738b187589', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u1-c30-q-1", "text": "Explain the Code of Ethics that governs advertising.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u1-c30-a-1", "text": "Meaning: A code of ethics in advertising is a set of self-regulatory principles that advertisers, agencies, and media are expected to follow to ensure truthful, fair, and responsible communication. Broad principles typically covered:", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph", "italic": true}, {"id": "adv-u1-c30-a-2", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Advertisements should be truthful and not misleading.", "Claims should be capable of substantiation.", "Advertising should not offend generally accepted standards of decency.", "Advertising directed at children should be handled with special care.", "Advertising should not denigrate a competitor unfairly.", "Advertisements should not encourage unsafe practices."]}, {"id": "adv-u1-c30-a-3", "text": "Note: Exact clause-level rules of any specific code are outside the scope of general Unit 1 preparation unless your professor has specified a particular code in class — treat these as broad, widely recognised principles rather than a specific statute.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:45.717564+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:45.717564+00'),
('2b6d9a11-3d3b-4bbb-a6a6-05fc67890b71', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'What is the role of regulatory bodies in advertising?', 'Priority: Tier 2', 'Meaning: Regulatory bodies are self-regulatory or statutory organisations that monitor advertising content to ensure it is legal, decent, honest, and truthful, and that address consumer complaints against misleading ads. Typical functions:

Setting and updating a code of advertising practice.
Reviewing consumer/competitor complaints against specific advertisements.
Directing withdrawal or modification of ads found to be misleading.
Issuing guidelines for sensitive categories (health, children''s products, financial products).

In India, self-regulation of advertising content is coordinated through an industry advertising standards body, working alongside statutory consumer-protection law; students should confirm the exact current body/authority name and powers from their prescribed textbook or latest class notes, since regulatory structures are periodically updated.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 31, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '2336ef22-ee28-4025-8ab7-16e92a0f915b', 'ae5f8348-55c7-48c7-9538-9e738b187589', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u1-c31-q-1", "text": "What is the role of regulatory bodies in advertising?", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u1-c31-a-1", "text": "Meaning: Regulatory bodies are self-regulatory or statutory organisations that monitor advertising content to ensure it is legal, decent, honest, and truthful, and that address consumer complaints against misleading ads. Typical functions:", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph", "italic": true}, {"id": "adv-u1-c31-a-2", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Setting and updating a code of advertising practice.", "Reviewing consumer/competitor complaints against specific advertisements.", "Directing withdrawal or modification of ads found to be misleading.", "Issuing guidelines for sensitive categories (health, children''s products, financial products)."]}, {"id": "adv-u1-c31-a-3", "text": "In India, self-regulation of advertising content is coordinated through an industry advertising standards body, working alongside statutory consumer-protection law; students should confirm the exact current body/authority name and powers from their prescribed textbook or latest class notes, since regulatory structures are periodically updated.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:45.717564+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:45.717564+00'),
('563b97c1-e219-480c-b38a-62515e4c05c6', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'Briefly explain laws and regulations relevant to advertising.', 'Priority: Tier 3', 'Broad scope: Beyond voluntary codes of ethics, advertising in India also operates within statutory consumer-protection and sector-specific legal frameworks that prohibit false, misleading, or unfair trade practices, and impose special restrictions on categories like tobacco, alcohol, and pharmaceuticals. General themes tested:

Prohibition of false or misleading claims.
Restrictions on advertising banned/restricted products (linked to Surrogate Advertising).
Special protections for vulnerable audiences, particularly children.

Note: Specific act names, sections, or penalty amounts should be verified against your current prescribed textbook/class notes rather than memorised from this general overview, since legal provisions can be updated.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 32, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '2336ef22-ee28-4025-8ab7-16e92a0f915b', 'ae5f8348-55c7-48c7-9538-9e738b187589', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u1-c32-q-1", "text": "Briefly explain laws and regulations relevant to advertising.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u1-c32-a-1", "text": "Broad scope: Beyond voluntary codes of ethics, advertising in India also operates within statutory consumer-protection and sector-specific legal frameworks that prohibit false, misleading, or unfair trade practices, and impose special restrictions on categories like tobacco, alcohol, and pharmaceuticals. General themes tested:", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph", "italic": true}, {"id": "adv-u1-c32-a-2", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Prohibition of false or misleading claims.", "Restrictions on advertising banned/restricted products (linked to Surrogate Advertising).", "Special protections for vulnerable audiences, particularly children."]}, {"id": "adv-u1-c32-a-3", "text": "Note: Specific act names, sections, or penalty amounts should be verified against your current prescribed textbook/class notes rather than memorised from this general overview, since legal provisions can be updated.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:45.717564+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:45.717564+00'),
('92a3acbd-38a7-4d9f-9b97-52c0dfe676f4', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'Why must advertising be integrated with other communication tools?', 'Priority: Tier 3', 'Integration (Integrated Marketing Communications, IMC) ensures advertising, sales promotion, public relations, direct marketing and digital/social media all deliver one consistent brand message instead of fragmented or contradictory signals. Benefits:

Reinforces the same positioning across every consumer touchpoint.
Increases the total impact of the promotional budget (synergy).
Builds a more coherent, trustworthy brand image.
Avoids consumer confusion from mixed messages across channels.

Exam Tip: Use the phrase "one sight, one sound, one sell" if you recall it — it is a widely used shorthand for IMC in Indian textbooks and is safe to use as it reflects standard, non-fabricated terminology.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 20, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ef0d1cf3-b12e-43ce-96f4-c320b8780da5', 'fcd68157-ab63-4d53-b5ea-c391ba9fc99a', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u2-c20-q-1", "text": "Why must advertising be integrated with other communication tools?", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u2-c20-a-1", "text": "Integration (Integrated Marketing Communications, IMC) ensures advertising, sales promotion, public relations, direct marketing and digital/social media all deliver one consistent brand message instead of fragmented or contradictory signals. Benefits:", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u2-c20-a-2", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Reinforces the same positioning across every consumer touchpoint.", "Increases the total impact of the promotional budget (synergy).", "Builds a more coherent, trustworthy brand image.", "Avoids consumer confusion from mixed messages across channels."]}, {"id": "adv-u2-c20-a-3", "text": "Exam Tip: Use the phrase \"one sight, one sound, one sell\" if you recall it — it is a widely used shorthand for IMC in Indian textbooks and is safe to use as it reflects standard, non-fabricated terminology.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:48.671005+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:48.671005+00'),
('727a1fa0-9bb9-4650-8181-ad16c8ab42ae', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'What is CSR (Corporate Social Responsibility) in the context of advertising?', 'Priority: Tier 2', 'Meaning: CSR advertising communicates a company''s voluntary social, environmental, or community initiatives, aiming to demonstrate that the organisation operates responsibly beyond pure profit-seeking. Purpose: Builds long-term brand trust and reputation; demonstrates accountability to society and environment; differentiates the brand on values, not just product features.

Example: A bank''s campaign highlighting its rural financial-literacy programme rather than any specific banking product.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 33, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '2336ef22-ee28-4025-8ab7-16e92a0f915b', 'ae5f8348-55c7-48c7-9538-9e738b187589', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u1-c33-q-1", "text": "What is CSR (Corporate Social Responsibility) in the context of advertising?", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u1-c33-a-1", "text": "Meaning: CSR advertising communicates a company''s voluntary social, environmental, or community initiatives, aiming to demonstrate that the organisation operates responsibly beyond pure profit-seeking. Purpose: Builds long-term brand trust and reputation; demonstrates accountability to society and environment; differentiates the brand on values, not just product features.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u1-c33-a-2", "text": "Example: A bank''s campaign highlighting its rural financial-literacy programme rather than any specific banking product.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:45.717564+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:45.717564+00'),
('96654815-6922-4963-8664-57ef8479d148', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'What is Public Service Advertising?', 'Priority: Tier 2', 'Meaning: Public Service Advertising (PSA) communicates messages in the public interest — health, safety, civic duty, social awareness — typically without a commercial/profit motive, often run by government bodies or non-profits. Example: A road-safety "wear your seatbelt" campaign or a polio-vaccination awareness drive.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 34, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '2336ef22-ee28-4025-8ab7-16e92a0f915b', 'ae5f8348-55c7-48c7-9538-9e738b187589', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u1-c34-q-1", "text": "What is Public Service Advertising?", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u1-c34-a-1", "text": "Meaning: Public Service Advertising (PSA) communicates messages in the public interest — health, safety, civic duty, social awareness — typically without a commercial/profit motive, often run by government bodies or non-profits. Example: A road-safety \"wear your seatbelt\" campaign or a polio-vaccination awareness drive.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:45.717564+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:45.717564+00'),
('9e338780-2c8c-4cfe-8098-f1b1bbfa1821', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'What is Corporate Advertising, and how is it different from Advocacy Advertising?', 'Priority: Tier 3', 'Corporate Advertising: Promotes the overall image, values, or reputation of a company as a whole, rather than any single product. Example: A conglomerate''s "we power progress" institutional campaign. Advocacy Advertising: Promotes a particular viewpoint, cause, or position on a social, economic, or political issue, often to shape public opinion rather than to sell a product. Example: An industry association running ads advocating for a specific policy position.

Basis | Corporate | Advocacy
Focus | Company''s own image/reputation | An issue, cause, or viewpoint
Objective | Build trust in the organisation | Shape opinion or influence policy', NULL, NULL, NULL, 35, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '2336ef22-ee28-4025-8ab7-16e92a0f915b', 'ae5f8348-55c7-48c7-9538-9e738b187589', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u1-c35-q-1", "text": "What is Corporate Advertising, and how is it different from Advocacy Advertising?", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u1-c35-a-1", "text": "Corporate Advertising: Promotes the overall image, values, or reputation of a company as a whole, rather than any single product. Example: A conglomerate''s \"we power progress\" institutional campaign. Advocacy Advertising: Promotes a particular viewpoint, cause, or position on a social, economic, or political issue, often to shape public opinion rather than to sell a product. Example: An industry association running ads advocating for a specific policy position.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph", "italic": true}, {"id": "adv-u1-c35-a-2", "rows": [["Basis", "Corporate", "Advocacy"], ["Focus", "Company''s own image/reputation", "An issue, cause, or viewpoint"], ["Objective", "Build trust in the organisation", "Shape opinion or influence policy"]], "type": "table"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:45.717564+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:45.717564+00'),
('2b2f1e8a-95a8-4228-95b8-134d5d241a8b', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'Distinguish between CSR, Public Service, Corporate, and Advocacy Advertising.', 'Priority: Tier 2', 'Type | Who runs it | Primary aim
CSR Advertising | A company | Showcase social/environmental initiatives
Public Service Advertising | Government/NGOs (usually) | Serve the public interest, no profit motive
Corporate Advertising | A company | Build the company''s overall image
Advocacy Advertising | Company, association, or interest group | Advance a viewpoint on an issue

All four are non-product-focused, which is what distinguishes them collectively from consumer/industrial advertising — but they differ in sponsor and underlying motive.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 36, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '2336ef22-ee28-4025-8ab7-16e92a0f915b', 'ae5f8348-55c7-48c7-9538-9e738b187589', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u1-c36-q-1", "text": "Distinguish between CSR, Public Service, Corporate, and Advocacy Advertising.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u1-c36-a-1", "rows": [["Type", "Who runs it", "Primary aim"], ["CSR Advertising", "A company", "Showcase social/environmental initiatives"], ["Public Service Advertising", "Government/NGOs (usually)", "Serve the public interest, no profit motive"], ["Corporate Advertising", "A company", "Build the company''s overall image"], ["Advocacy Advertising", "Company, association, or interest group", "Advance a viewpoint on an issue"]], "type": "table"}, {"id": "adv-u1-c36-a-2", "text": "All four are non-product-focused, which is what distinguishes them collectively from consumer/industrial advertising — but they differ in sponsor and underlying motive.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:45.717564+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:45.717564+00'),
('8d655667-8706-4601-8ed8-d612a9715cdf', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'Explain the social impact of advertising with relevant examples.', 'Priority: Tier 1', 'Advertising shapes not only what people buy but also social attitudes, aspirations, and norms, because of its wide reach and repetition. Positive social impact:

Spreads awareness on health, safety, and social causes through public service advertising.
Can promote inclusive, diverse representation when done responsibly.
Educates consumers about new products, technologies, and choices.

Negative/debated social impact:

Can encourage materialism and unrealistic lifestyle comparisons.
May reinforce stereotypes if not handled responsibly.
Can create pressure to conform to advertised trends.

Example: A menstrual-hygiene brand''s awareness campaigns have been credited with reducing taboo around the subject in Indian media — a positive social impact of advertising.

A balanced answer should note that advertising''s social impact depends heavily on execution and intent, rather than asserting it is uniformly good or bad.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 37, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '2336ef22-ee28-4025-8ab7-16e92a0f915b', 'e5977d67-bafb-4570-b45f-496c3d4f4afd', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u1-c37-q-1", "text": "Explain the social impact of advertising with relevant examples.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u1-c37-a-1", "text": "Advertising shapes not only what people buy but also social attitudes, aspirations, and norms, because of its wide reach and repetition. Positive social impact:", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u1-c37-a-2", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Spreads awareness on health, safety, and social causes through public service advertising.", "Can promote inclusive, diverse representation when done responsibly.", "Educates consumers about new products, technologies, and choices."]}, {"id": "adv-u1-c37-a-3", "text": "Negative/debated social impact:", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph", "italic": true}, {"id": "adv-u1-c37-a-4", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Can encourage materialism and unrealistic lifestyle comparisons.", "May reinforce stereotypes if not handled responsibly.", "Can create pressure to conform to advertised trends."]}, {"id": "adv-u1-c37-a-5", "text": "Example: A menstrual-hygiene brand''s awareness campaigns have been credited with reducing taboo around the subject in Indian media — a positive social impact of advertising.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u1-c37-a-6", "text": "A balanced answer should note that advertising''s social impact depends heavily on execution and intent, rather than asserting it is uniformly good or bad.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:45.717564+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:45.717564+00'),
('cc9a7f12-4890-4e23-a420-f3fe8d5bf860', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'Explain the cultural impact of advertising.', 'Priority: Tier 3', 'Shapes popular culture — memorable taglines and jingles often enter everyday language.
Can homogenise or globalise culture — international brands'' campaigns can spread similar aesthetic/lifestyle ideals across markets.
Can also celebrate local culture — festival- and regional-language advertising that reinforces local traditions and identity.
Influences fashion and lifestyle trends through celebrity and influencer-led campaigns.

Example: Festive-season advertising in India (Diwali/Eid/Christmas-themed campaigns) that both reflects and reinforces cultural traditions.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 38, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '2336ef22-ee28-4025-8ab7-16e92a0f915b', 'e5977d67-bafb-4570-b45f-496c3d4f4afd', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u1-c38-q-1", "text": "Explain the cultural impact of advertising.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u1-c38-a-1", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Shapes popular culture — memorable taglines and jingles often enter everyday language.", "Can homogenise or globalise culture — international brands'' campaigns can spread similar aesthetic/lifestyle ideals across markets.", "Can also celebrate local culture — festival- and regional-language advertising that reinforces local traditions and identity.", "Influences fashion and lifestyle trends through celebrity and influencer-led campaigns."]}, {"id": "adv-u1-c38-a-2", "text": "Example: Festive-season advertising in India (Diwali/Eid/Christmas-themed campaigns) that both reflects and reinforces cultural traditions.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:45.717564+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:45.717564+00'),
('af833f93-ac28-4d2d-bd81-44e3ed9b3d60', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'Explain the economic impact of advertising.', 'Priority: Tier 3', 'Stimulates demand — increases consumption which can support production and economies of scale, potentially lowering unit cost.
Funds the media industry — advertising revenue sustains newspapers, television, and digital platforms.
Creates employment — across agencies, media, production, and related creative industries.
Encourages competition — can drive innovation and quality improvement among competing brands.
Cost consideration — heavy ad spending can also raise the final product price, a trade-off worth noting for a balanced answer.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 39, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '2336ef22-ee28-4025-8ab7-16e92a0f915b', 'e5977d67-bafb-4570-b45f-496c3d4f4afd', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u1-c39-q-1", "text": "Explain the economic impact of advertising.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u1-c39-a-1", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Stimulates demand — increases consumption which can support production and economies of scale, potentially lowering unit cost.", "Funds the media industry — advertising revenue sustains newspapers, television, and digital platforms.", "Creates employment — across agencies, media, production, and related creative industries.", "Encourages competition — can drive innovation and quality improvement among competing brands.", "Cost consideration — heavy ad spending can also raise the final product price, a trade-off worth noting for a balanced answer."]}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:45.717564+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:45.717564+00'),
('299b313b-2c4a-4fbb-a379-c158f3fde549', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'Discuss the impact of advertising on children.', 'Priority: Tier 3', 'Children are considered a particularly impressionable audience with limited ability to critically evaluate persuasive intent.
Advertising can influence pester-power purchase requests toward parents (toys, snacks, gadgets).
Repeated exposure to advertising for unhealthy foods has raised public-health concerns in academic discussion.
This is why codes of ethics and several regulatory frameworks give special protection to advertising directed at, or featuring, children.

A well-rounded answer acknowledges both the persuasive risk and the industry/regulatory safeguards that exist specifically because of this concern.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 40, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '2336ef22-ee28-4025-8ab7-16e92a0f915b', 'e5977d67-bafb-4570-b45f-496c3d4f4afd', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u1-c40-q-1", "text": "Discuss the impact of advertising on children.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u1-c40-a-1", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Children are considered a particularly impressionable audience with limited ability to critically evaluate persuasive intent.", "Advertising can influence pester-power purchase requests toward parents (toys, snacks, gadgets).", "Repeated exposure to advertising for unhealthy foods has raised public-health concerns in academic discussion.", "This is why codes of ethics and several regulatory frameworks give special protection to advertising directed at, or featuring, children."]}, {"id": "adv-u1-c40-a-2", "text": "A well-rounded answer acknowledges both the persuasive risk and the industry/regulatory safeguards that exist specifically because of this concern.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:45.717564+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:45.717564+00'),
('ca02359f-fc1d-4c79-87bb-db578bf25ed8', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'Discuss the relationship between women and advertising.', 'Priority: Tier 3', 'Historical criticism: advertising has often been criticised academically for stereotypical portrayals of women (limited to domestic or beauty-focused roles).
Shift toward progressive portrayal: many contemporary campaigns actively challenge stereotypes and portray women in varied professional and personal roles.
Objectification concern: academic and regulatory discussion continues around the use of women primarily as visual/decorative elements unrelated to the product.
Women as a target segment: a large and influential consumer segment across nearly every product category, making responsible representation both an ethical and commercial consideration.

A balanced exam answer should present this as an evolving, debated relationship rather than a single fixed conclusion.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 41, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '2336ef22-ee28-4025-8ab7-16e92a0f915b', 'e5977d67-bafb-4570-b45f-496c3d4f4afd', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u1-c41-q-1", "text": "Discuss the relationship between women and advertising.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u1-c41-a-1", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Historical criticism: advertising has often been criticised academically for stereotypical portrayals of women (limited to domestic or beauty-focused roles).", "Shift toward progressive portrayal: many contemporary campaigns actively challenge stereotypes and portray women in varied professional and personal roles.", "Objectification concern: academic and regulatory discussion continues around the use of women primarily as visual/decorative elements unrelated to the product.", "Women as a target segment: a large and influential consumer segment across nearly every product category, making responsible representation both an ethical and commercial consideration."]}, {"id": "adv-u1-c41-a-2", "text": "A balanced exam answer should present this as an evolving, debated relationship rather than a single fixed conclusion.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:45.717564+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:45.717564+00'),
('f4a24929-9b84-449f-b809-95fe61a63f2c', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'Explain advertising in the digital age.', 'Priority: Tier 2', 'Meaning: Digital advertising refers to advertising delivered through internet-based platforms — search engines, websites, apps, and social media — rather than traditional print or broadcast media. Key features:

Targeting — ads can be shown to specific audience segments based on behaviour, interest, or demographics.
Measurability — clicks, views, and conversions can be tracked precisely, unlike most traditional media.
Interactivity — consumers can engage, comment, share, or purchase directly from the ad.
Cost flexibility — campaigns can often be run at flexible budgets compared to traditional mass media.
Speed — campaigns can be launched, tested, and modified much faster than traditional media buys.

Example: A D2C brand running targeted Instagram ads to a specific age and interest segment, tracked in real time.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 42, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '2336ef22-ee28-4025-8ab7-16e92a0f915b', '78634207-f3b5-4edc-899b-09356452118c', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u1-c42-q-1", "text": "Explain advertising in the digital age.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u1-c42-a-1", "text": "Meaning: Digital advertising refers to advertising delivered through internet-based platforms — search engines, websites, apps, and social media — rather than traditional print or broadcast media. Key features:", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph", "italic": true}, {"id": "adv-u1-c42-a-2", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Targeting — ads can be shown to specific audience segments based on behaviour, interest, or demographics.", "Measurability — clicks, views, and conversions can be tracked precisely, unlike most traditional media.", "Interactivity — consumers can engage, comment, share, or purchase directly from the ad.", "Cost flexibility — campaigns can often be run at flexible budgets compared to traditional mass media.", "Speed — campaigns can be launched, tested, and modified much faster than traditional media buys."]}, {"id": "adv-u1-c42-a-3", "text": "Example: A D2C brand running targeted Instagram ads to a specific age and interest segment, tracked in real time.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:45.717564+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:45.717564+00'),
('d11db4e5-d037-4bc4-8574-c8195f317744', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'How does digital advertising differ from traditional advertising?', 'Priority: Tier 2', 'Basis | Traditional advertising | Digital advertising
Direction | One-way (broadcast) | Two-way (interactive)
Targeting | Broad/mass audience | Precisely segmented
Measurement | Estimated reach (e.g. circulation, TRPs) | Exact clicks, views, conversions
Cost structure | Typically high, fixed media buys | Flexible, scalable budgets
Speed of change | Slower to launch/modify | Can be launched/adjusted quickly', NULL, NULL, NULL, 44, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '2336ef22-ee28-4025-8ab7-16e92a0f915b', '78634207-f3b5-4edc-899b-09356452118c', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u1-c44-q-1", "text": "How does digital advertising differ from traditional advertising?", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u1-c44-a-1", "rows": [["Basis", "Traditional advertising", "Digital advertising"], ["Direction", "One-way (broadcast)", "Two-way (interactive)"], ["Targeting", "Broad/mass audience", "Precisely segmented"], ["Measurement", "Estimated reach (e.g. circulation, TRPs)", "Exact clicks, views, conversions"], ["Cost structure", "Typically high, fixed media buys", "Flexible, scalable budgets"], ["Speed of change", "Slower to launch/modify", "Can be launched/adjusted quickly"]], "type": "table"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:45.717564+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:45.717564+00'),
('ec2b4e15-8cb2-488d-8583-b917c48bd3d5', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'What is the advertising planning process? Why is it important?', 'Priority: Tier 1', 'The advertising planning process is the systematic sequence of decisions a firm follows to move from a marketing plan to a specific, budgeted, measurable advertising campaign. Importance:

Ensures advertising decisions flow logically from overall marketing strategy rather than being ad-hoc.
Aligns creative and media choices with a clearly defined target audience and objective.
Provides a basis for budgeting and for measuring campaign effectiveness afterward.
Reduces wastage of promotional spend by forcing analysis before execution.
Creates accountability — each stage can be reviewed and evaluated.

Exam Tip: Always open a planning-process answer with "Advertising planning does not happen in isolation — it flows from the marketing plan" — examiners reward this linkage.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ef0d1cf3-b12e-43ce-96f4-c320b8780da5', '1ffdd554-b335-49b0-8912-1b104b209900', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u2-c1-q-1", "text": "What is the advertising planning process? Why is it important?", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u2-c1-a-1", "text": "The advertising planning process is the systematic sequence of decisions a firm follows to move from a marketing plan to a specific, budgeted, measurable advertising campaign. Importance:", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u2-c1-a-2", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Ensures advertising decisions flow logically from overall marketing strategy rather than being ad-hoc.", "Aligns creative and media choices with a clearly defined target audience and objective.", "Provides a basis for budgeting and for measuring campaign effectiveness afterward.", "Reduces wastage of promotional spend by forcing analysis before execution.", "Creates accountability — each stage can be reviewed and evaluated."]}, {"id": "adv-u2-c1-a-3", "text": "Exam Tip: Always open a planning-process answer with \"Advertising planning does not happen in isolation — it flows from the marketing plan\" — examiners reward this linkage.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:48.671005+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:48.671005+00'),
('5e353b23-3245-4080-8ed4-60d77c49e2c8', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'Explain the steps involved in the advertising planning process. (Core PYQ theme — 2025 Q2a)', 'Priority: Tier 1', 'Review of the Marketing Plan — understand overall marketing objectives, strategy and budget within which advertising must operate.
Background/Situational Analysis — study company, product, competitors, consumers and market trends (SWOT-type analysis) related to the advertising issue.
Setting Marketing Objectives — restate what the brand must achieve in the market (sales, share, launch, etc.).
Setting Advertising Objectives — translate marketing goals into communication goals (awareness, recall, attitude change, trial).
Identifying the Target Audience — define who the advertising must reach (demographic, psychographic, behavioural profile).
Brand Positioning — decide how the brand should be perceived relative to competitors in the target audience''s mind.
Developing Creative Strategy — decide the central creative idea/theme that will execute the positioning.
Developing Message Strategy — decide what exactly will be said (appeal, tone, copy platform).
Developing Media Strategy — decide which media, reach, frequency and scheduling will deliver the message.
Integration with Other Communication Tools — coordinate advertising with sales promotion, PR, direct marketing, digital/social media for a unified brand voice.
Budgeting — allocate funds across the above elements.
Implementation and Evaluation — execute the plan and measure results (pre-testing/post-testing) against objectives.

Exam Tip: For a 10-mark answer, write each step as a numbered heading with 1–2 lines of explanation — examiners scan for structure, not paragraphs.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 2, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ef0d1cf3-b12e-43ce-96f4-c320b8780da5', '1ffdd554-b335-49b0-8912-1b104b209900', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u2-c2-q-1", "text": "Explain the steps involved in the advertising planning process. (Core PYQ theme — 2025 Q2a)", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u2-c2-a-1", "type": "numberList", "items": ["Review of the Marketing Plan — understand overall marketing objectives, strategy and budget within which advertising must operate.", "Background/Situational Analysis — study company, product, competitors, consumers and market trends (SWOT-type analysis) related to the advertising issue.", "Setting Marketing Objectives — restate what the brand must achieve in the market (sales, share, launch, etc.).", "Setting Advertising Objectives — translate marketing goals into communication goals (awareness, recall, attitude change, trial).", "Identifying the Target Audience — define who the advertising must reach (demographic, psychographic, behavioural profile).", "Brand Positioning — decide how the brand should be perceived relative to competitors in the target audience''s mind.", "Developing Creative Strategy — decide the central creative idea/theme that will execute the positioning.", "Developing Message Strategy — decide what exactly will be said (appeal, tone, copy platform).", "Developing Media Strategy — decide which media, reach, frequency and scheduling will deliver the message.", "Integration with Other Communication Tools — coordinate advertising with sales promotion, PR, direct marketing, digital/social media for a unified brand voice.", "Budgeting — allocate funds across the above elements.", "Implementation and Evaluation — execute the plan and measure results (pre-testing/post-testing) against objectives."]}, {"id": "adv-u2-c2-a-2", "text": "Exam Tip: For a 10-mark answer, write each step as a numbered heading with 1–2 lines of explanation — examiners scan for structure, not paragraphs.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:48.671005+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:48.671005+00'),
('7a806e7e-4e5c-44cf-832f-ce5818160085', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'How are the steps of the advertising planning process interrelated?', 'Priority: Tier 1', 'The steps are sequential and interdependent, not isolated:

The marketing plan sets the boundary within which advertising objectives are framed.
Situational analysis feeds directly into realistic objective-setting.
Advertising objectives must match the target audience''s current stage of awareness.
Positioning decided for that audience shapes the creative and message strategy.
Media strategy is chosen based on where the defined target audience can best be reached with the chosen message.
Integration ensures all communication tools reinforce the same positioning decided earlier.

A weak link at any stage (e.g., a poorly defined target audience) weakens every subsequent decision.

Exam Tip: Draw a simple flow arrow diagram (Marketing Plan → Situation Analysis → Objectives → Target Audience → Positioning → Creative/Message/Media Strategy → Integration → Budget → Evaluation) — examiners give marks for the "neat diagram" instruction even when not explicitly asked.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 3, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ef0d1cf3-b12e-43ce-96f4-c320b8780da5', '1ffdd554-b335-49b0-8912-1b104b209900', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u2-c3-q-1", "text": "How are the steps of the advertising planning process interrelated?", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u2-c3-a-1", "text": "The steps are sequential and interdependent, not isolated:", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u2-c3-a-2", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["The marketing plan sets the boundary within which advertising objectives are framed.", "Situational analysis feeds directly into realistic objective-setting.", "Advertising objectives must match the target audience''s current stage of awareness.", "Positioning decided for that audience shapes the creative and message strategy.", "Media strategy is chosen based on where the defined target audience can best be reached with the chosen message.", "Integration ensures all communication tools reinforce the same positioning decided earlier."]}, {"id": "adv-u2-c3-a-3", "text": "A weak link at any stage (e.g., a poorly defined target audience) weakens every subsequent decision.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u2-c3-a-4", "text": "Exam Tip: Draw a simple flow arrow diagram (Marketing Plan → Situation Analysis → Objectives → Target Audience → Positioning → Creative/Message/Media Strategy → Integration → Budget → Evaluation) — examiners give marks for the \"neat diagram\" instruction even when not explicitly asked.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:48.671005+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:48.671005+00'),
('4fc17a90-af43-45fd-8a2c-8d54e36d9ed6', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'Illustrate the advertising planning process with a practical example.', 'Priority: Tier 1', 'Example — a new organic breakfast cereal brand:

Marketing objective: capture 5% share of the urban health-food segment in year one.
Situational analysis: rising health consciousness, competition from established cereal brands.
Advertising objective: build awareness and trial among health-conscious urban millennials.
Target audience: working professionals aged 25–40, health-conscious, urban.
Positioning: "the honest breakfast — no preservatives, all nutrition."
Creative strategy: relatable morning-routine storytelling.
Message strategy: rational appeal (nutrition facts) blended with emotional appeal (caring for family health).
Media strategy: digital-first (Instagram, YouTube) with in-store sampling.
Integration: coupon-based sales promotion + influencer PR to reinforce the same message.

Exam Tip: Examiners explicitly ask for "relevant examples" — always attach one running example across the whole answer rather than switching brands per step.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 4, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ef0d1cf3-b12e-43ce-96f4-c320b8780da5', '1ffdd554-b335-49b0-8912-1b104b209900', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u2-c4-q-1", "text": "Illustrate the advertising planning process with a practical example.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u2-c4-a-1", "text": "Example — a new organic breakfast cereal brand:", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph", "italic": true}, {"id": "adv-u2-c4-a-2", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Marketing objective: capture 5% share of the urban health-food segment in year one.", "Situational analysis: rising health consciousness, competition from established cereal brands.", "Advertising objective: build awareness and trial among health-conscious urban millennials.", "Target audience: working professionals aged 25–40, health-conscious, urban.", "Positioning: \"the honest breakfast — no preservatives, all nutrition.\"", "Creative strategy: relatable morning-routine storytelling.", "Message strategy: rational appeal (nutrition facts) blended with emotional appeal (caring for family health).", "Media strategy: digital-first (Instagram, YouTube) with in-store sampling.", "Integration: coupon-based sales promotion + influencer PR to reinforce the same message."]}, {"id": "adv-u2-c4-a-3", "text": "Exam Tip: Examiners explicitly ask for \"relevant examples\" — always attach one running example across the whole answer rather than switching brands per step.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:48.671005+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:48.671005+00'),
('6c9d9125-8eed-46d2-9b23-ee1b5c6483dc', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'A company is launching a new product. How would you develop an advertising plan for it?', 'Priority: Tier 1 (Application)', 'Apply the planning process as a case:

Study the parent marketing plan — launch objective, budget, target markets.
Conduct situational analysis — competitor advertising, category conventions, consumer readiness.
Set advertising objectives specific to launch — primarily awareness and trial, since the product is new.
Define the target audience narrowly for the launch phase (innovators/early adopters).
Decide positioning — a clear point of difference versus existing players.
Build creative and message strategy around that single point of difference.
Select media with high reach for fast awareness build-up (mass media/digital).
Integrate with launch-phase sales promotion (introductory offers) and PR (launch events, reviews).
Set an appropriate budget, weighted heavily toward the launch period.
Plan pre-testing of the ad concept before roll-out and post-testing after launch to check awareness levels achieved.

Exam Tip: Application questions are graded on whether you apply each planning step to the given scenario, not on repeating the theory list — name the scenario explicitly at each step.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 5, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ef0d1cf3-b12e-43ce-96f4-c320b8780da5', '1ffdd554-b335-49b0-8912-1b104b209900', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u2-c5-q-1", "text": "A company is launching a new product. How would you develop an advertising plan for it?", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u2-c5-a-1", "text": "Apply the planning process as a case:", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u2-c5-a-2", "type": "numberList", "items": ["Study the parent marketing plan — launch objective, budget, target markets.", "Conduct situational analysis — competitor advertising, category conventions, consumer readiness.", "Set advertising objectives specific to launch — primarily awareness and trial, since the product is new.", "Define the target audience narrowly for the launch phase (innovators/early adopters).", "Decide positioning — a clear point of difference versus existing players.", "Build creative and message strategy around that single point of difference.", "Select media with high reach for fast awareness build-up (mass media/digital).", "Integrate with launch-phase sales promotion (introductory offers) and PR (launch events, reviews).", "Set an appropriate budget, weighted heavily toward the launch period.", "Plan pre-testing of the ad concept before roll-out and post-testing after launch to check awareness levels achieved."]}, {"id": "adv-u2-c5-a-3", "text": "Exam Tip: Application questions are graded on whether you apply each planning step to the given scenario, not on repeating the theory list — name the scenario explicitly at each step.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:48.671005+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:48.671005+00'),
('e99d6af9-1912-446e-99d7-85ba8d0f1148', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'Differentiate between a Marketing Plan and an Advertising Plan.', 'Priority: Tier 2', 'Basis | Marketing Plan | Advertising Plan
Scope | Covers entire marketing mix (product, price, place, promotion) | Sub-set of promotion, focused only on advertising
Objectives | Business/market objectives (sales, share) | Communication objectives (awareness, recall, attitude)
Prepared by | Marketing team/management | Advertising manager/agency, guided by marketing plan
Time horizon | Usually broader, strategic | Often campaign-specific, tactical
Output | Overall strategy and budget across mix elements | Specific creative, message and media decisions

Exam Tip: Always state that the advertising plan is derived from and subordinate to the marketing plan — this single line often fetches a mark on its own.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 6, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ef0d1cf3-b12e-43ce-96f4-c320b8780da5', '1ffdd554-b335-49b0-8912-1b104b209900', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u2-c6-q-1", "text": "Differentiate between a Marketing Plan and an Advertising Plan.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u2-c6-a-1", "rows": [["Basis", "Marketing Plan", "Advertising Plan"], ["Scope", "Covers entire marketing mix (product, price, place, promotion)", "Sub-set of promotion, focused only on advertising"], ["Objectives", "Business/market objectives (sales, share)", "Communication objectives (awareness, recall, attitude)"], ["Prepared by", "Marketing team/management", "Advertising manager/agency, guided by marketing plan"], ["Time horizon", "Usually broader, strategic", "Often campaign-specific, tactical"], ["Output", "Overall strategy and budget across mix elements", "Specific creative, message and media decisions"]], "type": "table"}, {"id": "adv-u2-c6-a-2", "text": "Exam Tip: Always state that the advertising plan is derived from and subordinate to the marketing plan — this single line often fetches a mark on its own.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:48.671005+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:48.671005+00'),
('f2bd1089-d754-40f6-83d9-ca0e48388b6d', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'What is Situational Analysis in advertising planning? What does it cover?', 'Priority: Tier 2', 'Situational analysis is the fact-finding stage where the company studies the internal and external environment relevant to the advertising issue before setting objectives. Covers:

Company analysis — resources, past advertising performance, brand history.
Product analysis — features, USP, life-cycle stage.
Competitor analysis — competitor advertising themes, spend, positioning.
Consumer analysis — needs, attitudes, buying behaviour, media habits.
Market/environment analysis — trends, regulations, economic and social factors.

It essentially answers "where do we stand today?" before deciding "where do we want to go and how?"

Exam Tip: Frame it as a mini-SWOT specific to the advertising issue, not the whole company — examiners penalise generic SWOT dumps.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 7, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ef0d1cf3-b12e-43ce-96f4-c320b8780da5', '1ffdd554-b335-49b0-8912-1b104b209900', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u2-c7-q-1", "text": "What is Situational Analysis in advertising planning? What does it cover?", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u2-c7-a-1", "text": "Situational analysis is the fact-finding stage where the company studies the internal and external environment relevant to the advertising issue before setting objectives. Covers:", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u2-c7-a-2", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Company analysis — resources, past advertising performance, brand history.", "Product analysis — features, USP, life-cycle stage.", "Competitor analysis — competitor advertising themes, spend, positioning.", "Consumer analysis — needs, attitudes, buying behaviour, media habits.", "Market/environment analysis — trends, regulations, economic and social factors."]}, {"id": "adv-u2-c7-a-3", "text": "It essentially answers \"where do we stand today?\" before deciding \"where do we want to go and how?\"", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u2-c7-a-4", "text": "Exam Tip: Frame it as a mini-SWOT specific to the advertising issue, not the whole company — examiners penalise generic SWOT dumps.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:48.671005+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:48.671005+00'),
('dfbd6ffc-b602-4041-aa37-82b424afbb51', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'Differentiate Marketing Objectives from Advertising Objectives.', 'Priority: Tier 2', 'Basis | Marketing Objectives | Advertising Objectives
Nature | Business outcomes | Communication outcomes
Examples | Increase sales by 15%, gain market share, enter new segment | Build brand awareness, create favourable attitude, generate trial, aid recall
Measured by | Sales figures, market share data | Awareness scores, recall tests, attitude surveys
Relationship | Set first, at the marketing-plan stage | Derived from marketing objectives — advertising''s specific contribution to achieving them

Exam Tip: A common trap is treating "increase sales" as an advertising objective — advertising can only influence communication variables that lead to sales, so always translate the marketing goal into a communication goal.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 8, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ef0d1cf3-b12e-43ce-96f4-c320b8780da5', '1ffdd554-b335-49b0-8912-1b104b209900', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u2-c8-q-1", "text": "Differentiate Marketing Objectives from Advertising Objectives.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u2-c8-a-1", "rows": [["Basis", "Marketing Objectives", "Advertising Objectives"], ["Nature", "Business outcomes", "Communication outcomes"], ["Examples", "Increase sales by 15%, gain market share, enter new segment", "Build brand awareness, create favourable attitude, generate trial, aid recall"], ["Measured by", "Sales figures, market share data", "Awareness scores, recall tests, attitude surveys"], ["Relationship", "Set first, at the marketing-plan stage", "Derived from marketing objectives — advertising''s specific contribution to achieving them"]], "type": "table"}, {"id": "adv-u2-c8-a-2", "text": "Exam Tip: A common trap is treating \"increase sales\" as an advertising objective — advertising can only influence communication variables that lead to sales, so always translate the marketing goal into a communication goal.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:48.671005+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:48.671005+00'),
('1ce236eb-595f-4033-8903-4e34839f88c2', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'What is meant by "Target Audience" in advertising planning? Why is defining it important?', 'Priority: Tier 2', 'The target audience is the specific group of people the advertising message is designed to reach and influence, defined using demographic (age, income, gender), geographic, psychographic (lifestyle, values) and behavioural (usage rate, brand loyalty) variables. Importance:

Determines the tone, language and appeal of the message.
Guides media selection (where the audience can be found).
Prevents wastage of the advertising budget on non-prospects.
Enables measurable objectives (e.g., "80% awareness among urban women 25–35").

Exam Tip: Always list at least two segmentation bases (e.g., demographic + psychographic) when defining a target audience in an answer — single-basis definitions look thin.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 9, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ef0d1cf3-b12e-43ce-96f4-c320b8780da5', '1ffdd554-b335-49b0-8912-1b104b209900', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u2-c9-q-1", "text": "What is meant by \"Target Audience\" in advertising planning? Why is defining it important?", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u2-c9-a-1", "text": "The target audience is the specific group of people the advertising message is designed to reach and influence, defined using demographic (age, income, gender), geographic, psychographic (lifestyle, values) and behavioural (usage rate, brand loyalty) variables. Importance:", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u2-c9-a-2", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Determines the tone, language and appeal of the message.", "Guides media selection (where the audience can be found).", "Prevents wastage of the advertising budget on non-prospects.", "Enables measurable objectives (e.g., \"80% awareness among urban women 25–35\")."]}, {"id": "adv-u2-c9-a-3", "text": "Exam Tip: Always list at least two segmentation bases (e.g., demographic + psychographic) when defining a target audience in an answer — single-basis definitions look thin.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:48.671005+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:48.671005+00'),
('150ecf9e-282e-42a9-a34b-3c15fd2d714b', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'What is Brand Personality?', 'Priority: Tier 2', 'Brand personality is the set of human characteristics or traits attributed to a brand, making it feel like it has a "character" (e.g., sincere, exciting, rugged, sophisticated, competent). Advertising, tone of voice, spokespeople and visual style are the main tools used to build a brand''s personality. Example: Harley-Davidson''s personality is rugged and rebellious; Fastrack''s is youthful and cheeky. Exam Tip: When asked to differentiate, personality answers are strongest when you name specific human traits, not generic adjectives like "good" or "nice."', NULL, NULL, NULL, 14, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ef0d1cf3-b12e-43ce-96f4-c320b8780da5', 'ac96dcd7-a8ee-488b-a4e5-5bfad3645838', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u2-c14-q-1", "text": "What is Brand Personality?", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u2-c14-a-1", "text": "Brand personality is the set of human characteristics or traits attributed to a brand, making it feel like it has a \"character\" (e.g., sincere, exciting, rugged, sophisticated, competent). Advertising, tone of voice, spokespeople and visual style are the main tools used to build a brand''s personality. Example: Harley-Davidson''s personality is rugged and rebellious; Fastrack''s is youthful and cheeky. Exam Tip: When asked to differentiate, personality answers are strongest when you name specific human traits, not generic adjectives like \"good\" or \"nice.\"", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:48.671005+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:48.671005+00'),
('ea16da5f-de86-430a-8dba-62ecd248f7b5', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'What is Brand Positioning? Why is it important?', 'Priority: Tier 1', 'Brand positioning is the act of designing the company''s offering and image so that it occupies a distinct, valued place in the target consumer''s mind relative to competing brands. Importance:

Creates a clear point of differentiation in a cluttered market.
Guides all downstream creative, message and media decisions.
Builds long-term brand equity by consistently reinforcing one idea.
Helps consumers quickly categorise and recall the brand.

Exam Tip: Positioning is about "a place in the mind," not the physical product — always anchor the definition to consumer perception.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 10, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ef0d1cf3-b12e-43ce-96f4-c320b8780da5', 'ac96dcd7-a8ee-488b-a4e5-5bfad3645838', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u2-c10-q-1", "text": "What is Brand Positioning? Why is it important?", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u2-c10-a-1", "text": "Brand positioning is the act of designing the company''s offering and image so that it occupies a distinct, valued place in the target consumer''s mind relative to competing brands. Importance:", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u2-c10-a-2", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Creates a clear point of differentiation in a cluttered market.", "Guides all downstream creative, message and media decisions.", "Builds long-term brand equity by consistently reinforcing one idea.", "Helps consumers quickly categorise and recall the brand."]}, {"id": "adv-u2-c10-a-3", "text": "Exam Tip: Positioning is about \"a place in the mind,\" not the physical product — always anchor the definition to consumer perception.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:48.671005+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:48.671005+00'),
('81664fc9-9e74-491f-bbf8-600968f6a634', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'Explain any four brand positioning strategies with relevant advertisement examples. (Direct PYQ — Oct 2024)', 'Priority: Tier 1', 'Positioning by Product Attribute/Benefit — highlighting a specific feature or benefit. Example: Colgate positioned on "cavity protection."
Positioning by Price/Quality — associating the brand with premium quality or value pricing. Example: Titan positioned as affordable elegance versus premium Swiss watches.
Positioning by Use/Application — linking the brand to a specific usage occasion. Example: Glucon-D positioned for "instant energy" during summer/heat.
Positioning by User/Target Segment — associating the brand with a particular user group. Example: Johnson''s Baby positioned for new mothers and infants.
(Additional, if needed) Positioning against Competitor — defining the brand relative to a rival. Example: Pepsi positioned as the youthful alternative to Coca-Cola.

Exam Tip: The question asks for "any four" — pick the four you can support with a clean, well-known example; do not attempt all five under time pressure.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 11, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ef0d1cf3-b12e-43ce-96f4-c320b8780da5', 'ac96dcd7-a8ee-488b-a4e5-5bfad3645838', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u2-c11-q-1", "text": "Explain any four brand positioning strategies with relevant advertisement examples. (Direct PYQ — Oct 2024)", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u2-c11-a-1", "type": "numberList", "items": ["Positioning by Product Attribute/Benefit — highlighting a specific feature or benefit. Example: Colgate positioned on \"cavity protection.\"", "Positioning by Price/Quality — associating the brand with premium quality or value pricing. Example: Titan positioned as affordable elegance versus premium Swiss watches.", "Positioning by Use/Application — linking the brand to a specific usage occasion. Example: Glucon-D positioned for \"instant energy\" during summer/heat.", "Positioning by User/Target Segment — associating the brand with a particular user group. Example: Johnson''s Baby positioned for new mothers and infants.", "(Additional, if needed) Positioning against Competitor — defining the brand relative to a rival. Example: Pepsi positioned as the youthful alternative to Coca-Cola."]}, {"id": "adv-u2-c11-a-2", "text": "Exam Tip: The question asks for \"any four\" — pick the four you can support with a clean, well-known example; do not attempt all five under time pressure.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:48.671005+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:48.671005+00'),
('2ef6af8a-42b3-442c-9458-a18a5eb2523e', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'What is Brand Equity? Why does it matter to advertisers?', 'Priority: Tier 2', 'Brand equity is the added value a brand name gives to a product beyond its functional benefits — reflected in consumer awareness, perceived quality, loyalty and associations built up over time. Why it matters: Strong brand equity allows premium pricing, easier line extensions, resilience during competitive attacks, and greater trade leverage. Advertising is one of the primary tools used to build brand equity over time.

Exam Tip: Equity is the outcome/value, image is the perception, personality is the character — keep these three roles distinct when answering.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 12, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ef0d1cf3-b12e-43ce-96f4-c320b8780da5', 'ac96dcd7-a8ee-488b-a4e5-5bfad3645838', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u2-c12-q-1", "text": "What is Brand Equity? Why does it matter to advertisers?", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u2-c12-a-1", "text": "Brand equity is the added value a brand name gives to a product beyond its functional benefits — reflected in consumer awareness, perceived quality, loyalty and associations built up over time. Why it matters: Strong brand equity allows premium pricing, easier line extensions, resilience during competitive attacks, and greater trade leverage. Advertising is one of the primary tools used to build brand equity over time.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u2-c12-a-2", "text": "Exam Tip: Equity is the outcome/value, image is the perception, personality is the character — keep these three roles distinct when answering.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:48.671005+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:48.671005+00'),
('a5fbaa36-4c12-49d3-a4ea-172577fdc9a5', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'What is Brand Image?', 'Priority: Tier 2', 'Brand image is the current set of beliefs, associations and perceptions consumers hold about a brand, formed through advertising, usage experience, word-of-mouth and packaging. It answers "what do consumers currently think this brand is?" It can be positive, negative, weak or strong, and can change over time with communication efforts. Exam Tip: Distinguish image (perception as it exists today) from positioning (the deliberate, desired place the company wants to occupy).', NULL, NULL, NULL, 13, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ef0d1cf3-b12e-43ce-96f4-c320b8780da5', 'ac96dcd7-a8ee-488b-a4e5-5bfad3645838', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u2-c13-q-1", "text": "What is Brand Image?", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u2-c13-a-1", "text": "Brand image is the current set of beliefs, associations and perceptions consumers hold about a brand, formed through advertising, usage experience, word-of-mouth and packaging. It answers \"what do consumers currently think this brand is?\" It can be positive, negative, weak or strong, and can change over time with communication efforts. Exam Tip: Distinguish image (perception as it exists today) from positioning (the deliberate, desired place the company wants to occupy).", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:48.671005+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:48.671005+00'),
('4a7bb744-bb32-463d-be6c-2b85e62a0c3f', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'Differentiate between Brand Equity, Brand Image and Brand Personality.', 'Priority: Tier 2', 'Basis | Brand Equity | Brand Image | Brand Personality
Meaning | Added value/worth of the brand name | Current perception/associations in consumer''s mind | Human traits attributed to the brand
Nature | Financial + perceptual asset | Perceptual | Symbolic/character-based
Built through | Long-term consistent performance + communication | Advertising, usage experience, word-of-mouth | Tone, spokespeople, visual and verbal style
Key question answered | "How much value does this brand add?" | "What do people think of this brand?" | "If this brand were a person, who would it be?"
Example | Apple commands premium pricing due to high equity | Volvo''s image = safety | Fastrack''s personality = youthful, cheeky

Exam Tip: This exact table format is the safest way to answer any "differentiate" question — examiners reward clear column-wise contrast over descriptive paragraphs.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 15, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ef0d1cf3-b12e-43ce-96f4-c320b8780da5', 'ac96dcd7-a8ee-488b-a4e5-5bfad3645838', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u2-c15-q-1", "text": "Differentiate between Brand Equity, Brand Image and Brand Personality.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u2-c15-a-1", "rows": [["Basis", "Brand Equity", "Brand Image", "Brand Personality"], ["Meaning", "Added value/worth of the brand name", "Current perception/associations in consumer''s mind", "Human traits attributed to the brand"], ["Nature", "Financial + perceptual asset", "Perceptual", "Symbolic/character-based"], ["Built through", "Long-term consistent performance + communication", "Advertising, usage experience, word-of-mouth", "Tone, spokespeople, visual and verbal style"], ["Key question answered", "\"How much value does this brand add?\"", "\"What do people think of this brand?\"", "\"If this brand were a person, who would it be?\""], ["Example", "Apple commands premium pricing due to high equity", "Volvo''s image = safety", "Fastrack''s personality = youthful, cheeky"]], "type": "table"}, {"id": "adv-u2-c15-a-2", "text": "Exam Tip: This exact table format is the safest way to answer any \"differentiate\" question — examiners reward clear column-wise contrast over descriptive paragraphs.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:48.671005+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:48.671005+00'),
('2677329a-8495-44d3-80d8-3388482a126e', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'Explain brand positioning with reference to how it is executed in real advertisements.', 'Priority: Tier 1 (Application)', 'Positioning is executed through consistent repetition of one core idea across every ad execution of a brand — the same benefit, tone and visual cues repeated over time until it becomes the brand''s mental "shortcut" for consumers. Example: Dettol''s advertisements across decades consistently reinforce "protection from germs," regardless of the specific product (soap, handwash, sanitizer) — this consistency is what builds and sustains its positioning. Exam Tip: Whenever an example is demanded, choose a brand whose positioning has stayed consistent for years — it is easier to demonstrate depth with a familiar, stable example.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 16, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ef0d1cf3-b12e-43ce-96f4-c320b8780da5', 'ac96dcd7-a8ee-488b-a4e5-5bfad3645838', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u2-c16-q-1", "text": "Explain brand positioning with reference to how it is executed in real advertisements.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u2-c16-a-1", "text": "Positioning is executed through consistent repetition of one core idea across every ad execution of a brand — the same benefit, tone and visual cues repeated over time until it becomes the brand''s mental \"shortcut\" for consumers. Example: Dettol''s advertisements across decades consistently reinforce \"protection from germs,\" regardless of the specific product (soap, handwash, sanitizer) — this consistency is what builds and sustains its positioning. Exam Tip: Whenever an example is demanded, choose a brand whose positioning has stayed consistent for years — it is easier to demonstrate depth with a familiar, stable example.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:48.671005+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:48.671005+00'),
('00e00ca6-f931-4b8c-9658-f405fc295c95', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'What is Creative Strategy in advertising?', 'Priority: Tier 3', 'Creative strategy is the plan that translates the brand''s positioning into a central creative idea/theme that will run through the advertising campaign — deciding what the campaign will say and how it will be dramatised (storyline, characters, tone, visual style) to make the positioning memorable and persuasive. It sits between "what the brand wants to say" (positioning) and "what actually appears in the ad" (message/copy). Exam Tip: Creative strategy = the big idea/theme; message strategy = the specific content/appeal within that idea — keep the sequence clear if asked to relate the two.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 17, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ef0d1cf3-b12e-43ce-96f4-c320b8780da5', 'fcd68157-ab63-4d53-b5ea-c391ba9fc99a', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u2-c17-q-1", "text": "What is Creative Strategy in advertising?", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u2-c17-a-1", "text": "Creative strategy is the plan that translates the brand''s positioning into a central creative idea/theme that will run through the advertising campaign — deciding what the campaign will say and how it will be dramatised (storyline, characters, tone, visual style) to make the positioning memorable and persuasive. It sits between \"what the brand wants to say\" (positioning) and \"what actually appears in the ad\" (message/copy). Exam Tip: Creative strategy = the big idea/theme; message strategy = the specific content/appeal within that idea — keep the sequence clear if asked to relate the two.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:48.671005+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:48.671005+00'),
('eafae86a-2432-4440-938e-48cac5e7b44a', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'What is Message Strategy? What are its key elements?', 'Priority: Tier 3', 'Message strategy decides exactly what will be communicated to the target audience to support the positioning — including the appeal used (rational, emotional, humour), the tone, and the copy platform (headline, body copy, tagline). Key elements:

Appeal — rational (facts/features), emotional (feelings), humour, fear, or a blend.
Tone — serious, playful, aspirational, informative.
Copy structure — headline, sub-headline, body copy, slogan/tagline, visual.

Exam Tip: If a scenario asks you to "identify appeals" for a product, always justify why that appeal fits the target audience and product category, not just name it.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 18, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ef0d1cf3-b12e-43ce-96f4-c320b8780da5', 'fcd68157-ab63-4d53-b5ea-c391ba9fc99a', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u2-c18-q-1", "text": "What is Message Strategy? What are its key elements?", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u2-c18-a-1", "text": "Message strategy decides exactly what will be communicated to the target audience to support the positioning — including the appeal used (rational, emotional, humour), the tone, and the copy platform (headline, body copy, tagline). Key elements:", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u2-c18-a-2", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Appeal — rational (facts/features), emotional (feelings), humour, fear, or a blend.", "Tone — serious, playful, aspirational, informative.", "Copy structure — headline, sub-headline, body copy, slogan/tagline, visual."]}, {"id": "adv-u2-c18-a-3", "text": "Exam Tip: If a scenario asks you to \"identify appeals\" for a product, always justify why that appeal fits the target audience and product category, not just name it.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:48.671005+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:48.671005+00'),
('af27af51-4c83-430c-8f81-5249c8ee0e80', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'What is Media Strategy in advertising planning?', 'Priority: Tier 3', 'Media strategy decides how the message will be delivered to the target audience — which media vehicles (print, TV, digital, OOH, radio), what reach and frequency levels, what geographic scope, and what scheduling pattern (continuous, flighting, pulsing) will be used, within the available budget. It must match the media habits of the defined target audience for the plan to be efficient. Exam Tip: Mention reach, frequency and scheduling by name — these three terms are the standard vocabulary examiners look for in a media-strategy answer.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 19, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ef0d1cf3-b12e-43ce-96f4-c320b8780da5', 'fcd68157-ab63-4d53-b5ea-c391ba9fc99a', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u2-c19-q-1", "text": "What is Media Strategy in advertising planning?", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u2-c19-a-1", "text": "Media strategy decides how the message will be delivered to the target audience — which media vehicles (print, TV, digital, OOH, radio), what reach and frequency levels, what geographic scope, and what scheduling pattern (continuous, flighting, pulsing) will be used, within the available budget. It must match the media habits of the defined target audience for the plan to be efficient. Exam Tip: Mention reach, frequency and scheduling by name — these three terms are the standard vocabulary examiners look for in a media-strategy answer.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:48.671005+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:48.671005+00'),
('4dec9c3e-9e0e-4ab9-b129-78daa9e60642', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'Discuss the role of advertising in the marketing mix. (Direct PYQ — Oct 2024, 10 marks)', 'Priority: Tier 1', 'Advertising is one element of the Promotion mix but interacts with and supports every other element of the marketing mix (Product, Price, Place, Promotion):

It communicates product features/benefits and supports branding decisions.
It justifies and communicates pricing (value or premium positioning).
It builds demand that supports distribution — retailers stock brands with strong ad-driven pull.
Within promotion, it works alongside sales promotion, PR and personal selling to build long-term brand value (while sales promotion drives short-term action).

Overall, advertising''s marketing-mix role is to create awareness, communicate value and pull demand that makes the rest of the mix effective.

Exam Tip: Structure this answer element-by-element (Product / Price / Place / Promotion) with a sub-heading each — this is the exact structure examiners expect for a 10-mark "role in marketing mix" question.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 21, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ef0d1cf3-b12e-43ce-96f4-c320b8780da5', '225d405c-e3ff-4ea5-9418-af368fa5f348', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u2-c21-q-1", "text": "Discuss the role of advertising in the marketing mix. (Direct PYQ — Oct 2024, 10 marks)", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u2-c21-a-1", "text": "Advertising is one element of the Promotion mix but interacts with and supports every other element of the marketing mix (Product, Price, Place, Promotion):", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u2-c21-a-2", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["It communicates product features/benefits and supports branding decisions.", "It justifies and communicates pricing (value or premium positioning).", "It builds demand that supports distribution — retailers stock brands with strong ad-driven pull.", "Within promotion, it works alongside sales promotion, PR and personal selling to build long-term brand value (while sales promotion drives short-term action)."]}, {"id": "adv-u2-c21-a-3", "text": "Overall, advertising''s marketing-mix role is to create awareness, communicate value and pull demand that makes the rest of the mix effective.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u2-c21-a-4", "text": "Exam Tip: Structure this answer element-by-element (Product / Price / Place / Promotion) with a sub-heading each — this is the exact structure examiners expect for a 10-mark \"role in marketing mix\" question.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:48.671005+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:48.671005+00'),
('b0ef7e9c-b08a-4800-af99-cbec3b56296d', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'How does advertising support Product Planning and Brand Policy decisions?', 'Priority: Tier 2', 'Advertising communicates the product''s features, quality and USP to build awareness and desired image.
It supports brand policy decisions (individual branding, family branding, brand extension) by consistently communicating whichever brand architecture the company has chosen — e.g., reinforcing a shared brand image across a family-branded product line, or building a distinct identity for an individually branded product.
It helps differentiate the product from competitors at the perception level, even when physical differences are small.

Exam Tip: Always connect advertising to a decision (branding strategy chosen) rather than just describing the product — "role in marketing mix" answers must show contribution, not just correlation.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 22, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ef0d1cf3-b12e-43ce-96f4-c320b8780da5', '225d405c-e3ff-4ea5-9418-af368fa5f348', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u2-c22-q-1", "text": "How does advertising support Product Planning and Brand Policy decisions?", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u2-c22-a-1", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Advertising communicates the product''s features, quality and USP to build awareness and desired image.", "It supports brand policy decisions (individual branding, family branding, brand extension) by consistently communicating whichever brand architecture the company has chosen — e.g., reinforcing a shared brand image across a family-branded product line, or building a distinct identity for an individually branded product.", "It helps differentiate the product from competitors at the perception level, even when physical differences are small."]}, {"id": "adv-u2-c22-a-2", "text": "Exam Tip: Always connect advertising to a decision (branding strategy chosen) rather than just describing the product — \"role in marketing mix\" answers must show contribution, not just correlation.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:48.671005+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:48.671005+00'),
('b2270587-8065-430b-bc03-9528f6d805ae', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'How does advertising support Pricing decisions?', 'Priority: Tier 2', 'Advertising helps justify the price point set by the company — communicating value, quality or exclusivity so that consumers perceive the price as fair or as a mark of premium quality. Premium-priced brands rely heavily on advertising to build the perceived value that supports the higher price; value/economy brands use advertising to signal affordability and practicality. Advertising thus makes the chosen pricing strategy credible to the target audience. Exam Tip: Give one premium and one value-brand example to show both directions of this relationship.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 23, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ef0d1cf3-b12e-43ce-96f4-c320b8780da5', '225d405c-e3ff-4ea5-9418-af368fa5f348', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u2-c23-q-1", "text": "How does advertising support Pricing decisions?", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u2-c23-a-1", "text": "Advertising helps justify the price point set by the company — communicating value, quality or exclusivity so that consumers perceive the price as fair or as a mark of premium quality. Premium-priced brands rely heavily on advertising to build the perceived value that supports the higher price; value/economy brands use advertising to signal affordability and practicality. Advertising thus makes the chosen pricing strategy credible to the target audience. Exam Tip: Give one premium and one value-brand example to show both directions of this relationship.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:48.671005+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:48.671005+00'),
('ebbfd9d4-2bbf-46e7-ae86-92ae6beb873c', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'What role does advertising play with respect to Packaging?', 'Priority: Tier 2', 'Packaging itself acts as "the last advertisement" seen before purchase (especially at the point of sale), so advertising and packaging must be visually and thematically consistent — same colours, logo treatment and tagline — so consumers can instantly recognise the advertised brand on the shelf. Advertising often highlights specific packaging innovations (e.g., resealable packs, eco-friendly packaging) as part of the brand''s message. Exam Tip: Use the phrase "packaging as silent salesman" — it is standard terminology, not fabricated, and fits well here.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 24, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ef0d1cf3-b12e-43ce-96f4-c320b8780da5', '225d405c-e3ff-4ea5-9418-af368fa5f348', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u2-c24-q-1", "text": "What role does advertising play with respect to Packaging?", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u2-c24-a-1", "text": "Packaging itself acts as \"the last advertisement\" seen before purchase (especially at the point of sale), so advertising and packaging must be visually and thematically consistent — same colours, logo treatment and tagline — so consumers can instantly recognise the advertised brand on the shelf. Advertising often highlights specific packaging innovations (e.g., resealable packs, eco-friendly packaging) as part of the brand''s message. Exam Tip: Use the phrase \"packaging as silent salesman\" — it is standard terminology, not fabricated, and fits well here.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:48.671005+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:48.671005+00'),
('1971da24-1380-43d9-910f-9999429d8ed3', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'How does advertising support Distribution?', 'Priority: Tier 2', 'Effective advertising creates consumer pull — demand generated among end consumers that motivates retailers and distributors to stock the brand (pull strategy), reducing the company''s need to rely purely on trade incentives (push strategy). Strong advertising-driven brand demand also gives the company greater bargaining power/leverage with channel partners for shelf space and better placement. Exam Tip: Explicitly name "push" and "pull" strategy — this pair of terms is commonly expected in this answer.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 25, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ef0d1cf3-b12e-43ce-96f4-c320b8780da5', '225d405c-e3ff-4ea5-9418-af368fa5f348', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u2-c25-q-1", "text": "How does advertising support Distribution?", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u2-c25-a-1", "text": "Effective advertising creates consumer pull — demand generated among end consumers that motivates retailers and distributors to stock the brand (pull strategy), reducing the company''s need to rely purely on trade incentives (push strategy). Strong advertising-driven brand demand also gives the company greater bargaining power/leverage with channel partners for shelf space and better placement. Exam Tip: Explicitly name \"push\" and \"pull\" strategy — this pair of terms is commonly expected in this answer.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:48.671005+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:48.671005+00'),
('db3260d3-44ed-47c0-9e60-124bc99e85cf', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'What are the elements of Promotion, and where does advertising fit among them?', 'Priority: Tier 2', 'Elements of the promotion mix:

Advertising — paid, non-personal, mass communication.
Sales Promotion — short-term incentives (discounts, contests, sampling).
Personal Selling — direct, person-to-person persuasion.
Public Relations/Publicity — building goodwill, often unpaid/earned media.
Direct Marketing — direct communication to individual consumers (mailers, email, telemarketing).

Advertising''s distinct role is building long-term brand awareness and image, whereas sales promotion drives immediate action, personal selling handles individual persuasion, and PR builds credibility/trust.

Exam Tip: If asked to "elucidate elements of promotion," always close by explicitly stating advertising''s distinguishing role among them — this comparison earns extra marks.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 26, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ef0d1cf3-b12e-43ce-96f4-c320b8780da5', '225d405c-e3ff-4ea5-9418-af368fa5f348', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u2-c26-q-1", "text": "What are the elements of Promotion, and where does advertising fit among them?", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u2-c26-a-1", "text": "Elements of the promotion mix:", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u2-c26-a-2", "type": "numberList", "items": ["Advertising — paid, non-personal, mass communication.", "Sales Promotion — short-term incentives (discounts, contests, sampling).", "Personal Selling — direct, person-to-person persuasion.", "Public Relations/Publicity — building goodwill, often unpaid/earned media.", "Direct Marketing — direct communication to individual consumers (mailers, email, telemarketing)."]}, {"id": "adv-u2-c26-a-3", "text": "Advertising''s distinct role is building long-term brand awareness and image, whereas sales promotion drives immediate action, personal selling handles individual persuasion, and PR builds credibility/trust.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u2-c26-a-4", "text": "Exam Tip: If asked to \"elucidate elements of promotion,\" always close by explicitly stating advertising''s distinguishing role among them — this comparison earns extra marks.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:48.671005+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:48.671005+00'),
('2a677b66-837b-492c-bf8a-f8c379861990', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'What is the Product Life Cycle (PLC)? What are its stages?', 'Priority: Tier 1', 'The PLC describes the stages a product passes through in the market, from launch to withdrawal, based on sales and profit trends over time. Stages:

Introduction — product launched, low sales, high costs, low/no profit.
Growth — sales rise rapidly, competitors enter, profits increase.
Maturity — sales peak and stabilise, intense competition, profits may decline due to price competition.
Decline — sales and profits fall as the product becomes outdated or is replaced.

Exam Tip: This exact four-stage list is the backbone of every PLC answer — memorise it cold before attempting the diagram or strategy questions.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 27, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ef0d1cf3-b12e-43ce-96f4-c320b8780da5', 'b6bf7eed-a3a2-4c9a-bf48-8945bf7938ce', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u2-c27-q-1", "text": "What is the Product Life Cycle (PLC)? What are its stages?", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u2-c27-a-1", "text": "The PLC describes the stages a product passes through in the market, from launch to withdrawal, based on sales and profit trends over time. Stages:", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u2-c27-a-2", "type": "numberList", "items": ["Introduction — product launched, low sales, high costs, low/no profit.", "Growth — sales rise rapidly, competitors enter, profits increase.", "Maturity — sales peak and stabilise, intense competition, profits may decline due to price competition.", "Decline — sales and profits fall as the product becomes outdated or is replaced."]}, {"id": "adv-u2-c27-a-3", "text": "Exam Tip: This exact four-stage list is the backbone of every PLC answer — memorise it cold before attempting the diagram or strategy questions.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:48.671005+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:48.671005+00'),
('be3b0edc-5bf9-4ee1-ba7e-ac37323f2460', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'Elucidate the role of advertising in the PLC. Explain what strategies are adopted at each stage with a neat labelled diagram. (Direct PYQ — appeared in BOTH Oct 2024 and 2025 papers)', 'Priority: Tier 1', 'PLC Stage | Advertising Objective | Advertising Strategy
Introduction | Build awareness and encourage trial | Informative advertising explaining what the product is and its benefits; heavy initial spend
Growth | Build preference over competitors, encourage repeat purchase | Persuasive advertising highlighting brand differentiation as competitors enter
Maturity | Retain loyal customers, find new uses/users, defend market share | Reminder + comparative advertising; may include repositioning or promotional offers
Decline | Manage a cost-effective exit or milk the remaining loyal base | Minimal advertising, mainly reminder-level, spend sharply reduced

Diagram (exam-drawable):  Sales/Profit


/            \
/   MATURITY   \
/                _
/  GROWTH              \  DECLINE
/                         \
INTRODUCTION
/ Time

Label the x-axis as Time and y-axis as Sales/Profit, with the curve rising through Introduction → Growth → Maturity (peak/plateau) → Decline.

Exam Tip: This is the single most PYQ-tested theme in the supplied evidence — practise drawing the curve from memory and pair each stage instantly with its advertising objective + strategy, in table form, for a 10-mark answer.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 28, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ef0d1cf3-b12e-43ce-96f4-c320b8780da5', 'b6bf7eed-a3a2-4c9a-bf48-8945bf7938ce', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u2-c28-q-1", "text": "Elucidate the role of advertising in the PLC. Explain what strategies are adopted at each stage with a neat labelled diagram. (Direct PYQ — appeared in BOTH Oct 2024 and 2025 papers)", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u2-c28-a-1", "rows": [["PLC Stage", "Advertising Objective", "Advertising Strategy"], ["Introduction", "Build awareness and encourage trial", "Informative advertising explaining what the product is and its benefits; heavy initial spend"], ["Growth", "Build preference over competitors, encourage repeat purchase", "Persuasive advertising highlighting brand differentiation as competitors enter"], ["Maturity", "Retain loyal customers, find new uses/users, defend market share", "Reminder + comparative advertising; may include repositioning or promotional offers"], ["Decline", "Manage a cost-effective exit or milk the remaining loyal base", "Minimal advertising, mainly reminder-level, spend sharply reduced"]], "type": "table"}, {"id": "adv-u2-c28-a-2", "text": "Diagram (exam-drawable):  Sales/Profit", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u2-c28-a-3", "rows": [[""], ["/            \\"], ["/   MATURITY   \\"], ["/                _"], ["/  GROWTH              \\  DECLINE"], ["/                         \\"], ["INTRODUCTION"], ["/ Time"]], "type": "table"}, {"id": "adv-u2-c28-a-4", "text": "Label the x-axis as Time and y-axis as Sales/Profit, with the curve rising through Introduction → Growth → Maturity (peak/plateau) → Decline.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u2-c28-a-5", "text": "Exam Tip: This is the single most PYQ-tested theme in the supplied evidence — practise drawing the curve from memory and pair each stage instantly with its advertising objective + strategy, in table form, for a 10-mark answer.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:48.671005+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:48.671005+00'),
('67efdf0b-a51a-48de-8d1e-9e747144c9b8', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'Analyse the role of PLC in the advertising planning process, based on objectives, process and budget. (Direct PYQ — 2025 Q2b, 7.5 marks)', 'Priority: Tier 1', 'PLC stage directly shapes three planning inputs:

Objectives: Introduction → awareness/trial; Growth → preference/loyalty; Maturity → retention/repositioning; Decline → minimal reminder or phased exit.
Process: The situational analysis stage of planning must identify the product''s current PLC stage before objectives and creative/media strategy are finalised, since the "right" strategy differs completely by stage.
Budget: Advertising budgets are typically highest during Introduction and Growth (to build awareness and fight for share) and progressively reduced through Maturity and Decline (as the objective shifts from building demand to merely sustaining it).

Exam Tip: Structure the answer under the three headings the question itself gives you — Objectives, Process, Budget — examiners are explicitly signalling the expected structure.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 29, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ef0d1cf3-b12e-43ce-96f4-c320b8780da5', 'b6bf7eed-a3a2-4c9a-bf48-8945bf7938ce', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u2-c29-q-1", "text": "Analyse the role of PLC in the advertising planning process, based on objectives, process and budget. (Direct PYQ — 2025 Q2b, 7.5 marks)", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u2-c29-a-1", "text": "PLC stage directly shapes three planning inputs:", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u2-c29-a-2", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Objectives: Introduction → awareness/trial; Growth → preference/loyalty; Maturity → retention/repositioning; Decline → minimal reminder or phased exit.", "Process: The situational analysis stage of planning must identify the product''s current PLC stage before objectives and creative/media strategy are finalised, since the \"right\" strategy differs completely by stage.", "Budget: Advertising budgets are typically highest during Introduction and Growth (to build awareness and fight for share) and progressively reduced through Maturity and Decline (as the objective shifts from building demand to merely sustaining it)."]}, {"id": "adv-u2-c29-a-3", "text": "Exam Tip: Structure the answer under the three headings the question itself gives you — Objectives, Process, Budget — examiners are explicitly signalling the expected structure.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:48.671005+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:48.671005+00'),
('5a319f9b-a602-4280-b72f-a1bb482402ad', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'An established brand is facing declining sales. How can PLC analysis influence advertising strategy?', 'Priority: Tier 1 (Application)', 'First, confirm via situational analysis that the brand is genuinely in the Decline stage (falling sales/profits, reduced competitor activity) rather than facing a temporary dip.
If true decline: shift advertising objective from growth-building to cost-efficient reminder advertising, sharply cutting spend, or consider phased withdrawal.
Alternative strategic response: use advertising to support a repositioning or relaunch (new use, new target segment, or product modification) to push the brand back toward a renewed growth phase rather than accepting decline.
Budget should be reallocated toward better-performing products/stages rather than sustained heavy spend on a declining item, unless a relaunch is planned.

Exam Tip: Application questions on PLC reward showing two paths (accept decline vs. attempt repositioning) rather than only one — it demonstrates strategic judgement.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 30, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ef0d1cf3-b12e-43ce-96f4-c320b8780da5', 'b6bf7eed-a3a2-4c9a-bf48-8945bf7938ce', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u2-c30-q-1", "text": "An established brand is facing declining sales. How can PLC analysis influence advertising strategy?", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u2-c30-a-1", "type": "numberList", "items": ["First, confirm via situational analysis that the brand is genuinely in the Decline stage (falling sales/profits, reduced competitor activity) rather than facing a temporary dip.", "If true decline: shift advertising objective from growth-building to cost-efficient reminder advertising, sharply cutting spend, or consider phased withdrawal.", "Alternative strategic response: use advertising to support a repositioning or relaunch (new use, new target segment, or product modification) to push the brand back toward a renewed growth phase rather than accepting decline.", "Budget should be reallocated toward better-performing products/stages rather than sustained heavy spend on a declining item, unless a relaunch is planned."]}, {"id": "adv-u2-c30-a-2", "text": "Exam Tip: Application questions on PLC reward showing two paths (accept decline vs. attempt repositioning) rather than only one — it demonstrates strategic judgement.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:48.671005+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:48.671005+00'),
('47397075-7b64-41fe-9fdb-7664d1da4058', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'What are the main functions of an advertising agency?', 'Priority: Tier 2', 'Account Management — liaising with the client, understanding briefs, coordinating internal teams.
Creative Services — developing the campaign idea, copywriting, art/visual design.
Media Planning & Buying — selecting media vehicles, negotiating rates, scheduling.
Research — consumer research, pre-testing and post-testing of advertisements.
Production — producing the final ad (print, film, digital assets).
Administrative/Financial functions — billing, traffic management, coordinating timelines.

Exam Tip: Group functions under 4–5 clear heads rather than listing many overlapping small tasks — examiners look for structured categories.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 31, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ef0d1cf3-b12e-43ce-96f4-c320b8780da5', '7602e97c-08d3-4781-b333-71223c28ecf6', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u2-c31-q-1", "text": "What are the main functions of an advertising agency?", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u2-c31-a-1", "type": "numberList", "items": ["Account Management — liaising with the client, understanding briefs, coordinating internal teams.", "Creative Services — developing the campaign idea, copywriting, art/visual design.", "Media Planning & Buying — selecting media vehicles, negotiating rates, scheduling.", "Research — consumer research, pre-testing and post-testing of advertisements.", "Production — producing the final ad (print, film, digital assets).", "Administrative/Financial functions — billing, traffic management, coordinating timelines."]}, {"id": "adv-u2-c31-a-2", "text": "Exam Tip: Group functions under 4–5 clear heads rather than listing many overlapping small tasks — examiners look for structured categories.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:48.671005+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:48.671005+00'),
('e5c11fe7-1f6e-4c06-8def-f2bda157f5bf', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'Describe the typical structure of an advertising agency.', 'Priority: Tier 3', 'A full-service agency is typically structured into departments that mirror its core functions:

Account Management/Client Servicing Department — client relationship, briefs.
Creative Department — copywriters, art directors, visualisers.
Media Department — media planners and buyers.
Research Department — market and ad-effectiveness research.
Production Department — print/film/digital production.
Finance & Administration — billing and internal coordination.

These departments coordinate under overall agency management, with the account management team acting as the link between the client and all internal departments.

Exam Tip: If asked to draw a hierarchy, place "Agency Head/Management" at the top with the departments branching below it, and Client Servicing shown connecting outward to the client.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 32, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ef0d1cf3-b12e-43ce-96f4-c320b8780da5', '7602e97c-08d3-4781-b333-71223c28ecf6', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u2-c32-q-1", "text": "Describe the typical structure of an advertising agency.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u2-c32-a-1", "text": "A full-service agency is typically structured into departments that mirror its core functions:", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u2-c32-a-2", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Account Management/Client Servicing Department — client relationship, briefs.", "Creative Department — copywriters, art directors, visualisers.", "Media Department — media planners and buyers.", "Research Department — market and ad-effectiveness research.", "Production Department — print/film/digital production.", "Finance & Administration — billing and internal coordination."]}, {"id": "adv-u2-c32-a-3", "text": "These departments coordinate under overall agency management, with the account management team acting as the link between the client and all internal departments.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u2-c32-a-4", "text": "Exam Tip: If asked to draw a hierarchy, place \"Agency Head/Management\" at the top with the departments branching below it, and Client Servicing shown connecting outward to the client.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:48.671005+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:48.671005+00'),
('66d41d7c-5617-495a-98a6-dcee302891fa', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'What are the different types of advertising agencies?', 'Priority: Tier 3', 'Full-Service Agency — offers all services (creative, media, research, production) under one roof.
Specialised/Boutique Agency — focuses on one function, e.g., only creative, or only media buying.
In-House Agency — an advertising unit set up within the client company itself.
Digital/Interactive Agency — specialises in online and social media advertising.
Creative Hot-Shop — focuses purely on creative/conceptual work, often for specific campaigns.

Exam Tip: Full-service vs. in-house is the most commonly expected contrast — be ready to state one advantage and one limitation of each if the question asks for discussion, not just listing.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 33, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ef0d1cf3-b12e-43ce-96f4-c320b8780da5', '7602e97c-08d3-4781-b333-71223c28ecf6', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u2-c33-q-1", "text": "What are the different types of advertising agencies?", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u2-c33-a-1", "type": "numberList", "items": ["Full-Service Agency — offers all services (creative, media, research, production) under one roof.", "Specialised/Boutique Agency — focuses on one function, e.g., only creative, or only media buying.", "In-House Agency — an advertising unit set up within the client company itself.", "Digital/Interactive Agency — specialises in online and social media advertising.", "Creative Hot-Shop — focuses purely on creative/conceptual work, often for specific campaigns."]}, {"id": "adv-u2-c33-a-2", "text": "Exam Tip: Full-service vs. in-house is the most commonly expected contrast — be ready to state one advantage and one limitation of each if the question asks for discussion, not just listing.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:48.671005+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:48.671005+00'),
('e2910f2c-3c05-4f78-a29e-40c1e69455ae', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'What criteria should a company consider while selecting an advertising agency? (Direct PYQ — 2025 Q2c, 7.5 marks)', 'Priority: Tier 1', 'Agency''s track record and reputation — past campaigns, awards, client retention.
Range of services offered — whether it matches the company''s specific needs (full-service vs. specialised).
Creative capability — quality and originality of past creative work.
Media strength — media planning/buying relationships and negotiating power.
Industry experience — familiarity with the client''s product category, avoiding direct competitor conflicts.
Size and resources — whether agency scale matches the client''s budget and campaign scope.
Cost/compensation structure — transparency and affordability of fees/commission.
Personal chemistry/compatibility — working relationship fit between client and agency teams.

Exam Tip: Number the criteria (at least 6–8) — this question rewards breadth; a short 3–4 point answer will underperform relative to its 7.5-mark weight.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 34, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ef0d1cf3-b12e-43ce-96f4-c320b8780da5', '7602e97c-08d3-4781-b333-71223c28ecf6', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u2-c34-q-1", "text": "What criteria should a company consider while selecting an advertising agency? (Direct PYQ — 2025 Q2c, 7.5 marks)", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u2-c34-a-1", "type": "numberList", "items": ["Agency''s track record and reputation — past campaigns, awards, client retention.", "Range of services offered — whether it matches the company''s specific needs (full-service vs. specialised).", "Creative capability — quality and originality of past creative work.", "Media strength — media planning/buying relationships and negotiating power.", "Industry experience — familiarity with the client''s product category, avoiding direct competitor conflicts.", "Size and resources — whether agency scale matches the client''s budget and campaign scope.", "Cost/compensation structure — transparency and affordability of fees/commission.", "Personal chemistry/compatibility — working relationship fit between client and agency teams."]}, {"id": "adv-u2-c34-a-2", "text": "Exam Tip: Number the criteria (at least 6–8) — this question rewards breadth; a short 3–4 point answer will underperform relative to its 7.5-mark weight.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:48.671005+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:48.671005+00'),
('ac393899-bb0b-41d1-9f72-8ecf1d231b9b', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'A company needs to appoint an advertising agency. What criteria should it consider?', 'Priority: Tier 1 (Application)', 'Apply the selection criteria (Flashcard 34) to a scenario:

Shortlist agencies with proven experience in the company''s product category.
Evaluate creative pitches from 2–3 agencies for originality and fit with brand positioning.
Assess media buying strength, especially if the campaign needs broad reach on a limited budget.
Check for conflict of interest — avoid agencies already serving a direct competitor.
Compare compensation structures (commission vs. fee-based) against the company''s budget.
Finally, weigh compatibility — since agency-client relationships run over years, working chemistry matters as much as capability.

Exam Tip: When a question is framed as "a company needs to appoint an agency," always end with a one-line decision/recommendation rather than stopping at the list of criteria.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 35, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ef0d1cf3-b12e-43ce-96f4-c320b8780da5', '7602e97c-08d3-4781-b333-71223c28ecf6', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u2-c35-q-1", "text": "A company needs to appoint an advertising agency. What criteria should it consider?", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u2-c35-a-1", "text": "Apply the selection criteria (Flashcard 34) to a scenario:", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u2-c35-a-2", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Shortlist agencies with proven experience in the company''s product category.", "Evaluate creative pitches from 2–3 agencies for originality and fit with brand positioning.", "Assess media buying strength, especially if the campaign needs broad reach on a limited budget.", "Check for conflict of interest — avoid agencies already serving a direct competitor.", "Compare compensation structures (commission vs. fee-based) against the company''s budget.", "Finally, weigh compatibility — since agency-client relationships run over years, working chemistry matters as much as capability."]}, {"id": "adv-u2-c35-a-3", "text": "Exam Tip: When a question is framed as \"a company needs to appoint an agency,\" always end with a one-line decision/recommendation rather than stopping at the list of criteria.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:48.671005+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:48.671005+00'),
('c534f12a-6718-47b4-8504-2f3616f686ca', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'Write a note on the Client–Agency relationship. How can this relationship be mutually maintained? (Direct PYQ — appeared in BOTH Oct 2024 and 2025 papers)', 'Priority: Tier 1', 'The client-agency relationship is a partnership requiring trust, transparency and continuous communication for a campaign to succeed, since the agency depends on the client for accurate briefs and fair compensation, while the client depends on the agency for creative quality and strategic input. Ways to maintain the relationship:

Clear, honest briefing — client provides complete, accurate information upfront.
Regular communication — periodic review meetings, transparent progress updates.
Mutual respect for expertise — client trusts agency''s creative/strategic judgement; agency respects client''s business constraints.
Fair and timely compensation — reduces friction and builds agency commitment.
Constructive feedback — evaluating work objectively rather than reactively.
Long-term orientation — viewing the relationship as an ongoing partnership rather than a transactional, campaign-by-campaign engagement.
Conflict resolution mechanisms — agreed processes for handling creative disagreements.

Exam Tip: This exact theme has appeared in both supplied papers — treat it as a guaranteed-preparation topic; practise writing it to full 9–10 mark depth with both "relationship" and "how to maintain it" clearly separated.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 36, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ef0d1cf3-b12e-43ce-96f4-c320b8780da5', '7602e97c-08d3-4781-b333-71223c28ecf6', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u2-c36-q-1", "text": "Write a note on the Client–Agency relationship. How can this relationship be mutually maintained? (Direct PYQ — appeared in BOTH Oct 2024 and 2025 papers)", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u2-c36-a-1", "text": "The client-agency relationship is a partnership requiring trust, transparency and continuous communication for a campaign to succeed, since the agency depends on the client for accurate briefs and fair compensation, while the client depends on the agency for creative quality and strategic input. Ways to maintain the relationship:", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u2-c36-a-2", "type": "numberList", "items": ["Clear, honest briefing — client provides complete, accurate information upfront.", "Regular communication — periodic review meetings, transparent progress updates.", "Mutual respect for expertise — client trusts agency''s creative/strategic judgement; agency respects client''s business constraints.", "Fair and timely compensation — reduces friction and builds agency commitment.", "Constructive feedback — evaluating work objectively rather than reactively.", "Long-term orientation — viewing the relationship as an ongoing partnership rather than a transactional, campaign-by-campaign engagement.", "Conflict resolution mechanisms — agreed processes for handling creative disagreements."]}, {"id": "adv-u2-c36-a-3", "text": "Exam Tip: This exact theme has appeared in both supplied papers — treat it as a guaranteed-preparation topic; practise writing it to full 9–10 mark depth with both \"relationship\" and \"how to maintain it\" clearly separated.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:48.671005+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:48.671005+00'),
('9f424f4f-17d0-420e-b7b1-f7ae024884fc', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'How does maintaining a strong client–agency relationship impact the effectiveness of advertising campaigns? (Direct PYQ — 2025 Q2c)', 'Priority: Tier 1', 'Better creative output — trust allows the agency to take creative risks the client will support.
Faster execution — smoother approvals and fewer revision cycles when communication is clear.
Consistent brand voice — a long-term relationship builds deep brand understanding, improving message consistency over multiple campaigns.
Better use of budget — reduced friction and re-work saves cost and time.
Higher accountability — established trust allows honest post-campaign evaluation and learning for future campaigns.

Example: Long-standing agency-client pairings (e.g., a company retaining the same agency for years) often produce more consistent, recognisable brand campaigns than relationships with frequent agency churn.

Exam Tip: Directly answer the word "effectiveness" — tie every point back to campaign outcomes (creativity, speed, consistency, cost, learning), not just relationship harmony in the abstract.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 37, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ef0d1cf3-b12e-43ce-96f4-c320b8780da5', '7602e97c-08d3-4781-b333-71223c28ecf6', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u2-c37-q-1", "text": "How does maintaining a strong client–agency relationship impact the effectiveness of advertising campaigns? (Direct PYQ — 2025 Q2c)", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u2-c37-a-1", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Better creative output — trust allows the agency to take creative risks the client will support.", "Faster execution — smoother approvals and fewer revision cycles when communication is clear.", "Consistent brand voice — a long-term relationship builds deep brand understanding, improving message consistency over multiple campaigns.", "Better use of budget — reduced friction and re-work saves cost and time.", "Higher accountability — established trust allows honest post-campaign evaluation and learning for future campaigns."]}, {"id": "adv-u2-c37-a-2", "text": "Example: Long-standing agency-client pairings (e.g., a company retaining the same agency for years) often produce more consistent, recognisable brand campaigns than relationships with frequent agency churn.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u2-c37-a-3", "text": "Exam Tip: Directly answer the word \"effectiveness\" — tie every point back to campaign outcomes (creativity, speed, consistency, cost, learning), not just relationship harmony in the abstract.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:48.671005+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:48.671005+00'),
('aaa1701e-1b26-4539-abef-1b3043831617', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'What are the common methods of Agency Compensation?', 'Priority: Tier 3', 'Commission System — agency earns a percentage (traditionally ~15%) of the media spend placed on the client''s behalf.
Fee-Based System — client pays an agreed fee for services rendered, regardless of media spend.
Cost-Plus Method — agency charges actual costs incurred plus an agreed mark-up/profit margin.
Performance-Based/Incentive Compensation — payment linked to agreed performance outcomes (e.g., campaign results, sales impact).
Combination Method — a mix of a base fee plus commission or performance incentives.

Exam Tip: Commission and fee-based are the two most classical methods — mention these first and confidently, then add performance-based as the modern addition.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 38, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ef0d1cf3-b12e-43ce-96f4-c320b8780da5', '7602e97c-08d3-4781-b333-71223c28ecf6', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u2-c38-q-1", "text": "What are the common methods of Agency Compensation?", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u2-c38-a-1", "type": "numberList", "items": ["Commission System — agency earns a percentage (traditionally ~15%) of the media spend placed on the client''s behalf.", "Fee-Based System — client pays an agreed fee for services rendered, regardless of media spend.", "Cost-Plus Method — agency charges actual costs incurred plus an agreed mark-up/profit margin.", "Performance-Based/Incentive Compensation — payment linked to agreed performance outcomes (e.g., campaign results, sales impact).", "Combination Method — a mix of a base fee plus commission or performance incentives."]}, {"id": "adv-u2-c38-a-2", "text": "Exam Tip: Commission and fee-based are the two most classical methods — mention these first and confidently, then add performance-based as the modern addition.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:48.671005+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:48.671005+00'),
('06caeb24-127a-4bcd-ae6a-ec40f8b5ea56', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'What is creativity in advertising? Explain its importance.', 'Priority: Tier 1', 'Meaning: Creativity in advertising is the ability to generate original, relevant, and impactful ideas that present a product or service in a way that captures attention, communicates the message effectively, and persuades the target audience to act. Explanation: It is not randomness or novelty for its own sake — advertising creativity must be strategically relevant: it must connect the brand''s message to the consumer''s needs while standing out from competing clutter.

Key Points — Importance of Creativity:

Cuts through clutter — helps an ad stand out amid hundreds of daily commercial messages.
Builds brand recall — memorable, creative executions are retained longer by consumers.
Enhances persuasion — creative framing makes rational claims more emotionally compelling.
Differentiates the brand — supports positioning against competitors with similar products.
Increases engagement/share-ability, especially for digital and social campaigns.
Supports campaign objectives — awareness, consideration, or conversion goals are achieved more efficiently.

Example: Cadbury Dairy Milk''s "Kuch Khaas Hai" campaign used an ordinary moment (a girl running onto a cricket field) to build extraordinary emotional recall for a mass-market chocolate.

Application/Conclusion: Creativity is thus the bridge between a brand''s strategic objective and consumer attention — without it, even a sound marketing strategy fails to communicate effectively.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '498c2335-c7a8-46b5-92b1-4a9ba1d47e80', '98db0a64-c0db-4bc8-9e81-a9ffa36b19ff', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u3-c1-q-1", "text": "What is creativity in advertising? Explain its importance.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u3-c1-a-1", "text": "Meaning: Creativity in advertising is the ability to generate original, relevant, and impactful ideas that present a product or service in a way that captures attention, communicates the message effectively, and persuades the target audience to act. Explanation: It is not randomness or novelty for its own sake — advertising creativity must be strategically relevant: it must connect the brand''s message to the consumer''s needs while standing out from competing clutter.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u3-c1-a-2", "bold": true, "text": "Key Points — Importance of Creativity:", "type": "text", "style": "heading"}, {"id": "adv-u3-c1-a-3", "type": "numberList", "items": ["Cuts through clutter — helps an ad stand out amid hundreds of daily commercial messages.", "Builds brand recall — memorable, creative executions are retained longer by consumers.", "Enhances persuasion — creative framing makes rational claims more emotionally compelling.", "Differentiates the brand — supports positioning against competitors with similar products.", "Increases engagement/share-ability, especially for digital and social campaigns.", "Supports campaign objectives — awareness, consideration, or conversion goals are achieved more efficiently."]}, {"id": "adv-u3-c1-a-4", "text": "Example: Cadbury Dairy Milk''s \"Kuch Khaas Hai\" campaign used an ordinary moment (a girl running onto a cricket field) to build extraordinary emotional recall for a mass-market chocolate.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u3-c1-a-5", "text": "Application/Conclusion: Creativity is thus the bridge between a brand''s strategic objective and consumer attention — without it, even a sound marketing strategy fails to communicate effectively.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:51.006955+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:51.006955+00'),
('4ca4b5e2-a6e7-45a3-bb87-372df7f792ff', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'Explain the creative process in advertising.', 'Priority: Tier 1', 'Meaning: The creative process is the sequence of stages an advertiser/copywriter follows to convert a brand brief into a finished, persuasive advertisement.

Sequential Steps:

Immersion — gathering all relevant information: product facts, target audience, market, competition.
Digestion — working the information over mentally; identifying the core problem to be solved.
Incubation — stepping back and letting ideas develop subconsciously.
Illumination — the "Eureka moment" when the core creative idea emerges.
Verification/Evaluation — testing, refining and shaping the idea into a workable, on-brief execution.

Example: A copywriter briefed on an eco-friendly shoe brand immerses in research on sustainability trends, digests the target millennial audience''s values, incubates over a few days, arrives at the idea "Step Lightly on the Planet," then verifies it against the brand''s tone and USP.

Application/Conclusion: Following this process ensures ideas are both original and strategically grounded rather than arbitrary.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 2, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '498c2335-c7a8-46b5-92b1-4a9ba1d47e80', '98db0a64-c0db-4bc8-9e81-a9ffa36b19ff', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u3-c2-q-1", "text": "Explain the creative process in advertising.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u3-c2-a-1", "text": "Meaning: The creative process is the sequence of stages an advertiser/copywriter follows to convert a brand brief into a finished, persuasive advertisement.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u3-c2-a-2", "bold": true, "text": "Sequential Steps:", "type": "text", "style": "heading"}, {"id": "adv-u3-c2-a-3", "type": "numberList", "items": ["Immersion — gathering all relevant information: product facts, target audience, market, competition.", "Digestion — working the information over mentally; identifying the core problem to be solved.", "Incubation — stepping back and letting ideas develop subconsciously.", "Illumination — the \"Eureka moment\" when the core creative idea emerges.", "Verification/Evaluation — testing, refining and shaping the idea into a workable, on-brief execution."]}, {"id": "adv-u3-c2-a-4", "text": "Example: A copywriter briefed on an eco-friendly shoe brand immerses in research on sustainability trends, digests the target millennial audience''s values, incubates over a few days, arrives at the idea \"Step Lightly on the Planet,\" then verifies it against the brand''s tone and USP.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u3-c2-a-5", "text": "Application/Conclusion: Following this process ensures ideas are both original and strategically grounded rather than arbitrary.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:51.006955+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:51.006955+00'),
('016e61da-4728-463c-bd91-3b9d9114445f', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'What is creative strategy development? How does it relate to an advertising campaign?', 'Priority: Tier 1', 'Meaning: Creative strategy development is the process of deciding what to say (the message) and how to say it (the execution approach) to achieve the advertiser''s communication objective.

Key Points:

Begins with the client brief/marketing objective.
Identifies the target audience and their insight/need.
Determines the message theme or major selling idea (the single-minded proposition).
Selects an appeal and execution style suited to the message and audience.
Feeds into the advertising campaign — a coordinated series of ads, across media, built around one central creative strategy and theme, running over a defined period to achieve specific objectives.

Example: Amul''s decades-long campaign is built on one creative strategy — topical humour with the Amul girl — executed consistently across hundreds of individual ads.

Application/Conclusion: Creative strategy is the "brain" of a campaign; execution details (TV, print, radio ads) are its expressions, all traceable to one message theme.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 3, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '498c2335-c7a8-46b5-92b1-4a9ba1d47e80', '98db0a64-c0db-4bc8-9e81-a9ffa36b19ff', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u3-c3-q-1", "text": "What is creative strategy development? How does it relate to an advertising campaign?", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u3-c3-a-1", "text": "Meaning: Creative strategy development is the process of deciding what to say (the message) and how to say it (the execution approach) to achieve the advertiser''s communication objective.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u3-c3-a-2", "bold": true, "text": "Key Points:", "type": "text", "style": "heading"}, {"id": "adv-u3-c3-a-3", "type": "numberList", "items": ["Begins with the client brief/marketing objective.", "Identifies the target audience and their insight/need.", "Determines the message theme or major selling idea (the single-minded proposition).", "Selects an appeal and execution style suited to the message and audience.", "Feeds into the advertising campaign — a coordinated series of ads, across media, built around one central creative strategy and theme, running over a defined period to achieve specific objectives."]}, {"id": "adv-u3-c3-a-4", "text": "Example: Amul''s decades-long campaign is built on one creative strategy — topical humour with the Amul girl — executed consistently across hundreds of individual ads.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u3-c3-a-5", "text": "Application/Conclusion: Creative strategy is the \"brain\" of a campaign; execution details (TV, print, radio ads) are its expressions, all traceable to one message theme.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:51.006955+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:51.006955+00'),
('2159f9c7-2118-42da-bd7f-d0858ce3a3a3', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'What is meant by "Message Theme" or "Major Selling Idea" in advertising?', 'Priority: Tier 2', 'Meaning: The message theme (major selling idea) is the single central thought or benefit around which an entire advertising campaign is built — it answers "what is the one thing we want the consumer to remember?"

Key Points:

Must be rooted in the USP or a genuine consumer benefit.
Should be simple, memorable, and consistent across all campaign executions.
Guides copywriters and art directors in headline, visual and tagline creation.

Example: Fevicol''s message theme of "unbreakable bond" runs across all its humorous ad executions.

Application/Conclusion: Without a clear message theme, a campaign''s individual ads may look creative individually but fail to build a coherent brand image.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 4, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '498c2335-c7a8-46b5-92b1-4a9ba1d47e80', '98db0a64-c0db-4bc8-9e81-a9ffa36b19ff', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u3-c4-q-1", "text": "What is meant by \"Message Theme\" or \"Major Selling Idea\" in advertising?", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u3-c4-a-1", "text": "Meaning: The message theme (major selling idea) is the single central thought or benefit around which an entire advertising campaign is built — it answers \"what is the one thing we want the consumer to remember?\"", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u3-c4-a-2", "bold": true, "text": "Key Points:", "type": "text", "style": "heading"}, {"id": "adv-u3-c4-a-3", "type": "numberList", "items": ["Must be rooted in the USP or a genuine consumer benefit.", "Should be simple, memorable, and consistent across all campaign executions.", "Guides copywriters and art directors in headline, visual and tagline creation."]}, {"id": "adv-u3-c4-a-4", "text": "Example: Fevicol''s message theme of \"unbreakable bond\" runs across all its humorous ad executions.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u3-c4-a-5", "text": "Application/Conclusion: Without a clear message theme, a campaign''s individual ads may look creative individually but fail to build a coherent brand image.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:51.006955+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:51.006955+00'),
('3f0dc386-43eb-4faa-8d5b-5187ca23b780', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'What is USP (Unique Selling Proposition)? Explain its importance in advertising.', 'Priority: Tier 2', 'Meaning: USP is a distinct, meaningful benefit or feature of a product that differentiates it from competitors and gives consumers a specific reason to prefer it.

Key Points — Importance:

Forms the foundation of the message theme.
Helps in differentiation in a cluttered market.
Provides clarity of communication — one clear reason to buy.
Supports positioning in the consumer''s mind.
Guides copywriting (headline and body copy revolve around it).

Example: Domino''s USP — "30 minutes or it''s free" — was a distinct, verifiable promise rather than a generic quality claim.

Application/Conclusion: A strong USP makes both the creative strategy and the resulting advertisement sharper and more persuasive.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 5, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '498c2335-c7a8-46b5-92b1-4a9ba1d47e80', '98db0a64-c0db-4bc8-9e81-a9ffa36b19ff', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u3-c5-q-1", "text": "What is USP (Unique Selling Proposition)? Explain its importance in advertising.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u3-c5-a-1", "text": "Meaning: USP is a distinct, meaningful benefit or feature of a product that differentiates it from competitors and gives consumers a specific reason to prefer it.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u3-c5-a-2", "bold": true, "text": "Key Points — Importance:", "type": "text", "style": "heading"}, {"id": "adv-u3-c5-a-3", "type": "numberList", "items": ["Forms the foundation of the message theme.", "Helps in differentiation in a cluttered market.", "Provides clarity of communication — one clear reason to buy.", "Supports positioning in the consumer''s mind.", "Guides copywriting (headline and body copy revolve around it)."]}, {"id": "adv-u3-c5-a-4", "text": "Example: Domino''s USP — \"30 minutes or it''s free\" — was a distinct, verifiable promise rather than a generic quality claim.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u3-c5-a-5", "text": "Application/Conclusion: A strong USP makes both the creative strategy and the resulting advertisement sharper and more persuasive.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:51.006955+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:51.006955+00'),
('d5a0cce1-34b5-4ba3-9628-64509ec59dfc', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'Distinguish between USP and Positioning.', 'Priority: Tier 2', 'Basis | USP | Positioning
Meaning | A specific unique product benefit/feature | The place a brand occupies in the consumer''s mind relative to competitors
Focus | Product-centric (what the product offers) | Consumer-mind-centric (how the brand is perceived)
Scope | Often one clear claim | Broader — encompasses image, price tier, usage occasion, values
Example | "30-minute delivery" (Domino''s) | "The pizza brand for reliable, fast delivery" (Domino''s overall market position)
Relationship | USP often becomes the basis for positioning | Positioning is the strategic outcome built using the USP as a supporting proof point

Application/Conclusion: USP is a tool; positioning is the goal — the USP is communicated through advertising to secure the desired position.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 6, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '498c2335-c7a8-46b5-92b1-4a9ba1d47e80', '98db0a64-c0db-4bc8-9e81-a9ffa36b19ff', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u3-c6-q-1", "text": "Distinguish between USP and Positioning.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u3-c6-a-1", "rows": [["Basis", "USP", "Positioning"], ["Meaning", "A specific unique product benefit/feature", "The place a brand occupies in the consumer''s mind relative to competitors"], ["Focus", "Product-centric (what the product offers)", "Consumer-mind-centric (how the brand is perceived)"], ["Scope", "Often one clear claim", "Broader — encompasses image, price tier, usage occasion, values"], ["Example", "\"30-minute delivery\" (Domino''s)", "\"The pizza brand for reliable, fast delivery\" (Domino''s overall market position)"], ["Relationship", "USP often becomes the basis for positioning", "Positioning is the strategic outcome built using the USP as a supporting proof point"]], "type": "table"}, {"id": "adv-u3-c6-a-2", "text": "Application/Conclusion: USP is a tool; positioning is the goal — the USP is communicated through advertising to secure the desired position.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:51.006955+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:51.006955+00'),
('26e0556b-72ed-4dd5-9406-27cfd33c42e0', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'Explain the major positioning strategies used in advertising.', 'Priority: Tier 2', 'Meaning: Positioning strategy refers to the approach used to establish a distinct, favourable place for a brand in the target consumer''s mind relative to competing brands.

Key Points — Common Positioning Strategies:

Attribute/Feature-based positioning — emphasising a specific product feature (e.g., Colgate — cavity protection).
Benefit positioning — focusing on the benefit delivered rather than the feature (e.g., Dettol — protection/safety).
Price/Quality positioning — positioning on value-for-money or premium quality (e.g., D-Mart — everyday low prices; Mercedes — premium quality).
Use/Application positioning — associating the brand with a specific usage occasion (e.g., Cadbury 5-Star as a "me time" snack).
User/Target-audience positioning — positioning around a specific consumer segment (e.g., Horlicks for growing children).
Competitor positioning — positioning directly against a rival (e.g., comparative claims in detergent ads).
Category positioning — positioning as the leader/originator of a category (e.g., Bisleri as the pioneer of packaged drinking water in India).

Application/Conclusion: The choice of positioning strategy must align with the USP and target audience insight, and is then translated into advertising executions.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 7, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '498c2335-c7a8-46b5-92b1-4a9ba1d47e80', '98db0a64-c0db-4bc8-9e81-a9ffa36b19ff', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u3-c7-q-1", "text": "Explain the major positioning strategies used in advertising.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u3-c7-a-1", "text": "Meaning: Positioning strategy refers to the approach used to establish a distinct, favourable place for a brand in the target consumer''s mind relative to competing brands.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u3-c7-a-2", "bold": true, "text": "Key Points — Common Positioning Strategies:", "type": "text", "style": "heading"}, {"id": "adv-u3-c7-a-3", "type": "numberList", "items": ["Attribute/Feature-based positioning — emphasising a specific product feature (e.g., Colgate — cavity protection).", "Benefit positioning — focusing on the benefit delivered rather than the feature (e.g., Dettol — protection/safety).", "Price/Quality positioning — positioning on value-for-money or premium quality (e.g., D-Mart — everyday low prices; Mercedes — premium quality).", "Use/Application positioning — associating the brand with a specific usage occasion (e.g., Cadbury 5-Star as a \"me time\" snack).", "User/Target-audience positioning — positioning around a specific consumer segment (e.g., Horlicks for growing children).", "Competitor positioning — positioning directly against a rival (e.g., comparative claims in detergent ads).", "Category positioning — positioning as the leader/originator of a category (e.g., Bisleri as the pioneer of packaged drinking water in India)."]}, {"id": "adv-u3-c7-a-4", "text": "Application/Conclusion: The choice of positioning strategy must align with the USP and target audience insight, and is then translated into advertising executions.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:51.006955+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:51.006955+00'),
('57f6a1b3-3053-47f1-92d3-b74447fe71ff', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'What is persuasion in advertising? Explain the concept of advertising appeal.', 'Priority: Tier 1', 'Meaning: Persuasion is the process of influencing consumer attitudes and behaviour toward a brand through communication. An advertising appeal is the persuasive approach or strategy used to stimulate consumer interest and influence purchase decisions.

Key Points:

Appeals connect the product''s benefit to a psychological or emotional trigger in the consumer.
The right appeal depends on the product category, target audience, and objective.
Appeals shape the tone, message and execution of the advertisement.

Application/Conclusion: Selecting the wrong appeal (e.g., a purely humorous appeal for a life-insurance product) can undermine the credibility of the message, so appeal selection is a strategic creative decision — not purely subjective.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 8, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '498c2335-c7a8-46b5-92b1-4a9ba1d47e80', '98db0a64-c0db-4bc8-9e81-a9ffa36b19ff', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u3-c8-q-1", "text": "What is persuasion in advertising? Explain the concept of advertising appeal.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u3-c8-a-1", "text": "Meaning: Persuasion is the process of influencing consumer attitudes and behaviour toward a brand through communication. An advertising appeal is the persuasive approach or strategy used to stimulate consumer interest and influence purchase decisions.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u3-c8-a-2", "bold": true, "text": "Key Points:", "type": "text", "style": "heading"}, {"id": "adv-u3-c8-a-3", "type": "numberList", "items": ["Appeals connect the product''s benefit to a psychological or emotional trigger in the consumer.", "The right appeal depends on the product category, target audience, and objective.", "Appeals shape the tone, message and execution of the advertisement."]}, {"id": "adv-u3-c8-a-4", "text": "Application/Conclusion: Selecting the wrong appeal (e.g., a purely humorous appeal for a life-insurance product) can undermine the credibility of the message, so appeal selection is a strategic creative decision — not purely subjective.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:51.006955+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:51.006955+00'),
('7c0f2a24-5f28-481f-803f-b4f005fa58bf', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'Explain the types of advertising appeals with examples.', 'Priority: Tier 1', 'Meaning: Advertising appeals are the persuasive strategies used to stimulate a target consumer''s desire to purchase a product or service.

Key Points — Types of Appeals:

Rational Appeal — targets logic, facts, and functional benefits (price, quality, durability, performance). Example: A smartphone ad emphasising battery life and camera specifications.
Emotional Appeal — targets feelings such as love, nostalgia, fear, pride or belonging. Example: Cadbury''s father-daughter reunion ad appealing to familial love.
Humour Appeal — uses wit or comedy to make the ad memorable and likeable. Example: Amul''s topical cartoon-style hoardings.
Fear Appeal — highlights a risk or negative consequence of not using the product. Example: Insurance or antivirus software ads.
Sex/Glamour Appeal — uses attractiveness or aspirational imagery (used cautiously; regulated in India). Example: Certain fragrance/deodorant ads.
Scarcity/Urgency Appeal — creates a sense of limited availability or time. Example: "Sale ends tonight" e-commerce ads.
Social/Bandwagon Appeal — suggests that "everyone is doing it," using social proof. Example: "India''s No. 1 choice" claims.

Application/Conclusion: In practice, ads often blend appeals (e.g., rational + emotional), but the dominant appeal should match the product category and buying-decision type (high-involvement products lean rational; low-involvement/lifestyle products lean emotional or humour).', NULL, NULL, NULL, 9, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '498c2335-c7a8-46b5-92b1-4a9ba1d47e80', '98db0a64-c0db-4bc8-9e81-a9ffa36b19ff', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u3-c9-q-1", "text": "Explain the types of advertising appeals with examples.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u3-c9-a-1", "text": "Meaning: Advertising appeals are the persuasive strategies used to stimulate a target consumer''s desire to purchase a product or service.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u3-c9-a-2", "bold": true, "text": "Key Points — Types of Appeals:", "type": "text", "style": "heading"}, {"id": "adv-u3-c9-a-3", "type": "numberList", "items": ["Rational Appeal — targets logic, facts, and functional benefits (price, quality, durability, performance). Example: A smartphone ad emphasising battery life and camera specifications.", "Emotional Appeal — targets feelings such as love, nostalgia, fear, pride or belonging. Example: Cadbury''s father-daughter reunion ad appealing to familial love.", "Humour Appeal — uses wit or comedy to make the ad memorable and likeable. Example: Amul''s topical cartoon-style hoardings.", "Fear Appeal — highlights a risk or negative consequence of not using the product. Example: Insurance or antivirus software ads.", "Sex/Glamour Appeal — uses attractiveness or aspirational imagery (used cautiously; regulated in India). Example: Certain fragrance/deodorant ads.", "Scarcity/Urgency Appeal — creates a sense of limited availability or time. Example: \"Sale ends tonight\" e-commerce ads.", "Social/Bandwagon Appeal — suggests that \"everyone is doing it,\" using social proof. Example: \"India''s No. 1 choice\" claims."]}, {"id": "adv-u3-c9-a-4", "text": "Application/Conclusion: In practice, ads often blend appeals (e.g., rational + emotional), but the dominant appeal should match the product category and buying-decision type (high-involvement products lean rational; low-involvement/lifestyle products lean emotional or humour).", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:51.006955+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:51.006955+00'),
('5bbf58ad-c79e-42b5-85a2-209184c4213d', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'Differentiate between Rational and Emotional advertising appeals.', 'Priority: Tier 1', 'Basis | Rational Appeal | Emotional Appeal
Basis of persuasion | Logic, facts, functional benefits | Feelings, moods, psychological needs
Suited to | High-involvement, functional products (electronics, insurance, cars) | Low-involvement or lifestyle/aspirational products (chocolates, apparel, jewellery)
Content emphasis | Price, features, performance data, comparisons | Relationships, nostalgia, pride, belonging, humour
Example | A laptop ad citing processor speed and battery backup | A jewellery ad depicting a mother''s love for her daughter
Risk | May feel dry/uninvolving if overused | May lack credibility if not backed by real product benefit

Application/Conclusion: The two are not mutually exclusive — many successful campaigns use a rational USP delivered through an emotional narrative.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 10, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '498c2335-c7a8-46b5-92b1-4a9ba1d47e80', '98db0a64-c0db-4bc8-9e81-a9ffa36b19ff', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u3-c10-q-1", "text": "Differentiate between Rational and Emotional advertising appeals.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u3-c10-a-1", "rows": [["Basis", "Rational Appeal", "Emotional Appeal"], ["Basis of persuasion", "Logic, facts, functional benefits", "Feelings, moods, psychological needs"], ["Suited to", "High-involvement, functional products (electronics, insurance, cars)", "Low-involvement or lifestyle/aspirational products (chocolates, apparel, jewellery)"], ["Content emphasis", "Price, features, performance data, comparisons", "Relationships, nostalgia, pride, belonging, humour"], ["Example", "A laptop ad citing processor speed and battery backup", "A jewellery ad depicting a mother''s love for her daughter"], ["Risk", "May feel dry/uninvolving if overused", "May lack credibility if not backed by real product benefit"]], "type": "table"}, {"id": "adv-u3-c10-a-2", "text": "Application/Conclusion: The two are not mutually exclusive — many successful campaigns use a rational USP delivered through an emotional narrative.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:51.006955+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:51.006955+00'),
('89498110-8eda-480d-a08b-104c4b3f3512', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'A sportswear brand is launching eco-friendly running shoes. Identify the advertising appeals available and suggest which fits best.', 'Priority: Tier 1', 'Available appeals for this brand:

Rational appeal — sustainability credentials (recycled materials, comfort, durability).
Emotional appeal — pride in contributing to environmental protection; connection to an active, conscious lifestyle.
Humour appeal — lighter tone contrasting "old wasteful habits" vs. the new eco-shoe (lower fit for a values-driven product).

Recommended appeal: A combination of rational and emotional appeal fits best — rational to substantiate the eco-friendly claim (materials, certifications) and emotional to connect with the consumer''s self-image as an environmentally responsible, active individual.

Justification: Purely humour-based appeal risks undermining the seriousness of a sustainability message, while purely rational appeal may fail to create the aspirational pull needed in sportswear advertising. This is a creative judgment supported by category norms, not an absolute rule.

Application/Conclusion: This illustrates how appeal selection must be justified through product category and audience insight rather than treated as a fixed "correct answer."', NULL, NULL, NULL, 11, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '498c2335-c7a8-46b5-92b1-4a9ba1d47e80', '98db0a64-c0db-4bc8-9e81-a9ffa36b19ff', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u3-c11-q-1", "text": "A sportswear brand is launching eco-friendly running shoes. Identify the advertising appeals available and suggest which fits best.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u3-c11-a-1", "bold": true, "text": "Available appeals for this brand:", "type": "text", "style": "heading"}, {"id": "adv-u3-c11-a-2", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Rational appeal — sustainability credentials (recycled materials, comfort, durability).", "Emotional appeal — pride in contributing to environmental protection; connection to an active, conscious lifestyle.", "Humour appeal — lighter tone contrasting \"old wasteful habits\" vs. the new eco-shoe (lower fit for a values-driven product)."]}, {"id": "adv-u3-c11-a-3", "text": "Recommended appeal: A combination of rational and emotional appeal fits best — rational to substantiate the eco-friendly claim (materials, certifications) and emotional to connect with the consumer''s self-image as an environmentally responsible, active individual.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u3-c11-a-4", "text": "Justification: Purely humour-based appeal risks undermining the seriousness of a sustainability message, while purely rational appeal may fail to create the aspirational pull needed in sportswear advertising. This is a creative judgment supported by category norms, not an absolute rule.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u3-c11-a-5", "text": "Application/Conclusion: This illustrates how appeal selection must be justified through product category and audience insight rather than treated as a fixed \"correct answer.\"", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:51.006955+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:51.006955+00'),
('c0199dad-9c0a-4891-88ab-b3937bde004c', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'Explain the role of "source" in advertisements. Why does source credibility matter?', 'Priority: Tier 1', 'Meaning: The "source" is the person, character or entity that delivers or is associated with the advertising message — e.g., a celebrity, expert, company spokesperson, or an "ordinary consumer."

Key Points:

The source affects how credible, trustworthy and persuasive the message appears (Source Credibility Model — expertise + trustworthiness).
A source with attractiveness and likeability can improve audience attention and message retention (Source Attractiveness Model).
Source-message-product fit/congruence matters — a mismatched source can weaken believability.
Sources can be celebrities, experts, CEOs, or "typical consumer" testimonials, each suited to different message types.

Example: A doctor endorsing a health supplement uses expert credibility; a film star endorsing a soft drink uses attractiveness/likeability rather than expertise.

Application/Conclusion: Source selection is a strategic creative decision that must align with the brand''s message and target audience for the endorsement to enhance rather than dilute persuasion.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 12, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '498c2335-c7a8-46b5-92b1-4a9ba1d47e80', '98db0a64-c0db-4bc8-9e81-a9ffa36b19ff', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u3-c12-q-1", "text": "Explain the role of \"source\" in advertisements. Why does source credibility matter?", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u3-c12-a-1", "text": "Meaning: The \"source\" is the person, character or entity that delivers or is associated with the advertising message — e.g., a celebrity, expert, company spokesperson, or an \"ordinary consumer.\"", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u3-c12-a-2", "bold": true, "text": "Key Points:", "type": "text", "style": "heading"}, {"id": "adv-u3-c12-a-3", "type": "numberList", "items": ["The source affects how credible, trustworthy and persuasive the message appears (Source Credibility Model — expertise + trustworthiness).", "A source with attractiveness and likeability can improve audience attention and message retention (Source Attractiveness Model).", "Source-message-product fit/congruence matters — a mismatched source can weaken believability.", "Sources can be celebrities, experts, CEOs, or \"typical consumer\" testimonials, each suited to different message types."]}, {"id": "adv-u3-c12-a-4", "text": "Example: A doctor endorsing a health supplement uses expert credibility; a film star endorsing a soft drink uses attractiveness/likeability rather than expertise.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u3-c12-a-5", "text": "Application/Conclusion: Source selection is a strategic creative decision that must align with the brand''s message and target audience for the endorsement to enhance rather than dilute persuasion.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:51.006955+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:51.006955+00'),
('bb7c2383-cc87-4c08-a3e1-1f0a88374d66', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'Why are celebrities effective sources in Indian advertisements?', 'Priority: Tier 1', 'Meaning: Celebrity endorsement uses a well-known public figure (film star, sports personality, influencer) to promote a brand, leveraging the celebrity''s image to enhance the brand''s appeal.

Key Points — Why Effective in India:

High attention value — Indian consumers, especially in a cinema- and cricket-driven culture, pay strong attention to celebrity faces.
Instant credibility transfer — a trusted celebrity''s positive image "transfers" to the brand (Meaning Transfer Model).
Aspiration and relatability — celebrities represent aspirational lifestyles many consumers wish to emulate.
Wide reach — celebrities have mass recall across regions and languages, useful in a diverse market.
Differentiation — helps a brand stand out in a cluttered, price-competitive market.
Category relevance — sports stars for fitness/sportswear, film stars for FMCG/fashion, build believable associations.

Risks/Limitations (balance):

Overexposure of a celebrity across multiple brands dilutes impact.
Celebrity controversies can damage brand image ("source bloopers").
High cost relative to actual sales impact is not always guaranteed.

Application/Conclusion: Celebrities are effective largely due to attention, aspiration and credibility transfer, but their effectiveness is not unconditional — it depends on fit between celebrity image and brand/product category. (No unsupported claim of guaranteed purchase-influence should be presented as fact.)', NULL, NULL, NULL, 13, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '498c2335-c7a8-46b5-92b1-4a9ba1d47e80', '98db0a64-c0db-4bc8-9e81-a9ffa36b19ff', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u3-c13-q-1", "text": "Why are celebrities effective sources in Indian advertisements?", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u3-c13-a-1", "text": "Meaning: Celebrity endorsement uses a well-known public figure (film star, sports personality, influencer) to promote a brand, leveraging the celebrity''s image to enhance the brand''s appeal.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u3-c13-a-2", "bold": true, "text": "Key Points — Why Effective in India:", "type": "text", "style": "heading"}, {"id": "adv-u3-c13-a-3", "type": "numberList", "items": ["High attention value — Indian consumers, especially in a cinema- and cricket-driven culture, pay strong attention to celebrity faces.", "Instant credibility transfer — a trusted celebrity''s positive image \"transfers\" to the brand (Meaning Transfer Model).", "Aspiration and relatability — celebrities represent aspirational lifestyles many consumers wish to emulate.", "Wide reach — celebrities have mass recall across regions and languages, useful in a diverse market.", "Differentiation — helps a brand stand out in a cluttered, price-competitive market.", "Category relevance — sports stars for fitness/sportswear, film stars for FMCG/fashion, build believable associations."]}, {"id": "adv-u3-c13-a-4", "bold": true, "text": "Risks/Limitations (balance):", "type": "text", "style": "heading"}, {"id": "adv-u3-c13-a-5", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Overexposure of a celebrity across multiple brands dilutes impact.", "Celebrity controversies can damage brand image (\"source bloopers\").", "High cost relative to actual sales impact is not always guaranteed."]}, {"id": "adv-u3-c13-a-6", "text": "Application/Conclusion: Celebrities are effective largely due to attention, aspiration and credibility transfer, but their effectiveness is not unconditional — it depends on fit between celebrity image and brand/product category. (No unsupported claim of guaranteed purchase-influence should be presented as fact.)", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:51.006955+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:51.006955+00'),
('999c3c5b-45ed-407f-ac81-2945355dc8c8', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', '"Celebrity endorsement is a popular advertising strategy." Do you agree that celebrity endorsements influence purchase decisions? Justify.', 'Priority: Tier 1', 'Meaning: This is a "discuss and justify" question requiring a balanced, reasoned position rather than a one-sided claim.

Balanced Explanation:

Supporting view: Celebrities can increase attention, aid recall, and lend credibility/aspiration, which can influence consideration and, in some cases, purchase — especially for low-involvement, image-driven products.
Limiting view: Purchase decisions are also driven by price, product quality, need, and availability; celebrity presence alone does not guarantee a sale, and effectiveness varies by product fit, celebrity credibility, and audience scepticism toward paid endorsements.

Key Points to include in the answer:

Explain the mechanism (attention → credibility/aspiration → attitude change → possible purchase intent).
Note that impact is stronger for image-driven/lifestyle products and weaker for highly functional/technical purchases.
Mention fit/congruence as the deciding factor.
Conclude with a justified, moderate position: celebrities can influence purchase decisions when there is strong brand-celebrity fit and credibility, but they are not a substitute for product quality.

Application/Conclusion: A well-justified, nuanced answer scores higher than a blanket "yes" or "no."', NULL, NULL, NULL, 14, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '498c2335-c7a8-46b5-92b1-4a9ba1d47e80', '98db0a64-c0db-4bc8-9e81-a9ffa36b19ff', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u3-c14-q-1", "text": "\"Celebrity endorsement is a popular advertising strategy.\" Do you agree that celebrity endorsements influence purchase decisions? Justify.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u3-c14-a-1", "text": "Meaning: This is a \"discuss and justify\" question requiring a balanced, reasoned position rather than a one-sided claim.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u3-c14-a-2", "bold": true, "text": "Balanced Explanation:", "type": "text", "style": "heading"}, {"id": "adv-u3-c14-a-3", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Supporting view: Celebrities can increase attention, aid recall, and lend credibility/aspiration, which can influence consideration and, in some cases, purchase — especially for low-involvement, image-driven products.", "Limiting view: Purchase decisions are also driven by price, product quality, need, and availability; celebrity presence alone does not guarantee a sale, and effectiveness varies by product fit, celebrity credibility, and audience scepticism toward paid endorsements."]}, {"id": "adv-u3-c14-a-4", "bold": true, "text": "Key Points to include in the answer:", "type": "text", "style": "heading"}, {"id": "adv-u3-c14-a-5", "type": "numberList", "items": ["Explain the mechanism (attention → credibility/aspiration → attitude change → possible purchase intent).", "Note that impact is stronger for image-driven/lifestyle products and weaker for highly functional/technical purchases.", "Mention fit/congruence as the deciding factor.", "Conclude with a justified, moderate position: celebrities can influence purchase decisions when there is strong brand-celebrity fit and credibility, but they are not a substitute for product quality."]}, {"id": "adv-u3-c14-a-6", "text": "Application/Conclusion: A well-justified, nuanced answer scores higher than a blanket \"yes\" or \"no.\"", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:51.006955+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:51.006955+00'),
('c62aef47-b51a-4d2f-a23a-29e644ff292a', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'Explain the major execution styles used in presenting advertisements.', 'Priority: Tier 2', 'Meaning: Execution style refers to the creative approach or format used to present the advertising message to the target audience.

Key Points — Major Execution Styles:

Slice of Life — depicts an everyday situation where the product solves a problem. Example: A detergent ad showing a mother removing a child''s stain.
Lifestyle — shows the product fitting into a desirable, aspirational lifestyle. Example: A premium watch ad set in a luxury environment.
Fantasy — creates an imaginative, surreal scenario around the product. Example: An ad depicting a magical transformation.
Mood/Image — builds an emotional atmosphere or brand image rather than explicit claims. Example: A perfume ad relying on visuals and music alone.
Musical — communicates the message through a jingle or song. Example: Colours-jingle-driven FMCG ads.
Personality Symbol — uses a central character (human or animated) as the brand''s face. Example: The Amul girl, Vodafone ZooZoo.
Technical Expertise — highlights the company''s skill/craftsmanship in making the product. Example: A car ad showing engineering precision.
Scientific/Factual Evidence — presents survey/research data or expert proof. Example: Toothpaste ads citing dentist recommendations.
Testimonial — features a satisfied customer or expert vouching for the product.

Application/Conclusion: The chosen execution style must align with the appeal and message theme; e.g., an emotional appeal is best delivered through slice-of-life or mood/image execution, while a rational appeal suits scientific/factual execution.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 15, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '498c2335-c7a8-46b5-92b1-4a9ba1d47e80', '98db0a64-c0db-4bc8-9e81-a9ffa36b19ff', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u3-c15-q-1", "text": "Explain the major execution styles used in presenting advertisements.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u3-c15-a-1", "text": "Meaning: Execution style refers to the creative approach or format used to present the advertising message to the target audience.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u3-c15-a-2", "bold": true, "text": "Key Points — Major Execution Styles:", "type": "text", "style": "heading"}, {"id": "adv-u3-c15-a-3", "type": "numberList", "items": ["Slice of Life — depicts an everyday situation where the product solves a problem. Example: A detergent ad showing a mother removing a child''s stain.", "Lifestyle — shows the product fitting into a desirable, aspirational lifestyle. Example: A premium watch ad set in a luxury environment.", "Fantasy — creates an imaginative, surreal scenario around the product. Example: An ad depicting a magical transformation.", "Mood/Image — builds an emotional atmosphere or brand image rather than explicit claims. Example: A perfume ad relying on visuals and music alone.", "Musical — communicates the message through a jingle or song. Example: Colours-jingle-driven FMCG ads.", "Personality Symbol — uses a central character (human or animated) as the brand''s face. Example: The Amul girl, Vodafone ZooZoo.", "Technical Expertise — highlights the company''s skill/craftsmanship in making the product. Example: A car ad showing engineering precision.", "Scientific/Factual Evidence — presents survey/research data or expert proof. Example: Toothpaste ads citing dentist recommendations.", "Testimonial — features a satisfied customer or expert vouching for the product."]}, {"id": "adv-u3-c15-a-4", "text": "Application/Conclusion: The chosen execution style must align with the appeal and message theme; e.g., an emotional appeal is best delivered through slice-of-life or mood/image execution, while a rational appeal suits scientific/factual execution.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:51.006955+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:51.006955+00'),
('305849e5-b512-4062-a893-e99fbf7ddc5f', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'Define and explain the key elements of an advertisement: Logo, Company Signature, Slogan, Tagline, Jingle, Illustration.', 'Priority: Tier 2', 'Logo — a distinctive graphic symbol or design that visually identifies a brand or company at a glance (e.g., the Nike swoosh).
Company Signature — the formal, standardised way a company''s name/logo is presented in all its communications, often including the logo plus company name in a fixed style, ensuring consistent identity.
Slogan — a short, memorable phrase used for a specific campaign or product, which may change over time (e.g., "Impossible is Nothing" — used for a specific campaign period).
Tagline — a short, memorable phrase that captures the brand''s overall identity or promise and is used consistently over the long term (e.g., "Just Do It" — Nike''s enduring brand tagline).
Jingle — a short, catchy tune or musical phrase, often with lyrics, used to make the brand/message memorable, primarily in audio/audio-visual media.
Illustration — the visual artwork, photograph, or drawing used in an advertisement to attract attention, support the headline, and visually communicate the product benefit.

Application/Conclusion: These elements work together to build consistent brand identity and recall across every advertisement and touchpoint.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 16, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '498c2335-c7a8-46b5-92b1-4a9ba1d47e80', '98db0a64-c0db-4bc8-9e81-a9ffa36b19ff', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u3-c16-q-1", "text": "Define and explain the key elements of an advertisement: Logo, Company Signature, Slogan, Tagline, Jingle, Illustration.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u3-c16-a-1", "type": "numberList", "items": ["Logo — a distinctive graphic symbol or design that visually identifies a brand or company at a glance (e.g., the Nike swoosh).", "Company Signature — the formal, standardised way a company''s name/logo is presented in all its communications, often including the logo plus company name in a fixed style, ensuring consistent identity.", "Slogan — a short, memorable phrase used for a specific campaign or product, which may change over time (e.g., \"Impossible is Nothing\" — used for a specific campaign period).", "Tagline — a short, memorable phrase that captures the brand''s overall identity or promise and is used consistently over the long term (e.g., \"Just Do It\" — Nike''s enduring brand tagline).", "Jingle — a short, catchy tune or musical phrase, often with lyrics, used to make the brand/message memorable, primarily in audio/audio-visual media.", "Illustration — the visual artwork, photograph, or drawing used in an advertisement to attract attention, support the headline, and visually communicate the product benefit."]}, {"id": "adv-u3-c16-a-2", "text": "Application/Conclusion: These elements work together to build consistent brand identity and recall across every advertisement and touchpoint.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:51.006955+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:51.006955+00'),
('260c469b-96cb-464b-9c79-f347c40dacda', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'Differentiate between Slogan and Tagline.', 'Priority: Tier 2', 'Basis | Slogan | Tagline
Duration | Often campaign-specific; may change | Long-term; represents the brand consistently
Scope | Tied to a specific product/campaign message | Tied to the overall brand identity/philosophy
Purpose | Reinforces a specific promotional message | Encapsulates the brand''s core promise/personality
Example | A limited-period festive-sale slogan | "Just Do It" (Nike) — used for decades

Application/Conclusion: In practice the two terms are sometimes used interchangeably in casual usage, but in an examination answer this distinction (campaign-specific vs. brand-enduring) should be clearly drawn.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 17, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '498c2335-c7a8-46b5-92b1-4a9ba1d47e80', '98db0a64-c0db-4bc8-9e81-a9ffa36b19ff', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u3-c17-q-1", "text": "Differentiate between Slogan and Tagline.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u3-c17-a-1", "rows": [["Basis", "Slogan", "Tagline"], ["Duration", "Often campaign-specific; may change", "Long-term; represents the brand consistently"], ["Scope", "Tied to a specific product/campaign message", "Tied to the overall brand identity/philosophy"], ["Purpose", "Reinforces a specific promotional message", "Encapsulates the brand''s core promise/personality"], ["Example", "A limited-period festive-sale slogan", "\"Just Do It\" (Nike) — used for decades"]], "type": "table"}, {"id": "adv-u3-c17-a-2", "text": "Application/Conclusion: In practice the two terms are sometimes used interchangeably in casual usage, but in an examination answer this distinction (campaign-specific vs. brand-enduring) should be clearly drawn.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:51.006955+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:51.006955+00'),
('7f36d6f2-9fad-4dca-ba9b-5c119cedac4f', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'Differentiate between Logo and Company Signature.', 'Priority: Tier 2', 'Basis | Logo | Company Signature
Meaning | The graphic symbol/mark alone | The complete standardised presentation of brand identity (logo + name + sometimes tagline, in fixed typography/colour)
Scope | A single visual element | A broader identity system applied consistently across communications
Function | Quick visual recognition | Formal, consistent brand representation in all official communication
Example | The Apple bitten-apple symbol | The full "Apple Inc." lock-up used on official letterheads/ads

Application/Conclusion: The logo is a component of the company signature, not a substitute for it.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 18, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '498c2335-c7a8-46b5-92b1-4a9ba1d47e80', '98db0a64-c0db-4bc8-9e81-a9ffa36b19ff', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u3-c18-q-1", "text": "Differentiate between Logo and Company Signature.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u3-c18-a-1", "rows": [["Basis", "Logo", "Company Signature"], ["Meaning", "The graphic symbol/mark alone", "The complete standardised presentation of brand identity (logo + name + sometimes tagline, in fixed typography/colour)"], ["Scope", "A single visual element", "A broader identity system applied consistently across communications"], ["Function", "Quick visual recognition", "Formal, consistent brand representation in all official communication"], ["Example", "The Apple bitten-apple symbol", "The full \"Apple Inc.\" lock-up used on official letterheads/ads"]], "type": "table"}, {"id": "adv-u3-c18-a-2", "text": "Application/Conclusion: The logo is a component of the company signature, not a substitute for it.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:51.006955+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:51.006955+00'),
('1c8b8641-fbf0-4791-b174-7ead6cd92dbd', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'Differentiate between Headline and Slogan/Tagline.', 'Priority: Tier 2', 'Basis | Headline | Slogan/Tagline
Location/Use | Appears at the top of a specific print/digital ad | Appears across multiple ads/brand communications
Purpose | Grabs attention and draws the reader into that specific ad''s body copy | Reinforces brand/campaign identity consistently
Life span | Usually unique to a single advertisement | Reused over a campaign or the brand''s lifetime
Example | "Freshness that lasts all day" (specific ad headline) | "Thanda Matlab Coca-Cola" (long-running brand tagline)

Application/Conclusion: A headline sells the specific ad; a tagline/slogan sells the brand.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 19, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '498c2335-c7a8-46b5-92b1-4a9ba1d47e80', '98db0a64-c0db-4bc8-9e81-a9ffa36b19ff', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u3-c19-q-1", "text": "Differentiate between Headline and Slogan/Tagline.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u3-c19-a-1", "rows": [["Basis", "Headline", "Slogan/Tagline"], ["Location/Use", "Appears at the top of a specific print/digital ad", "Appears across multiple ads/brand communications"], ["Purpose", "Grabs attention and draws the reader into that specific ad''s body copy", "Reinforces brand/campaign identity consistently"], ["Life span", "Usually unique to a single advertisement", "Reused over a campaign or the brand''s lifetime"], ["Example", "\"Freshness that lasts all day\" (specific ad headline)", "\"Thanda Matlab Coca-Cola\" (long-running brand tagline)"]], "type": "table"}, {"id": "adv-u3-c19-a-2", "text": "Application/Conclusion: A headline sells the specific ad; a tagline/slogan sells the brand.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:51.006955+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:51.006955+00'),
('5223c0a3-d100-4cf2-9042-e099571da569', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'Explain the process of creating a TV commercial.', 'Priority: Tier 2', 'Meaning: Creating a TV commercial involves translating a creative concept into a moving, audio-visual advertisement through a structured production process.

Process + Components:

Concept/Idea Development — deriving the core creative idea from the message theme.
Script Writing — writing the dialogue, narration, and scene descriptions that carry the message.
Storyboard — a sequence of drawn panels/frames depicting each key shot, used to visualise the commercial before filming (see Flashcard 21 for structure).
Visual Techniques — camera angles, transitions, special effects, colour grading, and pacing used to enhance impact.
Optical Elements — graphics, text overlays, product shots, and visual effects layered onto the footage.
Soundtrack/Music — background score, jingles, and sound effects that reinforce mood and brand recall.
Production and Post-Production — filming, editing, and finalising the commercial for broadcast.

Example: A soft-drink TVC might use a fast-paced script, vibrant visual techniques (quick cuts, bright colour grading), an upbeat jingle, and celebratory optical text ("Chill Out!") to reinforce the brand''s youthful positioning.

Application/Conclusion: Each component must work together — a strong script with weak sound design, or vice versa, weakens the overall commercial''s impact.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 20, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '498c2335-c7a8-46b5-92b1-4a9ba1d47e80', '98db0a64-c0db-4bc8-9e81-a9ffa36b19ff', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u3-c20-q-1", "text": "Explain the process of creating a TV commercial.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u3-c20-a-1", "text": "Meaning: Creating a TV commercial involves translating a creative concept into a moving, audio-visual advertisement through a structured production process.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u3-c20-a-2", "bold": true, "text": "Process + Components:", "type": "text", "style": "heading"}, {"id": "adv-u3-c20-a-3", "type": "numberList", "items": ["Concept/Idea Development — deriving the core creative idea from the message theme.", "Script Writing — writing the dialogue, narration, and scene descriptions that carry the message.", "Storyboard — a sequence of drawn panels/frames depicting each key shot, used to visualise the commercial before filming (see Flashcard 21 for structure).", "Visual Techniques — camera angles, transitions, special effects, colour grading, and pacing used to enhance impact.", "Optical Elements — graphics, text overlays, product shots, and visual effects layered onto the footage.", "Soundtrack/Music — background score, jingles, and sound effects that reinforce mood and brand recall.", "Production and Post-Production — filming, editing, and finalising the commercial for broadcast."]}, {"id": "adv-u3-c20-a-4", "text": "Example: A soft-drink TVC might use a fast-paced script, vibrant visual techniques (quick cuts, bright colour grading), an upbeat jingle, and celebratory optical text (\"Chill Out!\") to reinforce the brand''s youthful positioning.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u3-c20-a-5", "text": "Application/Conclusion: Each component must work together — a strong script with weak sound design, or vice versa, weakens the overall commercial''s impact.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:51.006955+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:51.006955+00'),
('03ab1a0f-318a-4e46-a4a7-0b24faa649c9', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'What is a storyboard? Explain its structure with a diagram.', 'Priority: Tier 2', 'Meaning: A storyboard is a sequence of sketched panels (like a comic strip) that visually represents the key scenes/shots of a TV commercial in order, along with corresponding audio/dialogue notes, used for planning before actual filming.

Basic Storyboard Structure (exam-drawable):

------------------      ------------------      ------------------

PANEL 1 |  | PANEL 2 |  | PANEL 3
[Sketch of Shot] |  | [Sketch of Shot] |  | [Sketch of Shot]
 |  |  |  | 

------------------      ------------------      ------------------ Scene: Opening        Scene: Problem shown    Scene: Product used Dialogue/VO: ...        Dialogue/VO: ...        Dialogue/VO: ... Duration: 3 sec         Duration: 5 sec         Duration: 4 sec  Each panel typically notes: shot description (visual), dialogue/voiceover, sound/music cue, and duration.

Key Points:

Ensures all stakeholders (client, director, editor) share a common visual plan before costly filming begins.
Helps sequence the narrative logically: problem → product → benefit → brand sign-off.
Saves production time and cost by catching creative/logistical issues early.

Application/Conclusion: The storyboard is the bridge between the written script and the final filmed commercial.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 21, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '498c2335-c7a8-46b5-92b1-4a9ba1d47e80', '98db0a64-c0db-4bc8-9e81-a9ffa36b19ff', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u3-c21-q-1", "text": "What is a storyboard? Explain its structure with a diagram.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u3-c21-a-1", "text": "Meaning: A storyboard is a sequence of sketched panels (like a comic strip) that visually represents the key scenes/shots of a TV commercial in order, along with corresponding audio/dialogue notes, used for planning before actual filming.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u3-c21-a-2", "bold": true, "text": "Basic Storyboard Structure (exam-drawable):", "type": "text", "style": "heading"}, {"id": "adv-u3-c21-a-3", "text": "------------------      ------------------      ------------------", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u3-c21-a-4", "rows": [["PANEL 1", "", "PANEL 2", "", "PANEL 3"], ["[Sketch of Shot]", "", "[Sketch of Shot]", "", "[Sketch of Shot]"], ["", "", "", "", ""]], "type": "table"}, {"id": "adv-u3-c21-a-5", "text": "------------------      ------------------      ------------------ Scene: Opening        Scene: Problem shown    Scene: Product used Dialogue/VO: ...        Dialogue/VO: ...        Dialogue/VO: ... Duration: 3 sec         Duration: 5 sec         Duration: 4 sec  Each panel typically notes: shot description (visual), dialogue/voiceover, sound/music cue, and duration.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u3-c21-a-6", "bold": true, "text": "Key Points:", "type": "text", "style": "heading"}, {"id": "adv-u3-c21-a-7", "type": "numberList", "items": ["Ensures all stakeholders (client, director, editor) share a common visual plan before costly filming begins.", "Helps sequence the narrative logically: problem → product → benefit → brand sign-off.", "Saves production time and cost by catching creative/logistical issues early."]}, {"id": "adv-u3-c21-a-8", "text": "Application/Conclusion: The storyboard is the bridge between the written script and the final filmed commercial.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:51.006955+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:51.006955+00'),
('00c95601-3f75-42f2-af0b-2d95ddf39941', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'Explain the key elements involved in creating a Radio Commercial and why each matters.', 'Priority: Tier 2', 'Meaning: A radio commercial relies entirely on audio — words, sound, and music — to create a mental picture and persuade the listener, since there is no visual support.

Characteristics Explained (why each matters in radio):

Words — must be simple, vivid, and conversational since listeners cannot re-read them; word choice must create mental imagery.
Sound (sound effects) — used to set scenes and add realism/drama that visuals would otherwise provide (e.g., a door creaking, traffic noise).
Music — sets mood, reinforces brand identity (jingles), and aids recall in an audio-only medium.
Scriptwriting — must be tightly timed (usually 15–30–60 seconds) and structured for the ear, not the eye.
Clarity — the message must be immediately understandable on a single listen, since listeners cannot replay it.
Coherence — ideas must flow logically since there are no visual cues to guide understanding.
Pleasantness — the tone/voice/sound must be enjoyable, not irritating, to avoid negative brand association.
Believability — the message and voice must sound credible and authentic.
Interest — must capture attention within the first few seconds, as radio is often a "background" medium.
Distinctiveness — must stand out from other ads in the same commercial break through a unique voice, sound, or jingle.

Application/Conclusion: Because radio lacks visuals, every one of these characteristics compensates by making the audio itself memorable, clear, and persuasive.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 22, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '498c2335-c7a8-46b5-92b1-4a9ba1d47e80', '98db0a64-c0db-4bc8-9e81-a9ffa36b19ff', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u3-c22-q-1", "text": "Explain the key elements involved in creating a Radio Commercial and why each matters.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u3-c22-a-1", "text": "Meaning: A radio commercial relies entirely on audio — words, sound, and music — to create a mental picture and persuade the listener, since there is no visual support.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u3-c22-a-2", "bold": true, "text": "Characteristics Explained (why each matters in radio):", "type": "text", "style": "heading"}, {"id": "adv-u3-c22-a-3", "type": "numberList", "items": ["Words — must be simple, vivid, and conversational since listeners cannot re-read them; word choice must create mental imagery.", "Sound (sound effects) — used to set scenes and add realism/drama that visuals would otherwise provide (e.g., a door creaking, traffic noise).", "Music — sets mood, reinforces brand identity (jingles), and aids recall in an audio-only medium.", "Scriptwriting — must be tightly timed (usually 15–30–60 seconds) and structured for the ear, not the eye.", "Clarity — the message must be immediately understandable on a single listen, since listeners cannot replay it.", "Coherence — ideas must flow logically since there are no visual cues to guide understanding.", "Pleasantness — the tone/voice/sound must be enjoyable, not irritating, to avoid negative brand association.", "Believability — the message and voice must sound credible and authentic.", "Interest — must capture attention within the first few seconds, as radio is often a \"background\" medium.", "Distinctiveness — must stand out from other ads in the same commercial break through a unique voice, sound, or jingle."]}, {"id": "adv-u3-c22-a-4", "text": "Application/Conclusion: Because radio lacks visuals, every one of these characteristics compensates by making the audio itself memorable, clear, and persuasive.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:51.006955+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:51.006955+00'),
('3b8467c6-7444-45b4-b385-89c867510330', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'What is an advertisement copy? Explain its elements with examples.', 'Priority: Tier 1', 'Meaning: Advertisement copy is the complete written (and associated visual/layout) content of an advertisement — everything that communicates the message to the target audience, including headline, body text and supporting elements.

Elements of Advertisement Copy (component-wise):

Headline — the most prominent line, designed to grab attention and draw the reader into the ad. Example: "Melts in Your Mouth, Not in Your Hands."
Sub-headline — a supporting line that expands on the headline''s promise, bridging it to the body copy.
Layout — the visual arrangement of all elements (headline, visual, body copy, logo) for maximum readability and impact.
Body Copy — the main descriptive text that explains the product''s benefits, USP, and persuades the reader in detail.
Slogan — a short, memorable campaign-specific phrase reinforcing the message.
Signature (Company Signature) — the standardised brand identity block (logo + name) confirming who is advertising.
Closing Idea (Call to Action) — the final line prompting the reader to act (buy, visit, call, click).

Example (illustrative): A print ad for a juice brand — Headline: "Nature, Bottled Fresh"; Sub-headline: "100% Organic, Zero Preservatives"; Body copy explaining sourcing; Slogan: "Pure. Simple. You."; Logo/signature at the bottom; Closing idea: "Available at stores near you."

Application/Conclusion: A well-constructed copy ensures the reader''s attention flows logically from headline → interest → desire → action (AIDA model).', NULL, NULL, NULL, 23, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '498c2335-c7a8-46b5-92b1-4a9ba1d47e80', '98db0a64-c0db-4bc8-9e81-a9ffa36b19ff', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u3-c23-q-1", "text": "What is an advertisement copy? Explain its elements with examples.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u3-c23-a-1", "text": "Meaning: Advertisement copy is the complete written (and associated visual/layout) content of an advertisement — everything that communicates the message to the target audience, including headline, body text and supporting elements.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u3-c23-a-2", "bold": true, "text": "Elements of Advertisement Copy (component-wise):", "type": "text", "style": "heading"}, {"id": "adv-u3-c23-a-3", "type": "numberList", "items": ["Headline — the most prominent line, designed to grab attention and draw the reader into the ad. Example: \"Melts in Your Mouth, Not in Your Hands.\"", "Sub-headline — a supporting line that expands on the headline''s promise, bridging it to the body copy.", "Layout — the visual arrangement of all elements (headline, visual, body copy, logo) for maximum readability and impact.", "Body Copy — the main descriptive text that explains the product''s benefits, USP, and persuades the reader in detail.", "Slogan — a short, memorable campaign-specific phrase reinforcing the message.", "Signature (Company Signature) — the standardised brand identity block (logo + name) confirming who is advertising.", "Closing Idea (Call to Action) — the final line prompting the reader to act (buy, visit, call, click)."]}, {"id": "adv-u3-c23-a-4", "text": "Example (illustrative): A print ad for a juice brand — Headline: \"Nature, Bottled Fresh\"; Sub-headline: \"100% Organic, Zero Preservatives\"; Body copy explaining sourcing; Slogan: \"Pure. Simple. You.\"; Logo/signature at the bottom; Closing idea: \"Available at stores near you.\"", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u3-c23-a-5", "text": "Application/Conclusion: A well-constructed copy ensures the reader''s attention flows logically from headline → interest → desire → action (AIDA model).", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:51.006955+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:51.006955+00'),
('132b8d84-82eb-454d-8853-f0be90686049', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'Explain the Percentage-of-Sales method of advertising budgeting.', 'Priority: Tier 2', 'Meaning: The advertising budget is fixed as a percentage of either past sales (current or previous year) or forecasted/anticipated future sales. How it works: A fixed percentage (e.g., 5% of sales) is applied to the sales figure to arrive at the budget amount.

Advantages: Simple and widely used; keeps advertising expenditure proportionate to the firm''s financial capacity; encourages financial discipline.

Limitations: Reverses the cause-and-effect logic — sales should ideally result from advertising, not determine it; can lead to under-spending when sales are falling (exactly when more advertising may be needed) and overspending when sales are already strong.

Suitable application: Stable, mature product categories with predictable sales patterns, e.g., established FMCG brands.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 4, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '825dd962-2813-41cf-a5f5-0a886af35bb5', '582dfbe3-f05e-43ac-97da-309e85ada2e9', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u4-c4-q-1", "text": "Explain the Percentage-of-Sales method of advertising budgeting.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u4-c4-a-1", "text": "Meaning: The advertising budget is fixed as a percentage of either past sales (current or previous year) or forecasted/anticipated future sales. How it works: A fixed percentage (e.g., 5% of sales) is applied to the sales figure to arrive at the budget amount.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u4-c4-a-2", "text": "Advantages: Simple and widely used; keeps advertising expenditure proportionate to the firm''s financial capacity; encourages financial discipline.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u4-c4-a-3", "text": "Limitations: Reverses the cause-and-effect logic — sales should ideally result from advertising, not determine it; can lead to under-spending when sales are falling (exactly when more advertising may be needed) and overspending when sales are already strong.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u4-c4-a-4", "text": "Suitable application: Stable, mature product categories with predictable sales patterns, e.g., established FMCG brands.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:52.71444+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:52.71444+00'),
('8feeee6c-83cc-4e37-8f5b-6abe17d3880b', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'Explain the essentials of good copy and the principles of copywriting for Print and OOH (Out-of-Home).', 'Priority: Tier 2', 'Essentials of Good Copy:

Clear and simple language — easily understood at a glance.
Honest and credible — claims must be truthful and defensible.
Attention-grabbing headline.
Single-minded focus on one core benefit/USP.
Persuasive and benefit-oriented, not just feature-listing.
Includes a clear call to action.
Consistent with brand tone and identity.

Principles of Copywriting for Print:

Strong, benefit-driven headline.
Logical flow: headline → sub-headline → body copy → CTA.
Adequate white space; not overcrowded.
Body copy should be scannable (short paragraphs/bullet-like structure).
Visual and copy must complement, not duplicate, each other.

Principles of Copywriting for OOH (hoardings/billboards):

Extreme brevity — viewers see it for only a few seconds (ideally under 7–8 words).
Large, legible typography, readable from a distance/moving vehicle.
Minimal elements — one visual, one line, one brand mark.
High contrast and bold colours for visibility.
No complex body copy — OOH cannot carry detailed information.

Application/Conclusion: Print copy can be layered and detailed since readers engage voluntarily; OOH copy must be instantly graspable due to extremely limited exposure time.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 24, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '498c2335-c7a8-46b5-92b1-4a9ba1d47e80', '98db0a64-c0db-4bc8-9e81-a9ffa36b19ff', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u3-c24-q-1", "text": "Explain the essentials of good copy and the principles of copywriting for Print and OOH (Out-of-Home).", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u3-c24-a-1", "bold": true, "text": "Essentials of Good Copy:", "type": "text", "style": "heading"}, {"id": "adv-u3-c24-a-2", "type": "numberList", "items": ["Clear and simple language — easily understood at a glance.", "Honest and credible — claims must be truthful and defensible.", "Attention-grabbing headline.", "Single-minded focus on one core benefit/USP.", "Persuasive and benefit-oriented, not just feature-listing.", "Includes a clear call to action.", "Consistent with brand tone and identity."]}, {"id": "adv-u3-c24-a-3", "bold": true, "text": "Principles of Copywriting for Print:", "type": "text", "style": "heading"}, {"id": "adv-u3-c24-a-4", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Strong, benefit-driven headline.", "Logical flow: headline → sub-headline → body copy → CTA.", "Adequate white space; not overcrowded.", "Body copy should be scannable (short paragraphs/bullet-like structure).", "Visual and copy must complement, not duplicate, each other."]}, {"id": "adv-u3-c24-a-5", "bold": true, "text": "Principles of Copywriting for OOH (hoardings/billboards):", "type": "text", "style": "heading"}, {"id": "adv-u3-c24-a-6", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Extreme brevity — viewers see it for only a few seconds (ideally under 7–8 words).", "Large, legible typography, readable from a distance/moving vehicle.", "Minimal elements — one visual, one line, one brand mark.", "High contrast and bold colours for visibility.", "No complex body copy — OOH cannot carry detailed information."]}, {"id": "adv-u3-c24-a-7", "text": "Application/Conclusion: Print copy can be layered and detailed since readers engage voluntarily; OOH copy must be instantly graspable due to extremely limited exposure time.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:51.006955+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:51.006955+00'),
('c56c25cb-483b-4f01-bdb4-78891bd21186', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'Explain the types of advertisement copy.', 'Priority: Tier 2', 'Key Points — Types of Copy:

Reason-Why Copy — presents logical reasons/facts to justify the purchase (rational appeal-based).
Emotional Copy — appeals to feelings rather than logic.
Narrative/Story Copy — tells a story to engage the reader.
Institutional Copy — promotes the company''s image/reputation rather than a specific product.
Product Copy — focuses on a specific product''s features and benefits.
Testimonial Copy — uses real or implied customer/expert statements as proof.
Descriptive Copy — straightforwardly describes product features and uses.

Application/Conclusion: The choice of copy type should align with the chosen appeal and execution style covered earlier in the unit.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 25, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '498c2335-c7a8-46b5-92b1-4a9ba1d47e80', '98db0a64-c0db-4bc8-9e81-a9ffa36b19ff', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u3-c25-q-1", "text": "Explain the types of advertisement copy.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u3-c25-a-1", "bold": true, "text": "Key Points — Types of Copy:", "type": "text", "style": "heading"}, {"id": "adv-u3-c25-a-2", "type": "numberList", "items": ["Reason-Why Copy — presents logical reasons/facts to justify the purchase (rational appeal-based).", "Emotional Copy — appeals to feelings rather than logic.", "Narrative/Story Copy — tells a story to engage the reader.", "Institutional Copy — promotes the company''s image/reputation rather than a specific product.", "Product Copy — focuses on a specific product''s features and benefits.", "Testimonial Copy — uses real or implied customer/expert statements as proof.", "Descriptive Copy — straightforwardly describes product features and uses."]}, {"id": "adv-u3-c25-a-3", "text": "Application/Conclusion: The choice of copy type should align with the chosen appeal and execution style covered earlier in the unit.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:51.006955+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:51.006955+00'),
('1c1a93f5-738e-442b-b30a-494668eecd7b', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'What is copy research? Why is it conducted?', 'Priority: Tier 2', 'Meaning: Copy research (or copy testing) is the process of evaluating an advertisement''s copy — before or after release — to assess its clarity, appeal, persuasiveness, and likely effectiveness.

Key Points:

Pre-testing copy research — conducted before the ad is released, to check comprehension, recall potential, and appeal among a sample audience, allowing revisions before the full campaign launch.
Post-testing copy research — conducted after release, to measure actual recall, attitude change, and communication effectiveness.
Helps identify whether the headline, body copy, and visuals are being understood as intended.
Reduces the risk of wasted media spend on ineffective messaging.

Application/Conclusion: Copy research ensures that creative decisions are validated with audience feedback rather than resting purely on the creative team''s assumptions.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 26, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '498c2335-c7a8-46b5-92b1-4a9ba1d47e80', '98db0a64-c0db-4bc8-9e81-a9ffa36b19ff', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u3-c26-q-1", "text": "What is copy research? Why is it conducted?", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u3-c26-a-1", "text": "Meaning: Copy research (or copy testing) is the process of evaluating an advertisement''s copy — before or after release — to assess its clarity, appeal, persuasiveness, and likely effectiveness.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u3-c26-a-2", "bold": true, "text": "Key Points:", "type": "text", "style": "heading"}, {"id": "adv-u3-c26-a-3", "type": "numberList", "items": ["Pre-testing copy research — conducted before the ad is released, to check comprehension, recall potential, and appeal among a sample audience, allowing revisions before the full campaign launch.", "Post-testing copy research — conducted after release, to measure actual recall, attitude change, and communication effectiveness.", "Helps identify whether the headline, body copy, and visuals are being understood as intended.", "Reduces the risk of wasted media spend on ineffective messaging."]}, {"id": "adv-u3-c26-a-4", "text": "Application/Conclusion: Copy research ensures that creative decisions are validated with audience feedback rather than resting purely on the creative team''s assumptions.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:51.006955+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:51.006955+00'),
('135bc5eb-c395-40cb-ab20-d4e7bdabf244', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'What is an Advertising Budget? Explain its importance.', 'Priority: Tier 2', 'An advertising budget is the amount of money a firm allocates, over a defined period, to plan, create, and execute its advertising activities in order to achieve stated marketing and communication objectives. It converts advertising objectives into a financial plan and guides decisions on media selection, campaign scale, and frequency.

Importance:

Ensures advertising spend is aligned with marketing objectives rather than being arbitrary.
Acts as a control tool — actual spend can be compared against planned spend.
Helps in optimal allocation of funds across media, creative production, and research.
Prevents overspending (which hurts profitability) and underspending (which weakens brand recall and reach).
Provides a basis for evaluating the cost-effectiveness of a campaign.

Example: An FMCG company launching a new snack brand sets aside a fixed percentage of expected first-year sales specifically for television, digital, and in-store advertising.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '825dd962-2813-41cf-a5f5-0a886af35bb5', '582dfbe3-f05e-43ac-97da-309e85ada2e9', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u4-c1-q-1", "text": "What is an Advertising Budget? Explain its importance.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u4-c1-a-1", "text": "An advertising budget is the amount of money a firm allocates, over a defined period, to plan, create, and execute its advertising activities in order to achieve stated marketing and communication objectives. It converts advertising objectives into a financial plan and guides decisions on media selection, campaign scale, and frequency.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u4-c1-a-2", "bold": true, "text": "Importance:", "type": "text", "style": "heading"}, {"id": "adv-u4-c1-a-3", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Ensures advertising spend is aligned with marketing objectives rather than being arbitrary.", "Acts as a control tool — actual spend can be compared against planned spend.", "Helps in optimal allocation of funds across media, creative production, and research.", "Prevents overspending (which hurts profitability) and underspending (which weakens brand recall and reach).", "Provides a basis for evaluating the cost-effectiveness of a campaign."]}, {"id": "adv-u4-c1-a-4", "text": "Example: An FMCG company launching a new snack brand sets aside a fixed percentage of expected first-year sales specifically for television, digital, and in-store advertising.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph", "italic": true}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:52.71444+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:52.71444+00'),
('a816f064-54ed-4334-9852-a636493cfab4', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'State and explain the key features of an advertising budget.', 'Priority: Tier 2', 'Objective-linked: Derived from marketing and communication objectives (awareness, trial, recall, repositioning), not decided in isolation.
Time-bound: Prepared for a specific period — usually a financial year, a campaign period, or a product launch phase.
Flexible: Should allow adjustment for market conditions, competitor activity, or campaign performance mid-course.
Media-and-task specific: Broken down across media (TV, print, digital, outdoor) and tasks (creative production, media buying, research).
Measurable: Framed so that returns (reach, recall, sales lift) can be evaluated against the amount spent.
Influenced by multiple factors: Company size, product life-cycle stage, competition, target market size, and available funds all shape the final figure.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 2, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '825dd962-2813-41cf-a5f5-0a886af35bb5', '582dfbe3-f05e-43ac-97da-309e85ada2e9', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u4-c2-q-1", "text": "State and explain the key features of an advertising budget.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u4-c2-a-1", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Objective-linked: Derived from marketing and communication objectives (awareness, trial, recall, repositioning), not decided in isolation.", "Time-bound: Prepared for a specific period — usually a financial year, a campaign period, or a product launch phase.", "Flexible: Should allow adjustment for market conditions, competitor activity, or campaign performance mid-course.", "Media-and-task specific: Broken down across media (TV, print, digital, outdoor) and tasks (creative production, media buying, research).", "Measurable: Framed so that returns (reach, recall, sales lift) can be evaluated against the amount spent.", "Influenced by multiple factors: Company size, product life-cycle stage, competition, target market size, and available funds all shape the final figure."]}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:52.71444+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:52.71444+00'),
('a0924f2c-9508-4527-b574-0cc5c460880d', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'Explain the "Affordable" (All-You-Can-Afford) method of advertising budgeting.', 'Priority: Tier 2', 'Meaning: The firm allocates whatever amount of money it can afford to spend on advertising after covering all other essential operating costs. How it works: Management first meets production, distribution, and other operational expenses; the residual amount (if any) is assigned to advertising.

Advantages: Simple to apply; keeps the firm within its financial means; useful for small businesses or firms with limited funds.

Limitations: Ignores the relationship between advertising spend and sales/market objectives; budget can fluctuate unpredictably year to year, disrupting continuity; advertising is treated as a residual cost rather than an investment.

Suitable application: Small and medium enterprises, or firms in the early, cash-constrained stage of business.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 3, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '825dd962-2813-41cf-a5f5-0a886af35bb5', '582dfbe3-f05e-43ac-97da-309e85ada2e9', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u4-c3-q-1", "text": "Explain the \"Affordable\" (All-You-Can-Afford) method of advertising budgeting.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u4-c3-a-1", "text": "Meaning: The firm allocates whatever amount of money it can afford to spend on advertising after covering all other essential operating costs. How it works: Management first meets production, distribution, and other operational expenses; the residual amount (if any) is assigned to advertising.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u4-c3-a-2", "text": "Advantages: Simple to apply; keeps the firm within its financial means; useful for small businesses or firms with limited funds.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u4-c3-a-3", "text": "Limitations: Ignores the relationship between advertising spend and sales/market objectives; budget can fluctuate unpredictably year to year, disrupting continuity; advertising is treated as a residual cost rather than an investment.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u4-c3-a-4", "text": "Suitable application: Small and medium enterprises, or firms in the early, cash-constrained stage of business.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:52.71444+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:52.71444+00'),
('07269f81-b32c-49dc-9613-7d740f946de9', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'Explain the Competitive Parity method of advertising budgeting.', 'Priority: Tier 2', 'Meaning: The firm sets its advertising budget to match or stay proportionate to what competitors in the same industry are spending. How it works: The company benchmarks industry advertising-to-sales ratios or competitor spend levels and sets its own budget accordingly, to maintain "share of voice."

Advantages: Helps prevent advertising wars; maintains competitive parity in visibility; uses collective industry wisdom as a benchmark.

Limitations: Assumes competitors'' budgeting is rational, which may not be true; ignores the firm''s own specific objectives, brand strength, and opportunities; hard to get accurate competitor spend data.

Suitable application: Highly competitive, oligopolistic categories such as telecom, FMCG, and automobiles, where relative visibility matters.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 5, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '825dd962-2813-41cf-a5f5-0a886af35bb5', '582dfbe3-f05e-43ac-97da-309e85ada2e9', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u4-c5-q-1", "text": "Explain the Competitive Parity method of advertising budgeting.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u4-c5-a-1", "text": "Meaning: The firm sets its advertising budget to match or stay proportionate to what competitors in the same industry are spending. How it works: The company benchmarks industry advertising-to-sales ratios or competitor spend levels and sets its own budget accordingly, to maintain \"share of voice.\"", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u4-c5-a-2", "text": "Advantages: Helps prevent advertising wars; maintains competitive parity in visibility; uses collective industry wisdom as a benchmark.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u4-c5-a-3", "text": "Limitations: Assumes competitors'' budgeting is rational, which may not be true; ignores the firm''s own specific objectives, brand strength, and opportunities; hard to get accurate competitor spend data.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u4-c5-a-4", "text": "Suitable application: Highly competitive, oligopolistic categories such as telecom, FMCG, and automobiles, where relative visibility matters.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:52.71444+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:52.71444+00'),
('a17e51dd-5726-40ff-9854-63a05e7e01a6', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'Explain the Objective-and-Task method of advertising budgeting.', 'Priority: Tier 1 (frequently the most examinable method)', 'Meaning: The most logical and scientific method — the budget is built from the bottom up by first defining specific advertising objectives, then determining the tasks needed to achieve them, and finally costing those tasks.

How it works (3 steps):

Define clear, measurable advertising objectives (e.g., raise brand awareness from 20% to 40% among urban youth in 6 months).
Identify the specific tasks/activities required to achieve each objective (media mix, frequency, creative production).
Estimate the cost of performing each task; the sum of these costs becomes the budget.

Advantages: Directly ties spending to objectives; most defensible and professional approach; allows clear performance evaluation against pre-set goals.

Limitations: Time-consuming and requires skilled planning; harder to execute for firms without clear research/data support.

Suitable application: Large organisations and professionally managed brands running objective-driven campaigns, e.g., a new product launch with a defined awareness target.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 6, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '825dd962-2813-41cf-a5f5-0a886af35bb5', '582dfbe3-f05e-43ac-97da-309e85ada2e9', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u4-c6-q-1", "text": "Explain the Objective-and-Task method of advertising budgeting.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u4-c6-a-1", "text": "Meaning: The most logical and scientific method — the budget is built from the bottom up by first defining specific advertising objectives, then determining the tasks needed to achieve them, and finally costing those tasks.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u4-c6-a-2", "bold": true, "text": "How it works (3 steps):", "type": "text", "style": "heading"}, {"id": "adv-u4-c6-a-3", "type": "numberList", "items": ["Define clear, measurable advertising objectives (e.g., raise brand awareness from 20% to 40% among urban youth in 6 months).", "Identify the specific tasks/activities required to achieve each objective (media mix, frequency, creative production).", "Estimate the cost of performing each task; the sum of these costs becomes the budget."]}, {"id": "adv-u4-c6-a-4", "text": "Advantages: Directly ties spending to objectives; most defensible and professional approach; allows clear performance evaluation against pre-set goals.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u4-c6-a-5", "text": "Limitations: Time-consuming and requires skilled planning; harder to execute for firms without clear research/data support.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u4-c6-a-6", "text": "Suitable application: Large organisations and professionally managed brands running objective-driven campaigns, e.g., a new product launch with a defined awareness target.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:52.71444+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:52.71444+00'),
('51079a56-0938-45e0-9827-8f1068e4094d', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'Explain the Unit-of-Sales (Fixed Sum per Unit) method of advertising budgeting.', 'Priority: Tier 3', 'Meaning: A fixed rupee amount is allocated for advertising per unit of product sold (past or expected). How it works: Advertising budget = fixed amount per unit × number of units sold/expected to be sold.

Advantages: Simple to calculate; directly links spend to actual sales volume; commonly used in industries with clearly countable units (e.g., automobiles, appliances).

Limitations: Like the percentage-of-sales method, it makes advertising a consequence of sales rather than a driver of it; does not account for changing market conditions or competitive activity.

Suitable application: Durable-goods categories such as automobiles and consumer electronics, where a per-unit advertising cost is easy to track.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 7, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '825dd962-2813-41cf-a5f5-0a886af35bb5', '582dfbe3-f05e-43ac-97da-309e85ada2e9', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u4-c7-q-1", "text": "Explain the Unit-of-Sales (Fixed Sum per Unit) method of advertising budgeting.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u4-c7-a-1", "text": "Meaning: A fixed rupee amount is allocated for advertising per unit of product sold (past or expected). How it works: Advertising budget = fixed amount per unit × number of units sold/expected to be sold.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u4-c7-a-2", "text": "Advantages: Simple to calculate; directly links spend to actual sales volume; commonly used in industries with clearly countable units (e.g., automobiles, appliances).", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u4-c7-a-3", "text": "Limitations: Like the percentage-of-sales method, it makes advertising a consequence of sales rather than a driver of it; does not account for changing market conditions or competitive activity.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u4-c7-a-4", "text": "Suitable application: Durable-goods categories such as automobiles and consumer electronics, where a per-unit advertising cost is easy to track.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:52.71444+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:52.71444+00'),
('9bff1add-781c-42fd-9e69-0542da7a02d4', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'What is Advertising Research? How does it improve advertising effectiveness?', 'Priority: Tier 2', 'Advertising research is the systematic collection, analysis, and interpretation of data relating to an advertisement or campaign — before, during, or after its release — to judge whether it is likely to achieve, or has achieved, its communication and marketing objectives.

How it improves effectiveness:

Identifies weaknesses in message, appeal, or execution before large sums are committed (pre-testing).
Confirms whether the campaign actually achieved recall, persuasion, or sales objectives after release (post-testing).
Provides evidence-based direction for revising creative content, media choice, and targeting.
Reduces the risk of wasted advertising spend and reputational damage from a poorly received ad.
Supports accountability — agencies and marketers can justify budgets with measurable outcomes.

This is the natural framing question for: "What is Advertising Research? Differentiate between Pre-Testing and Post-Testing" (Oct 2024, Q4A).', NULL, NULL, NULL, 8, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '825dd962-2813-41cf-a5f5-0a886af35bb5', '9038817a-ab59-4e79-81d5-acff95bfbdf8', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u4-c8-q-1", "text": "What is Advertising Research? How does it improve advertising effectiveness?", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u4-c8-a-1", "text": "Advertising research is the systematic collection, analysis, and interpretation of data relating to an advertisement or campaign — before, during, or after its release — to judge whether it is likely to achieve, or has achieved, its communication and marketing objectives.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u4-c8-a-2", "bold": true, "text": "How it improves effectiveness:", "type": "text", "style": "heading"}, {"id": "adv-u4-c8-a-3", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Identifies weaknesses in message, appeal, or execution before large sums are committed (pre-testing).", "Confirms whether the campaign actually achieved recall, persuasion, or sales objectives after release (post-testing).", "Provides evidence-based direction for revising creative content, media choice, and targeting.", "Reduces the risk of wasted advertising spend and reputational damage from a poorly received ad.", "Supports accountability — agencies and marketers can justify budgets with measurable outcomes."]}, {"id": "adv-u4-c8-a-4", "text": "This is the natural framing question for: \"What is Advertising Research? Differentiate between Pre-Testing and Post-Testing\" (Oct 2024, Q4A).", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph", "italic": true}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:52.71444+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:52.71444+00'),
('0cacfdce-b693-4bff-9a4c-6b26d4154dc7', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'What is Pre-testing? Explain its objectives and importance.', 'Priority: Tier 1', 'Meaning: Pre-testing (also called developmental research) is the evaluation of an advertisement or campaign concept before it is finally produced and released, to check its likely effectiveness.

Objectives:

To check whether the message is clearly understood by the target audience.
To identify the strongest creative concept/appeal among alternatives.
To detect any unintended, confusing, or offensive interpretations.
To estimate likely attention, recall, and persuasion levels before investing in production and media.
To refine copy, visuals, and layout prior to final release.

Importance: Saves cost by catching problems early; reduces risk of campaign failure; increases confidence before a large media spend commitment.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 9, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '825dd962-2813-41cf-a5f5-0a886af35bb5', '9038817a-ab59-4e79-81d5-acff95bfbdf8', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u4-c9-q-1", "text": "What is Pre-testing? Explain its objectives and importance.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u4-c9-a-1", "text": "Meaning: Pre-testing (also called developmental research) is the evaluation of an advertisement or campaign concept before it is finally produced and released, to check its likely effectiveness.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u4-c9-a-2", "bold": true, "text": "Objectives:", "type": "text", "style": "heading"}, {"id": "adv-u4-c9-a-3", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["To check whether the message is clearly understood by the target audience.", "To identify the strongest creative concept/appeal among alternatives.", "To detect any unintended, confusing, or offensive interpretations.", "To estimate likely attention, recall, and persuasion levels before investing in production and media.", "To refine copy, visuals, and layout prior to final release."]}, {"id": "adv-u4-c9-a-4", "text": "Importance: Saves cost by catching problems early; reduces risk of campaign failure; increases confidence before a large media spend commitment.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:52.71444+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:52.71444+00'),
('a32e7a74-6fcf-4f69-84fb-97c8a4c12167', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'Explain the testing process/procedure followed in Pre-testing.', 'Priority: Tier 1', 'Define objectives — what exactly needs to be tested (message clarity, appeal strength, recall potential).
Select the target respondent group — representative of the intended audience.
Present rough/near-final ad versions (storyboards, mock-ups, animatics, or draft copy) to respondents.
Apply a suitable pre-testing method (concept test, focus group, consumer jury, etc.).
Collect responses — comprehension, recall, likeability, persuasion.
Analyse results and revise the ad concept/copy accordingly before final production.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 10, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '825dd962-2813-41cf-a5f5-0a886af35bb5', '9038817a-ab59-4e79-81d5-acff95bfbdf8', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u4-c10-q-1", "text": "Explain the testing process/procedure followed in Pre-testing.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u4-c10-a-1", "type": "numberList", "items": ["Define objectives — what exactly needs to be tested (message clarity, appeal strength, recall potential).", "Select the target respondent group — representative of the intended audience.", "Present rough/near-final ad versions (storyboards, mock-ups, animatics, or draft copy) to respondents.", "Apply a suitable pre-testing method (concept test, focus group, consumer jury, etc.).", "Collect responses — comprehension, recall, likeability, persuasion.", "Analyse results and revise the ad concept/copy accordingly before final production."]}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:52.71444+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:52.71444+00'),
('5bb03e93-cb31-444f-8e28-ca96abb1474f', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'Explain any three methods of Pre-testing. (Directly asked in Oct 2024 & 2025)', 'Priority: Tier 1', 'Concept Testing: The core advertising idea/concept is presented to a sample of the target audience (often as a simple statement, sketch, or storyboard) to gauge initial reaction, relevance, and comprehension before any detailed ad is created.
Focus Group Discussion: A small group (typically 6–10) of target consumers discusses the draft advertisement under a moderator''s guidance; qualitative feedback is gathered on message clarity, appeal, and likely impact.
Consumer Jury Test: A panel of target consumers is shown two or more versions of an advertisement and asked to rank or rate them on attributes like attention-getting power, believability, and persuasiveness.
(Additional methods worth knowing) Portfolio Test: Respondents view a "portfolio" of ads (including the test ad among unrelated ones) and are later asked to recall what they remember, indicating the test ad''s relative impact.
Theatre/Trailer Test: Respondents view the ad in a controlled theatre-like setting, often alongside other programming, and their reactions (sometimes via dial-response devices) are measured in real time.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 11, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '825dd962-2813-41cf-a5f5-0a886af35bb5', '9038817a-ab59-4e79-81d5-acff95bfbdf8', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u4-c11-q-1", "text": "Explain any three methods of Pre-testing. (Directly asked in Oct 2024 & 2025)", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u4-c11-a-1", "type": "numberList", "items": ["Concept Testing: The core advertising idea/concept is presented to a sample of the target audience (often as a simple statement, sketch, or storyboard) to gauge initial reaction, relevance, and comprehension before any detailed ad is created.", "Focus Group Discussion: A small group (typically 6–10) of target consumers discusses the draft advertisement under a moderator''s guidance; qualitative feedback is gathered on message clarity, appeal, and likely impact.", "Consumer Jury Test: A panel of target consumers is shown two or more versions of an advertisement and asked to rank or rate them on attributes like attention-getting power, believability, and persuasiveness.", "(Additional methods worth knowing) Portfolio Test: Respondents view a \"portfolio\" of ads (including the test ad among unrelated ones) and are later asked to recall what they remember, indicating the test ad''s relative impact.", "Theatre/Trailer Test: Respondents view the ad in a controlled theatre-like setting, often alongside other programming, and their reactions (sometimes via dial-response devices) are measured in real time."]}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:52.71444+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:52.71444+00'),
('47ed017a-b1ea-4d20-af1e-9ff0960088d3', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'What is Post-testing? Explain its objectives and importance.', 'Priority: Tier 1', 'Meaning: Post-testing (also called evaluative research) is the assessment of an advertisement or campaign after it has been released, to measure its actual impact on the target audience.

Objectives:

To measure how many people saw, recalled, or recognised the ad.
To assess whether the ad changed attitudes, preferences, or purchase intention.
To measure the actual impact on sales, enquiries, or market share.
To evaluate media efficiency — which media vehicles delivered the best results.
To provide learning for planning future campaigns.

Importance: Confirms whether the money spent achieved its objectives; provides accountability to management; generates insights for the next campaign cycle.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 12, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '825dd962-2813-41cf-a5f5-0a886af35bb5', '9038817a-ab59-4e79-81d5-acff95bfbdf8', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u4-c12-q-1", "text": "What is Post-testing? Explain its objectives and importance.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u4-c12-a-1", "text": "Meaning: Post-testing (also called evaluative research) is the assessment of an advertisement or campaign after it has been released, to measure its actual impact on the target audience.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u4-c12-a-2", "bold": true, "text": "Objectives:", "type": "text", "style": "heading"}, {"id": "adv-u4-c12-a-3", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["To measure how many people saw, recalled, or recognised the ad.", "To assess whether the ad changed attitudes, preferences, or purchase intention.", "To measure the actual impact on sales, enquiries, or market share.", "To evaluate media efficiency — which media vehicles delivered the best results.", "To provide learning for planning future campaigns."]}, {"id": "adv-u4-c12-a-4", "text": "Importance: Confirms whether the money spent achieved its objectives; provides accountability to management; generates insights for the next campaign cycle.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:52.71444+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:52.71444+00'),
('5c3e48ca-d42c-4714-9c88-1b79bce2d85b', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'Explain the testing process/procedure followed in Post-testing.', 'Priority: Tier 1', 'Set measurement objectives based on the original campaign goals (recall, attitude change, sales).
Decide the measurement period — how long after release the ad will be tested.
Select respondents exposed to the actual released advertisement.
Apply a suitable post-testing method (recall test, recognition test, sales test, etc.).
Compare results against pre-set objectives/benchmarks.
Use findings to adjust the ongoing campaign or inform future advertising decisions.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 13, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '825dd962-2813-41cf-a5f5-0a886af35bb5', '9038817a-ab59-4e79-81d5-acff95bfbdf8', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u4-c13-q-1", "text": "Explain the testing process/procedure followed in Post-testing.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u4-c13-a-1", "type": "numberList", "items": ["Set measurement objectives based on the original campaign goals (recall, attitude change, sales).", "Decide the measurement period — how long after release the ad will be tested.", "Select respondents exposed to the actual released advertisement.", "Apply a suitable post-testing method (recall test, recognition test, sales test, etc.).", "Compare results against pre-set objectives/benchmarks.", "Use findings to adjust the ongoing campaign or inform future advertising decisions."]}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:52.71444+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:52.71444+00'),
('ee8cfc15-1fde-4a41-8568-d9f6dc1fb2c0', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'Explain any three methods of Post-testing. (Directly asked in Oct 2024 & 2025)', 'Priority: Tier 1', 'Recall Test (Aided/Unaided): Respondents are asked whether they remember seeing the advertisement, either without any prompt (unaided recall) or after being given the brand/category as a cue (aided recall).
Recognition Test (e.g., Starch Test): Respondents are shown the actual publication/advertisement and asked to indicate which parts (headline, visual, brand name) they recognise as having seen before.
Sales Test: The advertisement''s effectiveness is judged by tracking actual changes in sales, enquiries, or market share during and after the campaign, often using controlled test markets.
(Additional methods worth knowing) Attitude/Opinion Test: Measures shifts in consumer attitude, brand preference, or purchase intention before and after exposure to the campaign.
Coupon/Inquiry Test: Effectiveness is judged by the number of responses (coupons redeemed, calls made, website visits) generated directly by the advertisement.
Tracking Study: Continuous, periodic surveys that monitor brand awareness, recall, and attitude over the life of a campaign to detect trends.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 14, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '825dd962-2813-41cf-a5f5-0a886af35bb5', '9038817a-ab59-4e79-81d5-acff95bfbdf8', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u4-c14-q-1", "text": "Explain any three methods of Post-testing. (Directly asked in Oct 2024 & 2025)", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u4-c14-a-1", "type": "numberList", "items": ["Recall Test (Aided/Unaided): Respondents are asked whether they remember seeing the advertisement, either without any prompt (unaided recall) or after being given the brand/category as a cue (aided recall).", "Recognition Test (e.g., Starch Test): Respondents are shown the actual publication/advertisement and asked to indicate which parts (headline, visual, brand name) they recognise as having seen before.", "Sales Test: The advertisement''s effectiveness is judged by tracking actual changes in sales, enquiries, or market share during and after the campaign, often using controlled test markets.", "(Additional methods worth knowing) Attitude/Opinion Test: Measures shifts in consumer attitude, brand preference, or purchase intention before and after exposure to the campaign.", "Coupon/Inquiry Test: Effectiveness is judged by the number of responses (coupons redeemed, calls made, website visits) generated directly by the advertisement.", "Tracking Study: Continuous, periodic surveys that monitor brand awareness, recall, and attitude over the life of a campaign to detect trends."]}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:52.71444+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:52.71444+00'),
('2b98e361-25d2-46bf-9c6d-f7c151cc6166', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'Differentiate between Pre-testing and Post-testing.', 'Priority: Tier 1 — HIGH-VALUE COMPARISON', '(see comparison table in Section C) In brief: pre-testing evaluates an advertisement before release to refine and de-risk it, while post-testing evaluates it after release to measure actual impact and inform future campaigns. Both are essential, complementary stages of advertising research rather than substitutes for each other.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 15, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '825dd962-2813-41cf-a5f5-0a886af35bb5', '9038817a-ab59-4e79-81d5-acff95bfbdf8', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u4-c15-q-1", "text": "Differentiate between Pre-testing and Post-testing.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u4-c15-a-1", "text": "(see comparison table in Section C) In brief: pre-testing evaluates an advertisement before release to refine and de-risk it, while post-testing evaluates it after release to measure actual impact and inform future campaigns. Both are essential, complementary stages of advertising research rather than substitutes for each other.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:52.71444+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:52.71444+00'),
('5fab390a-90a8-46c2-8547-7160a85f2001', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'Differentiate between Concept Testing and Copy Testing.', 'Priority: Tier 2', '(see comparison table in Section C) Concept testing evaluates the core advertising idea at a very early, often unfinished stage (a sketch, statement, or storyboard) to see if the underlying idea resonates with the target audience before resources are committed to full development.

Copy testing evaluates the finished or near-finished advertisement (actual headline, visuals, body copy, layout) to check whether the execution communicates effectively, is understood correctly, and is likely to persuade — it can be done both before release (as a form of pre-testing) and after release (as post-testing).

Example: A juice brand first concept-tests three possible campaign ideas ("health," "taste," "convenience") with consumers to choose one direction, then copy-tests the fully designed print ad built on the chosen "health" concept before it goes to print.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 16, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '825dd962-2813-41cf-a5f5-0a886af35bb5', '9038817a-ab59-4e79-81d5-acff95bfbdf8', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u4-c16-q-1", "text": "Differentiate between Concept Testing and Copy Testing.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u4-c16-a-1", "text": "(see comparison table in Section C) Concept testing evaluates the core advertising idea at a very early, often unfinished stage (a sketch, statement, or storyboard) to see if the underlying idea resonates with the target audience before resources are committed to full development.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u4-c16-a-2", "text": "Copy testing evaluates the finished or near-finished advertisement (actual headline, visuals, body copy, layout) to check whether the execution communicates effectively, is understood correctly, and is likely to persuade — it can be done both before release (as a form of pre-testing) and after release (as post-testing).", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u4-c16-a-3", "text": "Example: A juice brand first concept-tests three possible campaign ideas (\"health,\" \"taste,\" \"convenience\") with consumers to choose one direction, then copy-tests the fully designed print ad built on the chosen \"health\" concept before it goes to print.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph", "italic": true}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:52.71444+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:52.71444+00'),
('55f3fe26-8583-4e19-a712-f78d263b7a68', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'Explain Rural Advertising. (see also Rural vs Urban comparison, Section C)', 'Priority: Tier 1', 'Meaning: Advertising strategies specifically designed to reach consumers in villages and semi-urban areas, accounting for their distinct language, media habits, income levels, and cultural context. Key characteristics: Heavy reliance on local-language communication; use of mass, low-cost reach media (radio, wall paintings, haats/melas, van/tableau campaigns, regional TV); simple, relatable messaging tied to local festivals and occasions.

How it is used: Brands use folk media (nukkad nataks/street plays), local cinema, regional television, and on-ground activation at village fairs and markets, alongside growing use of mobile and vernacular digital content.

Advantages/opportunities: Rural India represents a very large and growing consumer base; lower media clutter than urban markets; strong word-of-mouth influence.

Limitations/challenges: Lower and inconsistent internet/media infrastructure in some regions; diverse languages and dialects increase production complexity; literacy variance requires visual/audio-first communication.

Example: An FMCG brand promoting affordable detergent through local-language radio jingles and van campaigns in village markets.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 17, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '825dd962-2813-41cf-a5f5-0a886af35bb5', 'ebbc5f86-e065-4e29-b44c-04742060d7c0', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u4-c17-q-1", "text": "Explain Rural Advertising. (see also Rural vs Urban comparison, Section C)", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u4-c17-a-1", "text": "Meaning: Advertising strategies specifically designed to reach consumers in villages and semi-urban areas, accounting for their distinct language, media habits, income levels, and cultural context. Key characteristics: Heavy reliance on local-language communication; use of mass, low-cost reach media (radio, wall paintings, haats/melas, van/tableau campaigns, regional TV); simple, relatable messaging tied to local festivals and occasions.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u4-c17-a-2", "text": "How it is used: Brands use folk media (nukkad nataks/street plays), local cinema, regional television, and on-ground activation at village fairs and markets, alongside growing use of mobile and vernacular digital content.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u4-c17-a-3", "text": "Advantages/opportunities: Rural India represents a very large and growing consumer base; lower media clutter than urban markets; strong word-of-mouth influence.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u4-c17-a-4", "text": "Limitations/challenges: Lower and inconsistent internet/media infrastructure in some regions; diverse languages and dialects increase production complexity; literacy variance requires visual/audio-first communication.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u4-c17-a-5", "text": "Example: An FMCG brand promoting affordable detergent through local-language radio jingles and van campaigns in village markets.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph", "italic": true}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:52.71444+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:52.71444+00'),
('b3072bdc-f25a-4cae-9288-a58debd63cda', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'Explain Urban Advertising.', 'Priority: Tier 1', 'Meaning: Advertising strategies targeted at city and metropolitan consumers, who typically have higher income, greater media exposure, and faster-paced lifestyles. Key characteristics: Multi-media, often digital-first approach; sophisticated, aspirational messaging; emphasis on brand image, lifestyle appeal, and convenience.

How it is used: Television, digital and social media, outdoor/hoarding advertising, mall and metro branding, influencer collaborations, and app-based promotions dominate urban campaigns.

Advantages/opportunities: Better media and digital infrastructure enables precise targeting and quick feedback; higher disposable income supports premium positioning.

Limitations/challenges: High media clutter and ad-fatigue; higher cost of media and production; shorter attention spans require sharper creative.

Example: A premium smartphone brand running Instagram and YouTube influencer campaigns alongside metro-station branding in Mumbai and Bengaluru.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 18, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '825dd962-2813-41cf-a5f5-0a886af35bb5', 'ebbc5f86-e065-4e29-b44c-04742060d7c0', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u4-c18-q-1", "text": "Explain Urban Advertising.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u4-c18-a-1", "text": "Meaning: Advertising strategies targeted at city and metropolitan consumers, who typically have higher income, greater media exposure, and faster-paced lifestyles. Key characteristics: Multi-media, often digital-first approach; sophisticated, aspirational messaging; emphasis on brand image, lifestyle appeal, and convenience.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u4-c18-a-2", "text": "How it is used: Television, digital and social media, outdoor/hoarding advertising, mall and metro branding, influencer collaborations, and app-based promotions dominate urban campaigns.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u4-c18-a-3", "text": "Advantages/opportunities: Better media and digital infrastructure enables precise targeting and quick feedback; higher disposable income supports premium positioning.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u4-c18-a-4", "text": "Limitations/challenges: High media clutter and ad-fatigue; higher cost of media and production; shorter attention spans require sharper creative.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u4-c18-a-5", "text": "Example: A premium smartphone brand running Instagram and YouTube influencer campaigns alongside metro-station branding in Mumbai and Bengaluru.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph", "italic": true}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:52.71444+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:52.71444+00'),
('17b276e8-c063-4099-bede-c1584c518979', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'Explain Digital Advertising.', 'Priority: Tier 2', 'Meaning: Advertising delivered through internet-based and digital channels — search engines, social media, websites, apps, and connected devices. Key characteristics: Highly measurable, targetable, and interactive; formats include search ads, display banners, social media ads, video ads, and programmatic ad buying.

How it is used: Brands use platforms such as Google, Meta, and YouTube to run targeted campaigns based on demographics, interests, and behaviour, with real-time performance tracking.

Advantages/opportunities: Precise targeting; measurable ROI (clicks, conversions); cost flexibility suited to small and large budgets alike; rapid campaign adjustment.

Limitations/challenges: Ad-blocking and banner blindness; data-privacy concerns and regulation; intense competition for attention (clutter).

Example: An e-commerce brand running Google Search ads and retargeted display banners to users who viewed a product but did not purchase.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 19, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '825dd962-2813-41cf-a5f5-0a886af35bb5', 'ebbc5f86-e065-4e29-b44c-04742060d7c0', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u4-c19-q-1", "text": "Explain Digital Advertising.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u4-c19-a-1", "text": "Meaning: Advertising delivered through internet-based and digital channels — search engines, social media, websites, apps, and connected devices. Key characteristics: Highly measurable, targetable, and interactive; formats include search ads, display banners, social media ads, video ads, and programmatic ad buying.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u4-c19-a-2", "text": "How it is used: Brands use platforms such as Google, Meta, and YouTube to run targeted campaigns based on demographics, interests, and behaviour, with real-time performance tracking.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u4-c19-a-3", "text": "Advantages/opportunities: Precise targeting; measurable ROI (clicks, conversions); cost flexibility suited to small and large budgets alike; rapid campaign adjustment.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u4-c19-a-4", "text": "Limitations/challenges: Ad-blocking and banner blindness; data-privacy concerns and regulation; intense competition for attention (clutter).", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u4-c19-a-5", "text": "Example: An e-commerce brand running Google Search ads and retargeted display banners to users who viewed a product but did not purchase.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph", "italic": true}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:52.71444+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:52.71444+00'),
('d9c4980c-fb4c-403a-bf11-5c4bf7d0394e', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'Explain Content Marketing as an advertising trend.', 'Priority: Tier 3', 'Meaning: Creating and distributing valuable, relevant, and non-overtly-promotional content (articles, videos, blogs) to attract and engage a target audience, building brand affinity rather than pushing a direct sales message. Key characteristics: Educates, entertains, or informs rather than directly advertising; builds long-term trust and brand authority.

How it is used: Brands publish how-to videos, blogs, webinars, or branded entertainment relevant to their category.

Advantages/opportunities: Builds credibility and long-term customer relationships; supports SEO and organic reach; less intrusive than direct advertising.

Limitations/challenges: Results build slowly, requiring sustained investment; harder to directly attribute sales impact.

Example: A skincare brand publishing YouTube videos on general skin-care routines that subtly feature its products.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 20, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '825dd962-2813-41cf-a5f5-0a886af35bb5', 'ebbc5f86-e065-4e29-b44c-04742060d7c0', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u4-c20-q-1", "text": "Explain Content Marketing as an advertising trend.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u4-c20-a-1", "text": "Meaning: Creating and distributing valuable, relevant, and non-overtly-promotional content (articles, videos, blogs) to attract and engage a target audience, building brand affinity rather than pushing a direct sales message. Key characteristics: Educates, entertains, or informs rather than directly advertising; builds long-term trust and brand authority.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u4-c20-a-2", "text": "How it is used: Brands publish how-to videos, blogs, webinars, or branded entertainment relevant to their category.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u4-c20-a-3", "text": "Advantages/opportunities: Builds credibility and long-term customer relationships; supports SEO and organic reach; less intrusive than direct advertising.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u4-c20-a-4", "text": "Limitations/challenges: Results build slowly, requiring sustained investment; harder to directly attribute sales impact.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u4-c20-a-5", "text": "Example: A skincare brand publishing YouTube videos on general skin-care routines that subtly feature its products.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph", "italic": true}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:52.71444+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:52.71444+00'),
('32ea48a9-911d-4a68-b056-b954ba9d6b4e', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'Explain Advertorials.', 'Priority: Tier 3', 'Meaning: Paid advertisements designed and formatted to resemble editorial/news content in a newspaper, magazine, or website, blending promotional messaging with an informative, article-like style. Key characteristics: Must typically be labelled "advertisement" or "sponsored content" for transparency; blends persuasive and informational tone.

How it is used: Brands publish advertorials in newspapers/magazines or as "sponsored articles" on websites to lend credibility associated with editorial content.

Advantages/opportunities: Higher perceived credibility than a standard ad; can explain complex products/benefits in more depth.

Limitations/challenges: Risk of reader distrust if the promotional intent is not clearly disclosed; regulatory/ethical scrutiny around disguised advertising.

Example: A wellness brand publishing a "sponsored" health-tips article in a lifestyle magazine that recommends its supplement.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 21, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '825dd962-2813-41cf-a5f5-0a886af35bb5', 'ebbc5f86-e065-4e29-b44c-04742060d7c0', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u4-c21-q-1", "text": "Explain Advertorials.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u4-c21-a-1", "text": "Meaning: Paid advertisements designed and formatted to resemble editorial/news content in a newspaper, magazine, or website, blending promotional messaging with an informative, article-like style. Key characteristics: Must typically be labelled \"advertisement\" or \"sponsored content\" for transparency; blends persuasive and informational tone.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u4-c21-a-2", "text": "How it is used: Brands publish advertorials in newspapers/magazines or as \"sponsored articles\" on websites to lend credibility associated with editorial content.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u4-c21-a-3", "text": "Advantages/opportunities: Higher perceived credibility than a standard ad; can explain complex products/benefits in more depth.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u4-c21-a-4", "text": "Limitations/challenges: Risk of reader distrust if the promotional intent is not clearly disclosed; regulatory/ethical scrutiny around disguised advertising.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u4-c21-a-5", "text": "Example: A wellness brand publishing a \"sponsored\" health-tips article in a lifestyle magazine that recommends its supplement.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph", "italic": true}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:52.71444+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:52.71444+00'),
('50d7261e-06fd-4c35-92ad-e42ce68e2b34', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'Explain Retail Advertising.', 'Priority: Tier 3', 'Meaning: Advertising undertaken by retailers (or for specific retail outlets) to draw footfall and promote products, offers, or the store itself, rather than a manufacturer promoting a brand generally. Key characteristics: Localised, often price/offer-driven, time-bound (sales, festive offers), and store-specific.

How it is used: In-store displays, local newspaper inserts, retailer apps/SMS alerts, point-of-sale promotions, and local social media ads.

Advantages/opportunities: Directly drives footfall and immediate purchase; can be highly targeted to a local catchment area.

Limitations/challenges: Short-term, offer-led focus can undercut long-term brand building; heavy discounting can erode margins.

Example: A supermarket chain advertising a weekend "Big Billion Days"-style discount through local flyers and app notifications.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 22, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '825dd962-2813-41cf-a5f5-0a886af35bb5', 'ebbc5f86-e065-4e29-b44c-04742060d7c0', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u4-c22-q-1", "text": "Explain Retail Advertising.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u4-c22-a-1", "text": "Meaning: Advertising undertaken by retailers (or for specific retail outlets) to draw footfall and promote products, offers, or the store itself, rather than a manufacturer promoting a brand generally. Key characteristics: Localised, often price/offer-driven, time-bound (sales, festive offers), and store-specific.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u4-c22-a-2", "text": "How it is used: In-store displays, local newspaper inserts, retailer apps/SMS alerts, point-of-sale promotions, and local social media ads.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u4-c22-a-3", "text": "Advantages/opportunities: Directly drives footfall and immediate purchase; can be highly targeted to a local catchment area.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u4-c22-a-4", "text": "Limitations/challenges: Short-term, offer-led focus can undercut long-term brand building; heavy discounting can erode margins.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u4-c22-a-5", "text": "Example: A supermarket chain advertising a weekend \"Big Billion Days\"-style discount through local flyers and app notifications.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph", "italic": true}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:52.71444+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:52.71444+00'),
('4334a805-9fd9-4507-8928-ef98ab130efc', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'Explain Lifestyle Advertising.', 'Priority: Tier 3', 'Meaning: Advertising that sells a product by associating it with a desirable lifestyle, identity, or set of values, rather than focusing purely on product features. Key characteristics: Emotion and aspiration-led; positions the brand as part of a way of living (e.g., adventure, luxury, wellness, sustainability).

How it is used: Brands feature consumers living the associated lifestyle in ads (e.g., fitness, travel, minimalism) rather than showcasing product specifications.

Advantages/opportunities: Builds strong emotional brand connection and loyalty; differentiates in categories where functional features are similar.

Limitations/challenges: Can feel disconnected from the product if overdone; risk of alienating audiences who don''t identify with the depicted lifestyle.

Example: An athletic wear brand''s campaign showing everyday people pursuing fitness goals, positioning the brand around determination and an active lifestyle.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 23, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '825dd962-2813-41cf-a5f5-0a886af35bb5', 'ebbc5f86-e065-4e29-b44c-04742060d7c0', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u4-c23-q-1", "text": "Explain Lifestyle Advertising.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u4-c23-a-1", "text": "Meaning: Advertising that sells a product by associating it with a desirable lifestyle, identity, or set of values, rather than focusing purely on product features. Key characteristics: Emotion and aspiration-led; positions the brand as part of a way of living (e.g., adventure, luxury, wellness, sustainability).", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u4-c23-a-2", "text": "How it is used: Brands feature consumers living the associated lifestyle in ads (e.g., fitness, travel, minimalism) rather than showcasing product specifications.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u4-c23-a-3", "text": "Advantages/opportunities: Builds strong emotional brand connection and loyalty; differentiates in categories where functional features are similar.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u4-c23-a-4", "text": "Limitations/challenges: Can feel disconnected from the product if overdone; risk of alienating audiences who don''t identify with the depicted lifestyle.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u4-c23-a-5", "text": "Example: An athletic wear brand''s campaign showing everyday people pursuing fitness goals, positioning the brand around determination and an active lifestyle.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph", "italic": true}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:52.71444+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:52.71444+00'),
('9e486b7b-38aa-4433-86c2-81989a40befb', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'Explain Ambush Advertising (Ambush Marketing).', 'Priority: Tier 3', 'Meaning: A strategy where a brand associates itself with a major event (such as a sporting tournament) without being an official sponsor, capturing some of the attention and association benefits of the event at lower cost. Key characteristics: Opportunistic, often clever/creative; deliberately positioned close to (but not officially tied to) the event.

How it is used: Brands run event-themed campaigns, social media activity, or on-ground activations timed with the event, without paying official sponsorship fees.

Advantages/opportunities: Lower cost than official sponsorship; can generate significant buzz through creativity.

Limitations/challenges: Legal and ethical grey areas; can invite disputes from official sponsors or event organisers; risk of appearing opportunistic.

Example: A soft-drink brand running a cricket-themed social media campaign during a major tournament without being an official sponsor.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 24, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '825dd962-2813-41cf-a5f5-0a886af35bb5', 'ebbc5f86-e065-4e29-b44c-04742060d7c0', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u4-c24-q-1", "text": "Explain Ambush Advertising (Ambush Marketing).", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u4-c24-a-1", "text": "Meaning: A strategy where a brand associates itself with a major event (such as a sporting tournament) without being an official sponsor, capturing some of the attention and association benefits of the event at lower cost. Key characteristics: Opportunistic, often clever/creative; deliberately positioned close to (but not officially tied to) the event.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u4-c24-a-2", "text": "How it is used: Brands run event-themed campaigns, social media activity, or on-ground activations timed with the event, without paying official sponsorship fees.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u4-c24-a-3", "text": "Advantages/opportunities: Lower cost than official sponsorship; can generate significant buzz through creativity.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u4-c24-a-4", "text": "Limitations/challenges: Legal and ethical grey areas; can invite disputes from official sponsors or event organisers; risk of appearing opportunistic.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u4-c24-a-5", "text": "Example: A soft-drink brand running a cricket-themed social media campaign during a major tournament without being an official sponsor.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph", "italic": true}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:52.71444+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:52.71444+00'),
('2a792008-813f-41eb-94ee-4b80db9f9633', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'Explain Influencer Marketing.', 'Priority: Tier 1', 'Meaning: Advertising that leverages individuals with an established, engaged following (social media influencers, celebrities, subject-matter experts) to promote a brand''s products or message to their audience. Key characteristics: Relies on the influencer''s credibility and relatability; ranges from mega-celebrities to niche "micro-influencers."

How it is used: Brands collaborate with influencers for sponsored posts, reviews, unboxing videos, and live demonstrations across platforms like Instagram and YouTube.

Advantages/opportunities: Access to a ready, engaged, and often niche audience; feels more authentic/relatable than traditional advertising; measurable engagement.

Limitations/challenges: Risk of misalignment between influencer image and brand; disclosure/transparency requirements; results can be hard to sustain long-term.

Example: A skincare brand sending products to beauty influencers for honest review videos on YouTube and Instagram Reels.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 25, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '825dd962-2813-41cf-a5f5-0a886af35bb5', 'ebbc5f86-e065-4e29-b44c-04742060d7c0', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u4-c25-q-1", "text": "Explain Influencer Marketing.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u4-c25-a-1", "text": "Meaning: Advertising that leverages individuals with an established, engaged following (social media influencers, celebrities, subject-matter experts) to promote a brand''s products or message to their audience. Key characteristics: Relies on the influencer''s credibility and relatability; ranges from mega-celebrities to niche \"micro-influencers.\"", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u4-c25-a-2", "text": "How it is used: Brands collaborate with influencers for sponsored posts, reviews, unboxing videos, and live demonstrations across platforms like Instagram and YouTube.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u4-c25-a-3", "text": "Advantages/opportunities: Access to a ready, engaged, and often niche audience; feels more authentic/relatable than traditional advertising; measurable engagement.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u4-c25-a-4", "text": "Limitations/challenges: Risk of misalignment between influencer image and brand; disclosure/transparency requirements; results can be hard to sustain long-term.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u4-c25-a-5", "text": "Example: A skincare brand sending products to beauty influencers for honest review videos on YouTube and Instagram Reels.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph", "italic": true}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:52.71444+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:52.71444+00'),
('179b5649-4b31-413a-b661-03b475d4311f', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'Explain AI-enabled Advertising.', 'Priority: Tier 2', 'Meaning: The use of artificial intelligence tools and techniques — data analysis, machine learning, generative AI — to plan, create, personalise, and optimise advertising campaigns. Key characteristics: Automates and improves tasks like audience targeting, ad creative generation, bidding, and performance prediction.

How it is used in advertising: AI powers programmatic media buying (automated ad placement bidding in real time), predictive analytics for audience targeting, chatbots for customer engagement, and generative tools that assist in producing ad copy or visuals.

Advantages/opportunities: Greater efficiency and speed in campaign execution; more precise targeting and personalisation at scale; data-driven optimisation of spend.

Limitations/challenges: Data-privacy and ethical concerns; risk of losing the human/emotional touch in creative; need for skilled talent to manage AI tools responsibly.

Example: A brand using an AI-driven programmatic platform to automatically place and optimise digital display ads across thousands of websites in real time.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 26, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '825dd962-2813-41cf-a5f5-0a886af35bb5', 'ebbc5f86-e065-4e29-b44c-04742060d7c0', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u4-c26-q-1", "text": "Explain AI-enabled Advertising.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u4-c26-a-1", "text": "Meaning: The use of artificial intelligence tools and techniques — data analysis, machine learning, generative AI — to plan, create, personalise, and optimise advertising campaigns. Key characteristics: Automates and improves tasks like audience targeting, ad creative generation, bidding, and performance prediction.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u4-c26-a-2", "text": "How it is used in advertising: AI powers programmatic media buying (automated ad placement bidding in real time), predictive analytics for audience targeting, chatbots for customer engagement, and generative tools that assist in producing ad copy or visuals.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u4-c26-a-3", "text": "Advantages/opportunities: Greater efficiency and speed in campaign execution; more precise targeting and personalisation at scale; data-driven optimisation of spend.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u4-c26-a-4", "text": "Limitations/challenges: Data-privacy and ethical concerns; risk of losing the human/emotional touch in creative; need for skilled talent to manage AI tools responsibly.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u4-c26-a-5", "text": "Example: A brand using an AI-driven programmatic platform to automatically place and optimise digital display ads across thousands of websites in real time.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph", "italic": true}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:52.71444+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:52.71444+00'),
('10b442da-40fe-4c14-a52a-0eb058b43396', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'Explain Personalised Advertising.', 'Priority: Tier 2', 'Meaning: Advertising tailored to an individual consumer''s preferences, past behaviour, or demographic profile, rather than a single generic message sent to everyone. How personalisation works conceptually: Data on a consumer''s browsing history, purchase behaviour, or stated preferences is used to customise the ad''s message, product recommendation, or offer shown to that specific individual.

Benefits: Higher relevance leads to better engagement and conversion; improves customer experience by reducing irrelevant messaging; strengthens brand-consumer relationships.

Challenges: Requires robust, ethically-sourced data; risk of appearing "intrusive" if personalisation feels excessive; data-privacy regulations restrict what data can be used.

Privacy/ethical considerations: Marketers must ensure transparent data collection, consent, and compliance with data-protection norms; over-personalisation can create discomfort ("how did they know that?").

Example: An e-commerce app showing a shopper product recommendations based specifically on items they recently viewed or purchased.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 27, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '825dd962-2813-41cf-a5f5-0a886af35bb5', 'ebbc5f86-e065-4e29-b44c-04742060d7c0', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u4-c27-q-1", "text": "Explain Personalised Advertising.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u4-c27-a-1", "text": "Meaning: Advertising tailored to an individual consumer''s preferences, past behaviour, or demographic profile, rather than a single generic message sent to everyone. How personalisation works conceptually: Data on a consumer''s browsing history, purchase behaviour, or stated preferences is used to customise the ad''s message, product recommendation, or offer shown to that specific individual.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u4-c27-a-2", "text": "Benefits: Higher relevance leads to better engagement and conversion; improves customer experience by reducing irrelevant messaging; strengthens brand-consumer relationships.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u4-c27-a-3", "text": "Challenges: Requires robust, ethically-sourced data; risk of appearing \"intrusive\" if personalisation feels excessive; data-privacy regulations restrict what data can be used.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u4-c27-a-4", "text": "Privacy/ethical considerations: Marketers must ensure transparent data collection, consent, and compliance with data-protection norms; over-personalisation can create discomfort (\"how did they know that?\").", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u4-c27-a-5", "text": "Example: An e-commerce app showing a shopper product recommendations based specifically on items they recently viewed or purchased.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph", "italic": true}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:52.71444+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:52.71444+00'),
('61c0fe10-8cb0-4c01-a854-d2077352f195', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'Explain Omnichannel Advertising.', 'Priority: Tier 2', 'Meaning: An advertising and marketing approach that delivers a consistent, integrated brand message and experience across all channels a customer might use — online and offline — rather than treating each channel separately. Core idea: The customer, not the channel, is at the centre; messaging, offers, and experience remain consistent whether the consumer encounters the brand on social media, in-store, via email, or on a mobile app.

Difference from single-channel approach: A single-channel (or even multi-channel) approach runs campaigns independently per platform; omnichannel deliberately connects these touchpoints so the customer journey feels seamless (e.g., an item added to an online cart is visible and purchasable in-store).

Customer journey/application: A consumer might discover a product via social media, research it on the website, receive a personalised email reminder, and finally purchase in-store — all recognised as one continuous journey by the brand''s systems.

Advantages: Improves customer experience and loyalty; increases the chances of conversion by meeting the customer wherever they are.

Example: A fashion retailer letting customers browse online, receive personalised app notifications about items on sale, and pick up or return the same item in a physical store.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 28, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '825dd962-2813-41cf-a5f5-0a886af35bb5', 'ebbc5f86-e065-4e29-b44c-04742060d7c0', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u4-c28-q-1", "text": "Explain Omnichannel Advertising.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u4-c28-a-1", "text": "Meaning: An advertising and marketing approach that delivers a consistent, integrated brand message and experience across all channels a customer might use — online and offline — rather than treating each channel separately. Core idea: The customer, not the channel, is at the centre; messaging, offers, and experience remain consistent whether the consumer encounters the brand on social media, in-store, via email, or on a mobile app.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u4-c28-a-2", "text": "Difference from single-channel approach: A single-channel (or even multi-channel) approach runs campaigns independently per platform; omnichannel deliberately connects these touchpoints so the customer journey feels seamless (e.g., an item added to an online cart is visible and purchasable in-store).", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u4-c28-a-3", "text": "Customer journey/application: A consumer might discover a product via social media, research it on the website, receive a personalised email reminder, and finally purchase in-store — all recognised as one continuous journey by the brand''s systems.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u4-c28-a-4", "text": "Advantages: Improves customer experience and loyalty; increases the chances of conversion by meeting the customer wherever they are.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u4-c28-a-5", "text": "Example: A fashion retailer letting customers browse online, receive personalised app notifications about items on sale, and pick up or return the same item in a physical store.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph", "italic": true}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:52.71444+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:52.71444+00'),
('35f5a846-7ca7-4496-ae91-e58389d0b386', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'What career options are available in the media side of the advertising industry?', 'Priority: Tier 2', 'Media Planner: Decides which media (TV, print, digital, outdoor) will best reach the target audience within budget.
Media Buyer: Negotiates and purchases advertising space/time from media owners.
Digital Media Specialist: Manages paid digital campaigns (search, social, programmatic) and analyses their performance.
Broadcast/Print Sales Executive: Works on the media-owner side, selling ad space/airtime to advertisers and agencies.
Media Research Analyst: Studies audience ratings, readership, and viewership data to guide planning decisions.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 29, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '825dd962-2813-41cf-a5f5-0a886af35bb5', '472fd3d7-772f-4d37-88af-7eb5ee71908b', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u4-c29-q-1", "text": "What career options are available in the media side of the advertising industry?", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u4-c29-a-1", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Media Planner: Decides which media (TV, print, digital, outdoor) will best reach the target audience within budget.", "Media Buyer: Negotiates and purchases advertising space/time from media owners.", "Digital Media Specialist: Manages paid digital campaigns (search, social, programmatic) and analyses their performance.", "Broadcast/Print Sales Executive: Works on the media-owner side, selling ad space/airtime to advertisers and agencies.", "Media Research Analyst: Studies audience ratings, readership, and viewership data to guide planning decisions."]}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:52.71444+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:52.71444+00'),
('432b569f-22bf-4af5-86c8-9c0007a221a6', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'What are "supporting firms" in the advertising industry, and what roles do they play?', 'Priority: Tier 2', 'Supporting firms are specialised organisations that provide services agencies and advertisers rely on but do not always perform in-house:

Market/Advertising Research Agencies: Conduct pre-testing, post-testing, and consumer research.
Production Houses: Handle filming, animation, and post-production of TV/digital advertisements.
Printing and Outdoor Media Vendors: Produce print materials, hoardings, and point-of-sale material.
Public Relations (PR) Firms: Manage brand reputation and media relations alongside advertising efforts.
Talent/Casting Agencies: Source models, actors, and voice-over artists for campaigns.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 30, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '825dd962-2813-41cf-a5f5-0a886af35bb5', '472fd3d7-772f-4d37-88af-7eb5ee71908b', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u4-c30-q-1", "text": "What are \"supporting firms\" in the advertising industry, and what roles do they play?", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u4-c30-a-1", "text": "Supporting firms are specialised organisations that provide services agencies and advertisers rely on but do not always perform in-house:", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u4-c30-a-2", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Market/Advertising Research Agencies: Conduct pre-testing, post-testing, and consumer research.", "Production Houses: Handle filming, animation, and post-production of TV/digital advertisements.", "Printing and Outdoor Media Vendors: Produce print materials, hoardings, and point-of-sale material.", "Public Relations (PR) Firms: Manage brand reputation and media relations alongside advertising efforts.", "Talent/Casting Agencies: Source models, actors, and voice-over artists for campaigns."]}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:52.71444+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:52.71444+00'),
('d71f7088-a60d-4dd6-a322-481b6b5c5052', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'Discuss freelancing options available in the advertising industry.', 'Priority: Tier 2', 'Advertising offers strong scope for independent, freelance careers because much of the work is project- and creative-based:

Freelance Copywriter: Writes ad copy, taglines, and scripts for multiple clients/agencies.
Freelance Graphic/Visual Designer: Creates print, digital, and packaging design assets.
Freelance Social Media Consultant: Manages content calendars and campaigns for small/medium brands.
Freelance Photographer/Videographer: Shoots product, lifestyle, or campaign visuals.
Freelance Strategist/Consultant: Advises smaller businesses on brand positioning and campaign planning without a full-time agency retainer.

Freelancing offers flexibility and diverse client exposure but requires strong self-marketing, portfolio-building, and client-management skills.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 31, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '825dd962-2813-41cf-a5f5-0a886af35bb5', '472fd3d7-772f-4d37-88af-7eb5ee71908b', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u4-c31-q-1", "text": "Discuss freelancing options available in the advertising industry.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u4-c31-a-1", "text": "Advertising offers strong scope for independent, freelance careers because much of the work is project- and creative-based:", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u4-c31-a-2", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Freelance Copywriter: Writes ad copy, taglines, and scripts for multiple clients/agencies.", "Freelance Graphic/Visual Designer: Creates print, digital, and packaging design assets.", "Freelance Social Media Consultant: Manages content calendars and campaigns for small/medium brands.", "Freelance Photographer/Videographer: Shoots product, lifestyle, or campaign visuals.", "Freelance Strategist/Consultant: Advises smaller businesses on brand positioning and campaign planning without a full-time agency retainer."]}, {"id": "adv-u4-c31-a-3", "text": "Freelancing offers flexibility and diverse client exposure but requires strong self-marketing, portfolio-building, and client-management skills.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:52.71444+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:52.71444+00'),
('b526b6a7-5574-4ab9-87ba-cbff8b34c839', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'Who is an Advertising Account Executive? Explain their role.', 'Priority: Tier 1', 'Meaning: The Account Executive (AE) is the primary link between the advertising agency and the client, responsible for managing the client relationship and ensuring the agency''s work meets the client''s brief, timeline, and budget.

Role and responsibilities:

Understanding the client''s brief, brand, and business objectives thoroughly.
Communicating the brief clearly to internal agency teams (creative, media, planning).
Coordinating timelines and ensuring campaigns are delivered on schedule and within budget.
Presenting creative work and campaign performance to the client.
Managing day-to-day client queries, feedback, and revisions.
Building and sustaining a long-term, trust-based client-agency relationship.

Skills required: Strong communication and interpersonal skills, organisation and time management, basic understanding of marketing/creative processes, client-servicing and negotiation ability.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 32, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '825dd962-2813-41cf-a5f5-0a886af35bb5', '472fd3d7-772f-4d37-88af-7eb5ee71908b', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u4-c32-q-1", "text": "Who is an Advertising Account Executive? Explain their role.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u4-c32-a-1", "text": "Meaning: The Account Executive (AE) is the primary link between the advertising agency and the client, responsible for managing the client relationship and ensuring the agency''s work meets the client''s brief, timeline, and budget.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u4-c32-a-2", "bold": true, "text": "Role and responsibilities:", "type": "text", "style": "heading"}, {"id": "adv-u4-c32-a-3", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Understanding the client''s brief, brand, and business objectives thoroughly.", "Communicating the brief clearly to internal agency teams (creative, media, planning).", "Coordinating timelines and ensuring campaigns are delivered on schedule and within budget.", "Presenting creative work and campaign performance to the client.", "Managing day-to-day client queries, feedback, and revisions.", "Building and sustaining a long-term, trust-based client-agency relationship."]}, {"id": "adv-u4-c32-a-4", "text": "Skills required: Strong communication and interpersonal skills, organisation and time management, basic understanding of marketing/creative processes, client-servicing and negotiation ability.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:52.71444+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:52.71444+00'),
('a48c9dfd-6c5d-4877-860a-4b33d180979d', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'As a student, explain a possible career path to become an Advertising Account Executive. (Directly asked in 2025, Q4c)', 'Priority: Tier 1 — DEDICATED EXAM ANSWER', 'This is one possible, logical progression — not the only route:

Educational foundation: Pursue a degree with a marketing/advertising/mass communication orientation (e.g., B.M.S., B.Com with marketing specialisation, or a mass communication degree), building core knowledge of marketing, consumer behaviour, and advertising principles.
Build relevant skills and exposure: Develop communication, presentation, and basic marketing-analytics skills; undertake internships with advertising agencies, marketing departments, or media houses to gain hands-on exposure.
Entry-level agency role: Join an agency as an Account Coordinator / Junior Account Executive / Client Servicing Trainee, assisting a senior Account Executive with coordination, documentation, and client communication.
Progress to Account Executive: After gaining experience in coordinating projects and understanding client needs, take on independent client-handling responsibility as a full Account Executive.
Career growth: With experience, progress to Senior Account Executive → Account Manager → Account Director / Client Servicing Head, taking on larger client portfolios, strategic responsibility, and team leadership.

Throughout this path, continuous learning (certifications in digital marketing/advertising, staying current with industry trends) and strong relationship-building skills accelerate growth.

Example pathway: A B.M.S. graduate interns at a mid-sized agency during college, joins as an Account Coordinator after graduation, is promoted to Account Executive within 1–2 years, and eventually leads client servicing for a major account as an Account Manager.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 33, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '825dd962-2813-41cf-a5f5-0a886af35bb5', '472fd3d7-772f-4d37-88af-7eb5ee71908b', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u4-c33-q-1", "text": "As a student, explain a possible career path to become an Advertising Account Executive. (Directly asked in 2025, Q4c)", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u4-c33-a-1", "text": "This is one possible, logical progression — not the only route:", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u4-c33-a-2", "type": "numberList", "items": ["Educational foundation: Pursue a degree with a marketing/advertising/mass communication orientation (e.g., B.M.S., B.Com with marketing specialisation, or a mass communication degree), building core knowledge of marketing, consumer behaviour, and advertising principles.", "Build relevant skills and exposure: Develop communication, presentation, and basic marketing-analytics skills; undertake internships with advertising agencies, marketing departments, or media houses to gain hands-on exposure.", "Entry-level agency role: Join an agency as an Account Coordinator / Junior Account Executive / Client Servicing Trainee, assisting a senior Account Executive with coordination, documentation, and client communication.", "Progress to Account Executive: After gaining experience in coordinating projects and understanding client needs, take on independent client-handling responsibility as a full Account Executive.", "Career growth: With experience, progress to Senior Account Executive → Account Manager → Account Director / Client Servicing Head, taking on larger client portfolios, strategic responsibility, and team leadership."]}, {"id": "adv-u4-c33-a-3", "text": "Throughout this path, continuous learning (certifications in digital marketing/advertising, staying current with industry trends) and strong relationship-building skills accelerate growth.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u4-c33-a-4", "text": "Example pathway: A B.M.S. graduate interns at a mid-sized agency during college, joins as an Account Coordinator after graduation, is promoted to Account Executive within 1–2 years, and eventually leads client servicing for a major account as an Account Manager.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph", "italic": true}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:52.71444+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:52.71444+00'),
('262d5e52-278b-447c-80a1-d373bd0db050', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'Explain the concept of the "Agency Family Tree" in advertising.', 'Priority: Tier 3', 'Meaning: In the advertising industry, large global "holding companies" (such as WPP, Omnicom, Publicis, IPG, and Dentsu) own multiple, distinct advertising and marketing agencies (called "agency networks" or "sister agencies") that operate under their own separate brand names but share ownership, resources, and sometimes back-end infrastructure. This layered ownership structure is informally described as the agency''s "family tree." How it works: A single holding company may own several full-service creative agencies, media-buying agencies, digital/data specialists, and PR firms, allowing it to offer clients a range of specialised services while avoiding direct conflicts of interest between competing-brand clients (each handled by a different "family member" agency).

Note: This is an explanatory interpretation of a widely-used industry concept — the syllabus does not prescribe one official diagram, so a student should describe the concept clearly with a real example rather than treating any single chart as the fixed "correct" answer.

Example: WPP (holding company) owns Ogilvy, Wunderman Thompson (formed from the earlier JWT), Grey, and GroupM (media buying) as separate agencies within its "family."', NULL, NULL, NULL, 34, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '825dd962-2813-41cf-a5f5-0a886af35bb5', '472fd3d7-772f-4d37-88af-7eb5ee71908b', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u4-c34-q-1", "text": "Explain the concept of the \"Agency Family Tree\" in advertising.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u4-c34-a-1", "text": "Meaning: In the advertising industry, large global \"holding companies\" (such as WPP, Omnicom, Publicis, IPG, and Dentsu) own multiple, distinct advertising and marketing agencies (called \"agency networks\" or \"sister agencies\") that operate under their own separate brand names but share ownership, resources, and sometimes back-end infrastructure. This layered ownership structure is informally described as the agency''s \"family tree.\" How it works: A single holding company may own several full-service creative agencies, media-buying agencies, digital/data specialists, and PR firms, allowing it to offer clients a range of specialised services while avoiding direct conflicts of interest between competing-brand clients (each handled by a different \"family member\" agency).", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "adv-u4-c34-a-2", "text": "Note: This is an explanatory interpretation of a widely-used industry concept — the syllabus does not prescribe one official diagram, so a student should describe the concept clearly with a real example rather than treating any single chart as the fixed \"correct\" answer.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph", "italic": true}, {"id": "adv-u4-c34-a-3", "text": "Example: WPP (holding company) owns Ogilvy, Wunderman Thompson (formed from the earlier JWT), Grey, and GroupM (media buying) as separate agencies within its \"family.\"", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph", "italic": true}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:52.71444+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:52.71444+00'),
('49563bbe-4420-4fa9-b49d-ac3bbe6f4c67', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'Name a few top advertising agencies (India-relevant) and their notable campaigns.', 'Priority: Tier 4', '(Industry examples — current as of available sources; agency ownership and notable work can change over time, so treat this as illustrative rather than fixed.)

Ogilvy India — Founded 1948 by David Ogilvy; part of the WPP Group; among India''s most awarded agencies. Notable campaigns: the Vodafone ZooZoos series and the long-running Fevicol advertisement series.
MullenLowe Lintas Group — One of India''s oldest agencies (originated as Lintas India, 1969), part of IPG. Notable campaigns: Surf Excel''s "Daag Acche Hain" and Asian Paints'' "Har Ghar Kuch Kehta Hai."
FCB Ulka — Incorporated in 1961 by Bal Mundkur in Mumbai. Notable campaigns: advertising for Amul''s "Women Power" campaign and Pediasure.
DDB Mudra Group — A full-funnel agency with clients spanning FMCG, real estate, and technology, including Hindustan Unilever.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 35, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '825dd962-2813-41cf-a5f5-0a886af35bb5', '472fd3d7-772f-4d37-88af-7eb5ee71908b', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u4-c35-q-1", "text": "Name a few top advertising agencies (India-relevant) and their notable campaigns.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u4-c35-a-1", "text": "(Industry examples — current as of available sources; agency ownership and notable work can change over time, so treat this as illustrative rather than fixed.)", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph", "italic": true}, {"id": "adv-u4-c35-a-2", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Ogilvy India — Founded 1948 by David Ogilvy; part of the WPP Group; among India''s most awarded agencies. Notable campaigns: the Vodafone ZooZoos series and the long-running Fevicol advertisement series.", "MullenLowe Lintas Group — One of India''s oldest agencies (originated as Lintas India, 1969), part of IPG. Notable campaigns: Surf Excel''s \"Daag Acche Hain\" and Asian Paints'' \"Har Ghar Kuch Kehta Hai.\"", "FCB Ulka — Incorporated in 1961 by Bal Mundkur in Mumbai. Notable campaigns: advertising for Amul''s \"Women Power\" campaign and Pediasure.", "DDB Mudra Group — A full-funnel agency with clients spanning FMCG, real estate, and technology, including Hindustan Unilever."]}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:52.71444+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:52.71444+00'),
('6da31c7e-8dee-4635-97f0-7a35c894cf08', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'A company is launching an advertisement and wants to test it before release. Which research stage should it use, and why?', 'Priority: Tier 1', 'The company should use pre-testing (developmental research). Since the advertisement has not yet been released, the objective is to check message clarity, audience comprehension, and likely persuasive impact before committing to full production and media spend. Suitable methods include concept testing (to validate the core idea) and consumer jury or focus group testing (to compare and refine creative executions). This minimizes the risk of releasing a weak or misunderstood advertisement.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 36, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '825dd962-2813-41cf-a5f5-0a886af35bb5', '054a2ab5-1f78-4bea-b5b8-cb6c32b2114d', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u4-c36-q-1", "text": "A company is launching an advertisement and wants to test it before release. Which research stage should it use, and why?", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u4-c36-a-1", "text": "The company should use pre-testing (developmental research). Since the advertisement has not yet been released, the objective is to check message clarity, audience comprehension, and likely persuasive impact before committing to full production and media spend. Suitable methods include concept testing (to validate the core idea) and consumer jury or focus group testing (to compare and refine creative executions). This minimizes the risk of releasing a weak or misunderstood advertisement.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:52.71444+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:52.71444+00'),
('b10ec795-aa96-403e-9e26-ad86d35a6346', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'A company wants to determine whether its final advertising copy communicates effectively. Explain the relevant testing approach.', 'Priority: Tier 1', 'This calls for copy testing — evaluating the finished (or near-finished) advertisement''s actual execution, since the question specifically concerns the "final" copy rather than the underlying idea. Copy testing checks whether the headline, visuals, and body copy together communicate the intended message clearly, are correctly understood, and are likely to persuade the target audience. It can be conducted just before release (as pre-testing, using methods like consumer jury or theatre tests) or shortly after release (as post-testing, using recall or recognition tests) to confirm actual communication effectiveness.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 37, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '825dd962-2813-41cf-a5f5-0a886af35bb5', '054a2ab5-1f78-4bea-b5b8-cb6c32b2114d', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u4-c37-q-1", "text": "A company wants to determine whether its final advertising copy communicates effectively. Explain the relevant testing approach.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u4-c37-a-1", "text": "This calls for copy testing — evaluating the finished (or near-finished) advertisement''s actual execution, since the question specifically concerns the \"final\" copy rather than the underlying idea. Copy testing checks whether the headline, visuals, and body copy together communicate the intended message clearly, are correctly understood, and are likely to persuade the target audience. It can be conducted just before release (as pre-testing, using methods like consumer jury or theatre tests) or shortly after release (as post-testing, using recall or recognition tests) to confirm actual communication effectiveness.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:52.71444+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:52.71444+00'),
('f0f999df-61c4-4498-b06f-d409c6fce49f', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'A brand wants to advertise differently in rural and urban markets. Explain the strategic differences.', 'Priority: Tier 1', 'The brand should adapt its strategy across several dimensions (see the full Rural vs Urban comparison table in Section C): use simpler, local-language messaging and mass-reach, low-cost media (radio, wall paintings, local melas/fairs) for rural markets, versus sophisticated, aspirational messaging across digital, social, and outdoor media for urban markets. Rural strategy should account for lower and more variable media/internet infrastructure and stronger reliance on community and word-of-mouth influence, while urban strategy can leverage precise digital targeting, higher media clutter awareness, and lifestyle-driven positioning.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 38, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '825dd962-2813-41cf-a5f5-0a886af35bb5', '054a2ab5-1f78-4bea-b5b8-cb6c32b2114d', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u4-c38-q-1", "text": "A brand wants to advertise differently in rural and urban markets. Explain the strategic differences.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u4-c38-a-1", "text": "The brand should adapt its strategy across several dimensions (see the full Rural vs Urban comparison table in Section C): use simpler, local-language messaging and mass-reach, low-cost media (radio, wall paintings, local melas/fairs) for rural markets, versus sophisticated, aspirational messaging across digital, social, and outdoor media for urban markets. Rural strategy should account for lower and more variable media/internet infrastructure and stronger reliance on community and word-of-mouth influence, while urban strategy can leverage precise digital targeting, higher media clutter awareness, and lifestyle-driven positioning.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:52.71444+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:52.71444+00'),
('db656da9-11d1-47f7-9a6d-151d1578d4f3', '4942b243-3eda-4100-9200-942a29e98d11', 'flashcard', 'Book Value Method: variations and quick numerical patterns.', 'Priority: Tier 2 | PYQ Connection: Variations of 2025 Q4.3 | PYQ Connection: Variations of 2025 Q4.3', 'Variation A: fictitious assets and accumulated loss.

Equity share capital ₹10,00,000 (₹10 shares), reserves ₹3,00,000, debit balance in P&L ₹1,00,000, preliminary expenses not written off ₹50,000.

Net worth = 10,00,000 + 3,00,000 − 1,00,000 − 50,000 = ₹11,50,000.
Shares = 1,00,000 → book value per share = ₹11.50.

Variation B: only totals are given.

Total assets ₹50,00,000 (including fictitious assets ₹2,00,000); outside liabilities ₹18,00,000; preference capital ₹6,00,000; equity shares 2,00,000.

Net worth = 50,00,000 − 2,00,000 − 18,00,000 − 6,00,000 = ₹24,00,000.
Book value per share = 24,00,000 ÷ 2,00,000 = ₹12.00.

Variation C: preference dividend arrears. If preference dividend arrears of ₹1,20,000 exist, they are also deducted from equity''s claim: 24,00,000 − 1,20,000 = 22,80,000 → ₹11.40 per share.

Common errors

Forgetting to deduct preference capital. Including fictitious assets. Using total share capital (not the number of shares) as the divisor. Adding reserves twice.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 7, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '36af92ff-aa78-49a6-b953-c816f8fa02f9', 'a6370687-af9d-43eb-9fe1-5cfd8e1ad30e', '{"blocks": [{"id": "equity-and-debt-markets-u4-c7-q", "text": "Book Value Method: variations and quick numerical patterns.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u4-c7-1", "text": "Variation A: fictitious assets and accumulated loss.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "edm-u4-c7-2", "text": "Equity share capital ₹10,00,000 (₹10 shares), reserves ₹3,00,000, debit balance in P&L ₹1,00,000, preliminary expenses not written off ₹50,000.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "edm-u4-c7-3", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Net worth = 10,00,000 + 3,00,000 − 1,00,000 − 50,000 = ₹11,50,000.", "Shares = 1,00,000 → book value per share = ₹11.50."]}, {"id": "edm-u4-c7-4", "text": "Variation B: only totals are given.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "edm-u4-c7-5", "text": "Total assets ₹50,00,000 (including fictitious assets ₹2,00,000); outside liabilities ₹18,00,000; preference capital ₹6,00,000; equity shares 2,00,000.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "edm-u4-c7-6", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Net worth = 50,00,000 − 2,00,000 − 18,00,000 − 6,00,000 = ₹24,00,000.", "Book value per share = 24,00,000 ÷ 2,00,000 = ₹12.00."]}, {"id": "edm-u4-c7-7", "type": "formula", "expression": "Variation C: preference dividend arrears. If preference dividend arrears of ₹1,20,000 exist, they are also deducted from equity''s claim: 24,00,000 − 1,20,000 = 22,80,000 → ₹11.40 per share."}, {"id": "edm-u4-c7-8", "bold": true, "text": "Common errors", "type": "text", "style": "heading"}, {"id": "edm-u4-c7-9", "text": "Forgetting to deduct preference capital. Including fictitious assets. Using total share capital (not the number of shares) as the divisor. Adding reserves twice.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-21 16:57:33.462562+00', '2026-09-21 16:57:33.462562+00'),
('f12e3b7e-3a3f-4e9b-8f10-7c1f25d75676', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'A student wants to enter advertising through an agency. Explain possible career routes and the role of an Account Executive.', 'Priority: Tier 1', 'The student has multiple entry routes depending on interest: client servicing (starting as an Account Coordinator and progressing to Account Executive — see Flashcard 33 for the full pathway), creative (copywriter or art director trainee), media planning/buying, digital/social media specialist, or strategy/planning roles. Since the question specifically asks about the Account Executive route, the student should follow the agency-based pathway: relevant education → internship exposure → Account Coordinator/Junior AE → Account Executive → Senior AE/Account Manager. The Account Executive specifically acts as the vital link between the client and the agency''s internal teams, managing briefs, timelines, budgets, and the overall client relationship (see Flashcard 32).', NULL, NULL, NULL, 39, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '825dd962-2813-41cf-a5f5-0a886af35bb5', '054a2ab5-1f78-4bea-b5b8-cb6c32b2114d', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u4-c39-q-1", "text": "A student wants to enter advertising through an agency. Explain possible career routes and the role of an Account Executive.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u4-c39-a-1", "text": "The student has multiple entry routes depending on interest: client servicing (starting as an Account Coordinator and progressing to Account Executive — see Flashcard 33 for the full pathway), creative (copywriter or art director trainee), media planning/buying, digital/social media specialist, or strategy/planning roles. Since the question specifically asks about the Account Executive route, the student should follow the agency-based pathway: relevant education → internship exposure → Account Coordinator/Junior AE → Account Executive → Senior AE/Account Manager. The Account Executive specifically acts as the vital link between the client and the agency''s internal teams, managing briefs, timelines, budgets, and the overall client relationship (see Flashcard 32).", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:52.71444+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:52.71444+00'),
('59ab71e9-f8e3-4a1c-b7c8-5cc57a9d70a7', 'e9c8f886-9fbb-4b24-980c-2cf403873185', 'flashcard', 'A brand wants personalised advertising across multiple customer touchpoints. Explain how personalised and omnichannel advertising can be used together.', 'Priority: Tier 2', 'The brand should combine both concepts: personalisation ensures the content of each ad or message is tailored to the individual customer''s preferences and behaviour (e.g., product recommendations based on browsing history), while an omnichannel approach ensures this personalised message is delivered consistently across every touchpoint the customer interacts with — website, app, social media, email, and physical store — so the experience feels seamless rather than fragmented. For example, a customer who views a product online could receive a personalised app notification about a related offer, and find the same offer honoured seamlessly if they later visit a physical store, creating one continuous, individually-relevant customer journey.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 40, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '825dd962-2813-41cf-a5f5-0a886af35bb5', '054a2ab5-1f78-4bea-b5b8-cb6c32b2114d', '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u4-c40-q-1", "text": "A brand wants personalised advertising across multiple customer touchpoints. Explain how personalised and omnichannel advertising can be used together.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "adv-u4-c40-a-1", "text": "The brand should combine both concepts: personalisation ensures the content of each ad or message is tailored to the individual customer''s preferences and behaviour (e.g., product recommendations based on browsing history), while an omnichannel approach ensures this personalised message is delivered consistently across every touchpoint the customer interacts with — website, app, social media, email, and physical store — so the experience feels seamless rather than fragmented. For example, a customer who views a product online could receive a personalised app notification about a related offer, and find the same offer honoured seamlessly if they later visit a physical store, creating one continuous, individually-relevant customer journey.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:19:52.71444+00', '2026-09-20 18:19:52.71444+00'),
('73e968f1-9979-4a36-b5ce-7ef79e0502b4', '1ba3fabc-a2c8-4c58-a388-cb07c2a4d70d', 'flashcard', 'What is the concept of entrepreneurship?', 'Priority: Tier 1 | PYQ Connection: Foundational — underlies both the 2024 "impact on society" question and the 2025 "definition + qualities" question.', 'Entrepreneurship is the process of identifying a business opportunity, organising the factors of production (land, labour, capital, and enterprise), innovating a product/process/service, and bearing the associated risk and uncertainty in order to establish and run an enterprise for profit and value creation. It is a dynamic, ongoing process rather than a one-time act of starting a business.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '032080f2-249a-4b0b-9e23-ad18a3e05122', '33af4325-6a5a-41db-96a8-3d764988668a', '{"blocks": [{"id": "bpem-u1-c1-q-1", "text": "What is the concept of entrepreneurship?", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "bpem-u1-c1-a-1", "text": "Entrepreneurship is the process of identifying a business opportunity, organising the factors of production (land, labour, capital, and enterprise), innovating a product/process/service, and bearing the associated risk and uncertainty in order to establish and run an enterprise for profit and value creation. It is a dynamic, ongoing process rather than a one-time act of starting a business.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-21 02:33:38.327363+00', '2026-09-21 02:33:38.327363+00'),
('67302222-9763-42f4-907a-1db0bf8f410c', '1ba3fabc-a2c8-4c58-a388-cb07c2a4d70d', 'flashcard', 'Why is entrepreneurship development needed in an economy?', 'Priority: Tier 1 | PYQ Connection: Directly supports the 2024 "entrepreneurs as agents of change/impact on society" question.', 'It is needed because entrepreneurs create new enterprises that generate employment, mobilise idle savings into productive capital, introduce innovation and new technology, promote balanced regional development by setting up units in backward areas, increase national income and exports, and reduce dependence on a few large business houses, thereby ensuring a more equitable distribution of economic power.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 2, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '032080f2-249a-4b0b-9e23-ad18a3e05122', '33af4325-6a5a-41db-96a8-3d764988668a', '{"blocks": [{"id": "bpem-u1-c2-q-1", "text": "Why is entrepreneurship development needed in an economy?", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "bpem-u1-c2-a-1", "text": "It is needed because entrepreneurs create new enterprises that generate employment, mobilise idle savings into productive capital, introduce innovation and new technology, promote balanced regional development by setting up units in backward areas, increase national income and exports, and reduce dependence on a few large business houses, thereby ensuring a more equitable distribution of economic power.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-21 02:33:38.327363+00', '2026-09-21 02:33:38.327363+00'),
('b297b809-596b-483c-a261-ffd1611be1a8', '1ba3fabc-a2c8-4c58-a388-cb07c2a4d70d', 'flashcard', 'Explain the importance and significance of the growth of entrepreneurial activities.', 'Priority: Tier 1 | PYQ Connection: 2024 — "impact of entrepreneurship on society" (10 marks).', 'Growth in entrepreneurial activity leads to: (1) employment generation, (2) capital formation through mobilisation of savings, (3) balanced regional development, (4) increase in GDP/national income, (5) technological and product innovation, (6) improvement in standard of living, (7) promotion of exports and foreign exchange earnings, (8) creation of ancillary/support industries, and (9) reduction of concentration of economic power in a few hands. It also fosters a culture of self-employment and reduces unemployment-driven social problems.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 3, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '032080f2-249a-4b0b-9e23-ad18a3e05122', '33af4325-6a5a-41db-96a8-3d764988668a', '{"blocks": [{"id": "bpem-u1-c3-q-1", "text": "Explain the importance and significance of the growth of entrepreneurial activities.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "bpem-u1-c3-a-1", "text": "Growth in entrepreneurial activity leads to: (1) employment generation, (2) capital formation through mobilisation of savings, (3) balanced regional development, (4) increase in GDP/national income, (5) technological and product innovation, (6) improvement in standard of living, (7) promotion of exports and foreign exchange earnings, (8) creation of ancillary/support industries, and (9) reduction of concentration of economic power in a few hands. It also fosters a culture of self-employment and reduces unemployment-driven social problems.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-21 02:33:38.327363+00', '2026-09-21 02:33:38.327363+00'),
('36bb5313-4557-450a-9aed-ec12a1df81ad', '1ba3fabc-a2c8-4c58-a388-cb07c2a4d70d', 'flashcard', 'Define "Entrepreneur."', 'Priority: Tier 1 | PYQ Connection: 2025 — "Define an Entrepreneur. State qualities... with examples" (15 marks, compulsory-choice).', 'An entrepreneur is an individual who perceives a business opportunity, organises and mobilises the necessary resources (capital, labour, raw material, technology), innovates a product, process or service, and bears the risk and uncertainty of the venture with the objective of earning profit and creating value for society.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 4, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '032080f2-249a-4b0b-9e23-ad18a3e05122', 'd7c72185-38ac-4f23-8995-c93d13b4cfc9', '{"blocks": [{"id": "bpem-u1-c4-q-1", "text": "Define \"Entrepreneur.\"", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "bpem-u1-c4-a-1", "text": "An entrepreneur is an individual who perceives a business opportunity, organises and mobilises the necessary resources (capital, labour, raw material, technology), innovates a product, process or service, and bears the risk and uncertainty of the venture with the objective of earning profit and creating value for society.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-21 02:33:38.327363+00', '2026-09-21 02:33:38.327363+00'),
('6cbf3c5f-972b-4b14-9f55-e5ba47b66b1f', '1ba3fabc-a2c8-4c58-a388-cb07c2a4d70d', 'flashcard', 'What is the difference between "Entrepreneur" and "Entrepreneurship"?', 'Priority: Tier 2 | PYQ Connection: Conceptual base for both 2024 and 2025 definition-based questions.', 'The entrepreneur is the person (the actor/individual) who takes the initiative, while entrepreneurship is the process or activity that person performs — identifying opportunity, innovating, organising resources, and bearing risk. In short, the entrepreneur is the doer; entrepreneurship is the doing.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 5, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '032080f2-249a-4b0b-9e23-ad18a3e05122', 'd7c72185-38ac-4f23-8995-c93d13b4cfc9', '{"blocks": [{"id": "bpem-u1-c5-q-1", "text": "What is the difference between \"Entrepreneur\" and \"Entrepreneurship\"?", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "bpem-u1-c5-a-1", "text": "The entrepreneur is the person (the actor/individual) who takes the initiative, while entrepreneurship is the process or activity that person performs — identifying opportunity, innovating, organising resources, and bearing risk. In short, the entrepreneur is the doer; entrepreneurship is the doing.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-21 02:33:38.327363+00', '2026-09-21 02:33:38.327363+00'),
('a5b37cab-006c-4f71-b9e4-a58e12decc19', '1ba3fabc-a2c8-4c58-a388-cb07c2a4d70d', 'flashcard', 'State and explain the qualities and characteristics of an entrepreneur, with a suitable example.', 'Priority: Tier 1 | PYQ Connection: 2024 Q1B (9 marks) and 2025 Q1.1 (15 marks) — this exact theme has appeared in both supplied papers and should be treated as core.', 'Key qualities/characteristics include:

Risk-taking ability – willingness to bear calculated (not reckless) business risk.
Innovativeness and creativity – ability to devise new products, processes or methods.
Self-confidence – belief in one''s own decisions and abilities.
Vision and foresight – capacity to anticipate market needs and future trends.
Perseverance and hard work – sustained effort despite setbacks.
Decision-making ability – quick, sound decisions under uncertainty.
Leadership quality – ability to guide and motivate a team.
High need for achievement – internal drive to excel and set challenging goals.
Opportunity-seeking ability – alertness to unmet market needs.
Flexibility/adaptability – readiness to change strategy as conditions change.

Example: Dhirubhai Ambani identified an opportunity in the textile/polyester trade with limited resources, took calculated risks, showed strong vision and perseverance, and built Reliance Industries into a conglomerate — illustrating opportunity-seeking, risk-taking, and vision in practice.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 6, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '032080f2-249a-4b0b-9e23-ad18a3e05122', 'd7c72185-38ac-4f23-8995-c93d13b4cfc9', '{"blocks": [{"id": "bpem-u1-c6-q-1", "text": "State and explain the qualities and characteristics of an entrepreneur, with a suitable example.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "bpem-u1-c6-a-1", "text": "Key qualities/characteristics include:", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "bpem-u1-c6-a-2", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Risk-taking ability – willingness to bear calculated (not reckless) business risk.", "Innovativeness and creativity – ability to devise new products, processes or methods.", "Self-confidence – belief in one''s own decisions and abilities.", "Vision and foresight – capacity to anticipate market needs and future trends.", "Perseverance and hard work – sustained effort despite setbacks.", "Decision-making ability – quick, sound decisions under uncertainty.", "Leadership quality – ability to guide and motivate a team.", "High need for achievement – internal drive to excel and set challenging goals.", "Opportunity-seeking ability – alertness to unmet market needs.", "Flexibility/adaptability – readiness to change strategy as conditions change."]}, {"id": "bpem-u1-c6-a-3", "text": "Example: Dhirubhai Ambani identified an opportunity in the textile/polyester trade with limited resources, took calculated risks, showed strong vision and perseverance, and built Reliance Industries into a conglomerate — illustrating opportunity-seeking, risk-taking, and vision in practice.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-21 02:33:38.327363+00', '2026-09-21 02:33:38.327363+00'),
('0befe211-41fe-4b56-8068-1658c0caea80', '1ba3fabc-a2c8-4c58-a388-cb07c2a4d70d', 'flashcard', 'Explain qualities of an entrepreneur — alternate framing: "Discuss the role of entrepreneurial qualities in successful entrepreneurship."', 'Priority: Tier 1 | PYQ Connection: Reworded variant of the recurring "qualities" question — examiners may frame this analytically instead of descriptively.', '(Same core quality list as above.) Each quality directly supports venture success: risk-taking enables entry into uncertain markets; innovativeness creates competitive advantage; vision guides long-term strategy; perseverance sustains the venture through early losses; leadership builds an effective team; decision-making ensures timely responses to market changes. Together these qualities differentiate a successful entrepreneur from a mere business owner.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 7, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '032080f2-249a-4b0b-9e23-ad18a3e05122', 'd7c72185-38ac-4f23-8995-c93d13b4cfc9', '{"blocks": [{"id": "bpem-u1-c7-q-1", "text": "Explain qualities of an entrepreneur — alternate framing: \"Discuss the role of entrepreneurial qualities in successful entrepreneurship.\"", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "bpem-u1-c7-a-1", "text": "(Same core quality list as above.) Each quality directly supports venture success: risk-taking enables entry into uncertain markets; innovativeness creates competitive advantage; vision guides long-term strategy; perseverance sustains the venture through early losses; leadership builds an effective team; decision-making ensures timely responses to market changes. Together these qualities differentiate a successful entrepreneur from a mere business owner.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-21 02:33:38.327363+00', '2026-09-21 02:33:38.327363+00'),
('3cedd9fc-b732-420d-b5ac-c187c15002a4', '1ba3fabc-a2c8-4c58-a388-cb07c2a4d70d', 'flashcard', 'What is the role/importance of an entrepreneur in economic development?', 'Priority: Tier 2 | PYQ Connection: Supports 2024''s "entrepreneurs are agents of change in society."', 'The entrepreneur acts as a catalyst of economic development by: creating employment, introducing innovation, mobilising capital and other resources, promoting balanced regional growth, increasing national income, encouraging capital formation, improving standard of living, and acting as an agent of social and economic change.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 8, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '032080f2-249a-4b0b-9e23-ad18a3e05122', 'd7c72185-38ac-4f23-8995-c93d13b4cfc9', '{"blocks": [{"id": "bpem-u1-c8-q-1", "text": "What is the role/importance of an entrepreneur in economic development?", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "bpem-u1-c8-a-1", "text": "The entrepreneur acts as a catalyst of economic development by: creating employment, introducing innovation, mobilising capital and other resources, promoting balanced regional growth, increasing national income, encouraging capital formation, improving standard of living, and acting as an agent of social and economic change.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-21 02:33:38.327363+00', '2026-09-21 02:33:38.327363+00'),
('bd1937fd-fd73-4aeb-823c-b9ba2d37b472', '1ba3fabc-a2c8-4c58-a388-cb07c2a4d70d', 'flashcard', '"Entrepreneurs are agents of change in the society." Discuss the impact of entrepreneurship on society.', 'Priority: Tier 1 | PYQ Connection: 2024 Q1A (10 marks) — appeared as the opening compulsory question; treat as very high priority for analytical/essay-type preparation.', 'Entrepreneurs bring change by:

Employment generation – reducing unemployment and underemployment.
Wealth creation and distribution – spreading income across a wider base rather than concentrating it.
Innovation diffusion – introducing new products/services that improve quality of life.
Empowerment – especially of women and marginalised groups through self-employment.
Infrastructure and ancillary development – growth of supporting industries and services around a new enterprise.
Social status mobility – enabling upward mobility for individuals and communities.
Standard of living – better goods, services and incomes raise overall living standards.
Changing social attitudes – normalising risk-taking, self-reliance and entrepreneurial culture, especially among youth.

Thus, entrepreneurship does not merely create individual wealth — it restructures social and economic relationships, justifying the description of entrepreneurs as "agents of change."', NULL, NULL, NULL, 9, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '032080f2-249a-4b0b-9e23-ad18a3e05122', '4a762f5b-4714-47aa-bb4e-8d43b85cb040', '{"blocks": [{"id": "bpem-u1-c9-q-1", "text": "\"Entrepreneurs are agents of change in the society.\" Discuss the impact of entrepreneurship on society.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "bpem-u1-c9-a-1", "text": "Entrepreneurs bring change by:", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "bpem-u1-c9-a-2", "type": "numberList", "items": ["Employment generation – reducing unemployment and underemployment.", "Wealth creation and distribution – spreading income across a wider base rather than concentrating it.", "Innovation diffusion – introducing new products/services that improve quality of life.", "Empowerment – especially of women and marginalised groups through self-employment.", "Infrastructure and ancillary development – growth of supporting industries and services around a new enterprise.", "Social status mobility – enabling upward mobility for individuals and communities.", "Standard of living – better goods, services and incomes raise overall living standards.", "Changing social attitudes – normalising risk-taking, self-reliance and entrepreneurial culture, especially among youth."]}, {"id": "bpem-u1-c9-a-3", "text": "Thus, entrepreneurship does not merely create individual wealth — it restructures social and economic relationships, justifying the description of entrepreneurs as \"agents of change.\"", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-21 02:33:38.327363+00', '2026-09-21 02:33:38.327363+00'),
('fcb2f582-008f-4f1f-ab1a-0bfc661cb35e', '1ba3fabc-a2c8-4c58-a388-cb07c2a4d70d', 'flashcard', 'How does entrepreneurship contribute to economic development?', 'Priority: Tier 2 | PYQ Connection: Complements the 2024 "impact on society" question if the examiner narrows the ask to the economic dimension specifically.', 'Through capital formation (channelling savings into productive investment), employment generation, increase in per-capita and national income, balanced regional development (setting up units in backward regions), promotion of exports, technological upgradation, and development of ancillary/small-scale industries that support larger enterprises.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 10, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '032080f2-249a-4b0b-9e23-ad18a3e05122', '4a762f5b-4714-47aa-bb4e-8d43b85cb040', '{"blocks": [{"id": "bpem-u1-c10-q-1", "text": "How does entrepreneurship contribute to economic development?", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "bpem-u1-c10-a-1", "text": "Through capital formation (channelling savings into productive investment), employment generation, increase in per-capita and national income, balanced regional development (setting up units in backward regions), promotion of exports, technological upgradation, and development of ancillary/small-scale industries that support larger enterprises.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-21 02:33:38.327363+00', '2026-09-21 02:33:38.327363+00'),
('03ee3a5d-c2ae-41e6-b7ab-830e9ab667b6', '1ba3fabc-a2c8-4c58-a388-cb07c2a4d70d', 'flashcard', 'Explain Schumpeter''s Innovation Theory of entrepreneurship.', 'Priority: Tier 1 | PYQ Connection: 2024 Q1P — "comparative study between Innovation theory and Profit Theory" (10 marks).', 'Schumpeter viewed the entrepreneur primarily as an innovator, not merely a risk-bearer or manager. The entrepreneur disturbs the existing economic equilibrium by introducing "new combinations," which include: (1) introduction of a new product, (2) introduction of a new method of production, (3) opening of a new market, (4) discovery of a new source of raw material/supply, and (5) carrying out a new organisation of an industry. This continuous process of innovation followed by imitation by others, and eventual disruption by fresh innovation, is what Schumpeter called "creative destruction." Economic development, in this view, is driven fundamentally by entrepreneurial innovation rather than by capital accumulation alone.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 11, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '032080f2-249a-4b0b-9e23-ad18a3e05122', 'e58b582e-24f9-451f-a326-d32b30d64420', '{"blocks": [{"id": "bpem-u1-c11-q-1", "text": "Explain Schumpeter''s Innovation Theory of entrepreneurship.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "bpem-u1-c11-a-1", "text": "Schumpeter viewed the entrepreneur primarily as an innovator, not merely a risk-bearer or manager. The entrepreneur disturbs the existing economic equilibrium by introducing \"new combinations,\" which include: (1) introduction of a new product, (2) introduction of a new method of production, (3) opening of a new market, (4) discovery of a new source of raw material/supply, and (5) carrying out a new organisation of an industry. This continuous process of innovation followed by imitation by others, and eventual disruption by fresh innovation, is what Schumpeter called \"creative destruction.\" Economic development, in this view, is driven fundamentally by entrepreneurial innovation rather than by capital accumulation alone.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-21 02:33:38.327363+00', '2026-09-21 02:33:38.327363+00'),
('b77e3f44-cc3e-44f5-977a-775824cee2b2', '1ba3fabc-a2c8-4c58-a388-cb07c2a4d70d', 'flashcard', 'Explain the Imitation Theory of entrepreneurship.', 'Priority: Tier 2 | PYQ Connection: Not directly asked in the supplied papers, but relevant for comparison with Innovation Theory and for "explain any theory" style questions.', 'This theory explains entrepreneurship in developing/follower economies as largely a process of imitating and adapting innovations, technologies and business models that have already been successfully introduced in more advanced economies, rather than originating wholly new innovations. Entrepreneurs in such economies study proven products/processes abroad and adapt them to local conditions, resources and consumer needs. This allows relatively faster industrial growth in developing countries because the risk of the "first innovation" has already been absorbed elsewhere, and the imitating entrepreneur only bears the (lower) risk of local adaptation.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 12, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '032080f2-249a-4b0b-9e23-ad18a3e05122', 'e58b582e-24f9-451f-a326-d32b30d64420', '{"blocks": [{"id": "bpem-u1-c12-q-1", "text": "Explain the Imitation Theory of entrepreneurship.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "bpem-u1-c12-a-1", "text": "This theory explains entrepreneurship in developing/follower economies as largely a process of imitating and adapting innovations, technologies and business models that have already been successfully introduced in more advanced economies, rather than originating wholly new innovations. Entrepreneurs in such economies study proven products/processes abroad and adapt them to local conditions, resources and consumer needs. This allows relatively faster industrial growth in developing countries because the risk of the \"first innovation\" has already been absorbed elsewhere, and the imitating entrepreneur only bears the (lower) risk of local adaptation.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-21 02:33:38.327363+00', '2026-09-21 02:33:38.327363+00'),
('fdfa0085-0981-4e88-b5e4-57dbc8944e86', '1ba3fabc-a2c8-4c58-a388-cb07c2a4d70d', 'flashcard', 'Explain McClelland''s Theory of High Achievement (Need for Achievement).', 'Priority: Tier 2 | PYQ Connection: No direct PYQ in the supplied papers, but strong potential for a standalone or comparison question given its close link to EDP topics elsewhere in the syllabus.', 'McClelland argued that entrepreneurship is driven by a psychological factor — the need for achievement (n-Ach) — which is stronger in some individuals than others. People with high n-Ach: prefer situations involving personal responsibility for outcomes, prefer moderate risk (neither too safe nor reckless) so that success reflects their own ability, and desire concrete feedback on their performance so they can measure success. McClelland also proposed that this achievement motivation is not purely innate — it can be developed through training and the right upbringing/environment, which is the basis for many modern Entrepreneurship Development Programmes (EDPs).', NULL, NULL, NULL, 13, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '032080f2-249a-4b0b-9e23-ad18a3e05122', 'e58b582e-24f9-451f-a326-d32b30d64420', '{"blocks": [{"id": "bpem-u1-c13-q-1", "text": "Explain McClelland''s Theory of High Achievement (Need for Achievement).", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "bpem-u1-c13-a-1", "text": "McClelland argued that entrepreneurship is driven by a psychological factor — the need for achievement (n-Ach) — which is stronger in some individuals than others. People with high n-Ach: prefer situations involving personal responsibility for outcomes, prefer moderate risk (neither too safe nor reckless) so that success reflects their own ability, and desire concrete feedback on their performance so they can measure success. McClelland also proposed that this achievement motivation is not purely innate — it can be developed through training and the right upbringing/environment, which is the basis for many modern Entrepreneurship Development Programmes (EDPs).", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-21 02:33:38.327363+00', '2026-09-21 02:33:38.327363+00'),
('0903ea0b-2b5f-4793-a724-b7dab4f6b0e9', '1ba3fabc-a2c8-4c58-a388-cb07c2a4d70d', 'flashcard', 'Explain Leibenstein''s X-Efficiency Theory.', 'Priority: Tier 2 | PYQ Connection: Not directly asked, but a natural candidate for "explain any theory" or comparison-type framing.', 'Leibenstein observed that firms rarely operate at their full potential efficiency; the gap between actual and potential output/efficiency is called "X-inefficiency." He assigned the entrepreneur two key roles in correcting this: (1) Gap-filling — identifying and filling gaps in the market or in the production process that others have overlooked, and (2) Input-completing — supplying missing inputs (information, coordination, management) needed to convert potential production into actual production. The entrepreneur''s function, in this theory, is essentially to reduce inefficiency and market gaps rather than to be a pure innovator.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 14, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '032080f2-249a-4b0b-9e23-ad18a3e05122', 'e58b582e-24f9-451f-a326-d32b30d64420', '{"blocks": [{"id": "bpem-u1-c14-q-1", "text": "Explain Leibenstein''s X-Efficiency Theory.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "bpem-u1-c14-a-1", "text": "Leibenstein observed that firms rarely operate at their full potential efficiency; the gap between actual and potential output/efficiency is called \"X-inefficiency.\" He assigned the entrepreneur two key roles in correcting this: (1) Gap-filling — identifying and filling gaps in the market or in the production process that others have overlooked, and (2) Input-completing — supplying missing inputs (information, coordination, management) needed to convert potential production into actual production. The entrepreneur''s function, in this theory, is essentially to reduce inefficiency and market gaps rather than to be a pure innovator.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-21 02:33:38.327363+00', '2026-09-21 02:33:38.327363+00')
